Guide.content._register("w02", {"id":"w02","order":2,"region":"foundations","title":"Latency, KV Cache and Prompt Caching","short":"Latency and caching","tagline":"Why the answer is slow, and which cache makes it fast without making it wrong.","story":"The question has reached the model and it waits: in a queue, in prefill, and in a decode loop that emits one token at a time. Latency and cost both trace back to how the model stores what it has already read. Every cache you add trades speed and money against correctness and isolation, and the quietest failures are the caches that stop hitting.","oneLiner":"Name the bottleneck first, then pick the lever and the cache whose risk your dials can afford.","youWillLearn":["Separate TTFT, TPOT, queueing and total time, and read each from the right metric","Explain why prefill is compute-bound and decode is memory-bound, and which serving lever fixes which symptom","Size a KV cache from layers, KV heads, head dimension and precision, and see what GQA, MLA, paging and quantization buy","Configure prompt caching on OpenAI, Azure OpenAI and Anthropic, and compute the break-even for write charges","Engineer the hit rate: stable prefixes, key sharding, lifetime, and the changes that silently reset a cache","Choose between prefix, exact, semantic and embedding caches by repetition, tenancy and tolerance for wrong answers","Build an end-to-end latency budget and decide where parallelism, streaming and capacity modes help"],"minutes":125,"prereqs":["w01"],"flashcards":[{"id":"w02-fc-001","front":"What does **TTFT** include, and what makes it grow?","back":"Queueing, prefill and network time up to the first token. It grows with prompt length, because prefill reads the whole prompt before any output.","tags":["latency"]},{"id":"w02-fc-002","front":"How do you compute inter-token latency (ITL, the same as TPOT) from a request trace?","back":"`ITL = (e2e_latency - TTFT) / (output_tokens - 1)`. Some benchmark tools include TTFT in the average and some exclude it, so tool outputs do not compare.","tags":["latency"]},{"id":"w02-fc-003","front":"Why is **decode** memory-bandwidth bound but **prefill** compute-bound?","back":"Prefill processes the whole prompt in parallel and saturates compute. Decode emits one token per request per step and mostly streams weights and KV from memory.","tags":["inference"]},{"id":"w02-fc-004","front":"Write the KV cache size per token.","back":"`2 x n_layers x n_kv_heads x head_dim x bytes_per_element`. The 2 is keys plus values. Multiply by tokens and by batch for the total.","tags":["kv-cache"]},{"id":"w02-fc-005","front":"A model has 32 layers, 8 KV heads, head dim 128 and bf16 KV. KV bytes per token?","back":"2 x 32 x 8 x 128 x 2 = 131,072 bytes (128 KiB). At 8,192 tokens that is 1 GiB per sequence.","tags":["kv-cache"]},{"id":"w02-fc-006","front":"MHA, GQA and MQA differ in what?","back":"The number of KV heads. MHA keeps one per query head, MQA shares a single KV head, GQA uses a group count in between. Fewer KV heads means a smaller KV cache.","tags":["kv-cache"]},{"id":"w02-fc-007","front":"What does DeepSeek-V2's **MLA** change, and by how much?","back":"It compresses keys and values into a latent vector. The paper reports a 93.3% smaller KV cache and 5.76x peak generation throughput versus DeepSeek 67B.","tags":["kv-cache"]},{"id":"w02-fc-008","front":"What problem does **PagedAttention** solve?","back":"KV memory fragmentation and duplication. It stores KV in fixed-size blocks like virtual-memory pages, so waste is near zero and blocks can be shared across requests.","tags":["kv-cache"]},{"id":"w02-fc-009","front":"What does **chunked prefill** fix?","back":"Decode stalls caused by long prefills. It splits a prompt into chunks so decodes for other requests keep running, at some TTFT cost for the chunked request.","tags":["inference"]},{"id":"w02-fc-010","front":"What does speculative decoding guarantee about its output?","back":"The output distribution is unchanged. A cheap draft proposes tokens and the large model verifies them in parallel, so the gain is speed only.","tags":["inference"]},{"id":"w02-fc-011","front":"Complete: provider prompt caching reuses the _____ for a repeated prefix.","back":"Prefill state, meaning the KV tensors, not the text. Output is unchanged. Only the matched prefix from the start of the prompt can hit.","tags":["prompt-caching"]},{"id":"w02-fc-012","front":"On GPT-5.6 and later, what do cache writes and reads cost?","back":"A write costs 1.25x the uncached input rate and a read costs 0.1x (0.05x on GPT-6.1 Sol). One write plus nine reads costs 2.15x versus 10x uncached.","tags":["prompt-caching"]},{"id":"w02-fc-013","front":"What are the two values of `prompt_cache_options.mode`?","back":"`implicit` puts a breakpoint at the end of the latest eligible message. `explicit` uses only breakpoints you mark. Explicit with none means no caching and no write charge.","tags":["prompt-caching"]},{"id":"w02-fc-014","front":"Anthropic cache TTLs and write multipliers?","back":"5 minutes by default at 1.25x write, optional 1 hour at 2x write. Reads cost 0.1x on most models. A hit refreshes the entry at no extra charge.","tags":["prompt-caching"]},{"id":"w02-fc-015","front":"Roughly what request rate per prefix and key causes OpenAI and Azure misses, and the fix?","back":"About 15 requests per minute per prefix and key. Above it, traffic can overflow to other machines. Shard busy prefixes across several stable keys.","tags":["prompt-caching"]},{"id":"w02-fc-016","front":"Before GPT-5.6, how did Azure report cache hits?","back":"`prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens`, counted in 128-token increments after an identical first 1,024 tokens. One differing character in those tokens gives zero.","tags":["prompt-caching"]},{"id":"w02-fc-017","front":"In the APIM semantic cache, does a lower `score-threshold` match more or fewer prompts?","back":"Fewer. Lower values demand higher similarity. Microsoft suggests starting near 0.05 and warns that above 0.2 may cause mismatches.","tags":["semantic-cache"]},{"id":"w02-fc-018","front":"Name five things a semantic cache partition key should carry.","back":"Tenant or user scope, model id, embedding model id, prompt template version and safety-policy version. Add the tool configuration version when tools are involved.","tags":["semantic-cache"]},{"id":"w02-fc-019","front":"What did Gu et al. (ICML 2025) find about prompt caches, and what is the engine-level fix?","back":"Timing revealed global cache sharing across users at several providers. In vLLM, a per-request `cache_salt` isolates tenants.","tags":["security"]},{"id":"w02-fc-020","front":"Does streaming reduce total generation time?","back":"No. It lowers time to first response and avoids client timeouts, but total time stays the same.","tags":["latency"]},{"id":"w02-fc-021","front":"Rule of thumb: output tokens versus input tokens for latency?","back":"Cutting 50% of output tokens may cut about 50% of latency. Cutting 50% of the prompt may give only 1% to 5%, except at very long contexts.","tags":["latency"]},{"id":"w02-t01-fc1","front":"What does TTFT include and what grows it?","back":"Queueing, prefill and network time. It grows with prompt length and with load.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t01"},{"id":"w02-t01-fc2","front":"Write ITL in terms of e2e latency and TTFT.","back":"ITL = (e2e latency - TTFT) / (total output tokens - 1). It is the same as TPOT.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t01"},{"id":"w02-t01-fc3","front":"Why can raising concurrency hurt users while lowering cost?","back":"System TPS rises to a saturation point while per-user TPS falls, and queueing grows near full utilization.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t01"},{"id":"w02-t02-fc1","front":"What does streaming change and not change?","back":"It lowers time to the first visible token and avoids client timeouts. Total generation time stays about the same.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t02"},{"id":"w02-t02-fc2","front":"Name two reasons streaming can hurt.","back":"Moderation of partial text is harder, and full-object validation or buffering proxies defeat it.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t02"},{"id":"w02-t02-fc3","front":"What keeps an SSE connection alive through legacy proxies?","back":"A comment line (starting with a colon) sent about every 15 seconds.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t02"},{"id":"w02-t03-fc1","front":"Why is prefill compute-bound and decode memory-bound?","back":"Prefill processes all prompt tokens in parallel and saturates compute. Decode emits one token per step and mostly reads weights and KV from memory.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t03"},{"id":"w02-t03-fc2","front":"Which cuts latency more: halving output tokens or halving input tokens?","back":"Halving output may cut about 50% of latency. Halving input may give only 1% to 5%, more at very long contexts.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t03"},{"id":"w02-t03-fc3","front":"What do chunked prefill and disaggregation fix?","back":"Stalls and interference when long prefills share capacity with decodes.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t03"},{"id":"w02-t04-fc1","front":"Write the KV cache bytes per token formula.","back":"2 x n_layers x n_kv_heads x head_dim x bytes_per_element. Multiply by tokens and by concurrent sequences for the total.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t04"},{"id":"w02-t04-fc2","front":"How much KV does one token need on Llama 3 8B in bf16?","back":"128 KiB per token, which is 1 GiB at 8,192 tokens (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128).","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t04"},{"id":"w02-t04-fc3","front":"What happens when a serving engine runs out of KV space?","back":"It preempts requests and recomputes them later. In vLLM V1 the default mode is recompute, which costs latency.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t04"},{"id":"w02-t05-fc1","front":"How does GQA differ from MHA and MQA?","back":"MHA gives every query head its own KV head, MQA shares one KV head across all, and GQA shares one KV head per group of query heads.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t05"},{"id":"w02-t05-fc2","front":"What did the GQA paper show about converting MHA checkpoints?","back":"Uptraining with about 5% of pre-training compute reached quality near MHA at speed near MQA.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t05"},{"id":"w02-t05-fc3","front":"What does MLA do differently?","back":"It compresses keys and values into a latent vector. DeepSeek-V2 reports a 93.3% smaller KV cache versus DeepSeek 67B.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t05"},{"id":"w02-t06-fc1","front":"What problem does PagedAttention solve?","back":"Reserved-but-unused and fragmented KV memory. Fixed-size blocks and a block table allow on-demand allocation and sharing.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t06"},{"id":"w02-t06-fc2","front":"What did the vLLM paper report?","back":"Near-zero KV waste and 2x to 4x throughput at equal latency versus the state of the art in 2023.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t06"},{"id":"w02-t06-fc3","front":"Does paging make the KV pool bigger?","back":"No. It makes more of the pool useful. When the pool is empty, requests are preempted and recomputed.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t06"},{"id":"w02-t07-fc1","front":"What does continuous batching change versus static batching?","back":"It schedules per iteration, so finished requests leave and waiting ones join at once instead of waiting for the slowest request.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t07"},{"id":"w02-t07-fc2","front":"What does chunked prefill fix, and what does it cost?","back":"It removes decode stalls from long prefills. The chunked prompt's own TTFT rises somewhat.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t07"},{"id":"w02-t07-fc3","front":"How does max_num_batched_tokens trade ITL against TTFT in vLLM?","back":"Smaller values (such as 2048) give better ITL. Larger values give better TTFT and throughput.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t07"},{"id":"w02-t08-fc1","front":"How does vLLM identify a cached KV block?","back":"By a hash of the parent block hash, the block's tokens and extra hashes such as LoRA ID, multimodal hash and cache_salt. Only full blocks are cached.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t08"},{"id":"w02-t08-fc2","front":"What does RadixAttention do?","back":"It keeps KV in a radix tree of token sequences so prefixes are reused automatically across calls. SGLang reports up to 6.4x throughput on structured programs.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t08"},{"id":"w02-t08-fc3","front":"How do you stop cross-tenant timing probes in vLLM?","back":"Send a per-request cache_salt, which is injected into the first block hash, so only same-salt requests share blocks.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t08"},{"id":"w02-t09-fc1","front":"How does speculative decoding keep outputs identical?","back":"The target verifies all drafted tokens in one pass, keeps the agreeing prefix and resamples the first disagreement, so the distribution is unchanged.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t09"},{"id":"w02-t09-fc2","front":"When does speculation help most, and when does it fade?","back":"It helps at low to medium QPS in memory-bound decode. Gains shrink as batches saturate or acceptance falls.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t09"},{"id":"w02-t09-fc3","front":"What is EAGLE?","back":"A speculative sampling method that drafts at the feature level (second-to-top-layer features) instead of the token level.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t09"},{"id":"w02-t10-fc1","front":"Which limit does weight quantization fix, and which does KV quantization fix?","back":"Weight quantization shrinks model memory and speeds memory-bound decode. KV quantization lets more sequences fit at long context.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t10"},{"id":"w02-t10-fc2","front":"What does W4A16 mean?","back":"4-bit weights with 16-bit activations. The first number is weight bits and the second is activation bits.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t10"},{"id":"w02-t10-fc3","front":"What is the safe way to adopt KV quantization?","back":"Calibrate scales, skip sensitive layers and gate on an eval at your real maximum context length.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t10"},{"id":"w02-t11-fc1","front":"What does a provider prompt cache store?","back":"The KV tensors for a prefix, not text or answers. Hits change cost and TTFT, never the output.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t11"},{"id":"w02-t11-fc2","front":"What are the four conditions for a prompt cache hit?","back":"Enough tokens, an identical prefix, routing to the machine holding it, and arrival inside the lifetime.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t11"},{"id":"w02-t11-fc3","front":"How many reuses does a 1.25x write need to break even?","back":"Two. One request costs 1.25x versus 1x, but two cost 1.35x versus 2x.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t11"},{"id":"w02-t12-fc1","front":"What are the two prompt_cache_options modes on GPT-5.6 and later?","back":"implicit (default) puts a breakpoint at the end of the latest eligible message. explicit uses only the breakpoints you mark.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t12"},{"id":"w02-t12-fc2","front":"How do you disable caching on a GPT-5.6+ Standard deployment?","back":"Set mode to explicit and place no breakpoints. There is no caching and no write charge.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t12"},{"id":"w02-t12-fc3","front":"What changes for PTU-M deployments?","back":"Caching continues without breakpoints or cache_write_tokens (per the Azure prompt-caching page). On models that deduct cached tokens in full, cached tokens do not consume PTU capacity, so a higher cache rate needs fewer PTUs. GPT-6 normalized-token models weigh cached input and writes instead.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t12"},{"id":"w02-t13-fc1","front":"How many cache breakpoints can an Anthropic request have, and what is the lookback?","back":"Up to 4 breakpoints, and each looks back 20 block positions for an earlier write. Add a breakpoint when a turn adds 20 or more blocks.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t13"},{"id":"w02-t13-fc2","front":"What do the Anthropic multipliers look like?","back":"5-minute write 1.25x, 1-hour write 2x, read 0.1x (0.05x or 0.025x on some models). They stack with Batch pricing.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t13"},{"id":"w02-t13-fc3","front":"What does input_tokens mean in Anthropic usage?","back":"Only tokens after the last cache breakpoint. Total input is input_tokens plus cache read plus cache creation.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t13"},{"id":"w02-t14-fc1","front":"What is the layout rule for cache-friendly prompts?","back":"Stable first, dynamic last, history append-only. Tools, then system, then static reference, then history, then retrieved text and the question.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t14"},{"id":"w02-t14-fc2","front":"How many keys does one prefix at 1,000 requests per minute need?","back":"At least 67, from 1,000 / 15 requests per minute per key, using a stable mapping.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t14"},{"id":"w02-t14-fc3","front":"How do you remove a tool without breaking the cache on OpenAI?","back":"Do not remove it. Use tool_choice none or allowed_tools so the tools array, and the prefix, stay identical.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t14"},{"id":"w02-t15-fc1","front":"How does a semantic cache differ from a prompt cache?","back":"A prompt cache reuses prefill state and leaves the answer unchanged. A semantic cache returns a stored answer, which can be wrong, stale or leaked.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t15"},{"id":"w02-t15-fc2","front":"What is the break-even hit rate for a semantic cache?","back":"C_lookup divided by C_model. Embedding and lookup are paid on every request, and a hit saves the model call.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t15"},{"id":"w02-t15-fc3","front":"What threshold does Microsoft suggest for APIM semantic caching?","back":"Start low, for example 0.05, where lower is stricter. Values above 0.2 may cause mismatches.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t15"},{"id":"w02-t16-fc1","front":"What must an exact-match response cache key include?","back":"Model ID, messages, sampling parameters, tools, template version and the caller's scope. Anything that changes the answer.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t16"},{"id":"w02-t16-fc2","front":"What must an embedding cache key include?","back":"A content hash plus the embedding model and its version, since vectors from different models are not comparable.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t16"},{"id":"w02-t16-fc3","front":"What are ttlMs and cacheScope in MCP?","back":"ttlMs is the freshness lifetime in ms, like max-age. cacheScope is public or private, and private entries must not cross authorization contexts.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t16"},{"id":"w02-t17-fc1","front":"What is the critical path in a latency budget?","back":"The longest chain of dependent stages. Stages that overlap, such as a guard beside retrieval, add only if they are longer than the chain they overlap.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t17"},{"id":"w02-t17-fc2","front":"Why does fan-out make the tail matter?","back":"With 100 parallel calls that are each slow 1% of the time, 63% of requests see at least one slow call.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t17"},{"id":"w02-t17-fc3","front":"What did the Tail at Scale benchmark show for hedging?","back":"A 10 ms hedge cut the 99.9th percentile of 1,000 reads from 1,800 ms to 74 ms for about 2% more requests.","tags":[],"topic":"w02-t17"}],"references":[{"id":"r1","title":"Prompt caching (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Exact GPT-5.6 and earlier caching rules, prices, keys, breakpoints and prewarm.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r2","title":"Prompt caching (Azure OpenAI in Foundry Models)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Azure breakpoints, prompt_cache_options, retention, PTU limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r3","title":"Prompt caching (Claude API)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-caching","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Automatic and explicit caching, TTLs, multipliers, lookback and invalidation.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r4","title":"Prompt caching for faster model inference (Amazon Bedrock)","url":"https://docs.aws.amazon.com/bedrock/latest/userguide/prompt-caching.html","type":"doc","publisher":"AWS","note":"Checkpoints, minimum sizes and the batch exclusion.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r5","title":"Context caching overview (Gemini Enterprise Agent Platform)","url":"https://docs.cloud.google.com/gemini-enterprise-agent-platform/models/context-cache/context-cache-overview","type":"doc","publisher":"Google Cloud","note":"Implicit versus explicit caching, discounts, limits and isolation.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r6","title":"Efficient Memory Management for Large Language Model Serving with PagedAttention","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2309.06180","type":"paper","publisher":"UC Berkeley and others (SOSP 2023)","note":"Origin of vLLM and block-based KV cache management.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r7","title":"Automatic Prefix Caching (vLLM design doc)","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/design/prefix_caching/","type":"doc","publisher":"vLLM project","note":"Hash-based block reuse and cache_salt tenant isolation.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r8","title":"SGLang: Efficient Execution of Structured Language Model Programs","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2312.07104","type":"paper","publisher":"LMSYS and others","note":"RadixAttention prefix reuse across calls.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r9","title":"Taming Throughput-Latency Tradeoff in LLM Inference with Sarathi-Serve","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.02310","type":"paper","publisher":"Microsoft Research and others (OSDI 2024)","note":"Prefill versus decode and chunked prefill.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r10","title":"GQA: Training Generalized Multi-Query Transformer Models from Multi-Head Checkpoints","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2305.13245","type":"paper","publisher":"Google Research","note":"MQA, GQA and MHA trade-off and uptraining.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r11","title":"DeepSeek-V2: A Strong, Economical, and Efficient Mixture-of-Experts Language Model","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.04434","type":"paper","publisher":"DeepSeek","note":"Multi-head latent attention and the 93.3% KV cache reduction.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r12","title":"Auditing Prompt Caching in Language Model APIs","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.07776","type":"paper","publisher":"Stanford (ICML 2025)","note":"Timing side channels and cross-user cache sharing.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r13","title":"LLM Inference Benchmarking: Fundamental Concepts","url":"https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/llm-benchmarking-fundamental-concepts/","type":"blog","publisher":"NVIDIA","note":"TTFT, ITL and throughput definitions, and where tools disagree.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r14","title":"Performance and latency (Azure OpenAI in Foundry Models)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/latency","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Latency formula, Azure Monitor metrics and levers.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r15","title":"Latency optimization (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/latency-optimization","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Seven principles and rules of thumb for latency.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r16","title":"llm-semantic-cache-lookup policy (Azure API Management)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/llm-semantic-cache-lookup-policy","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Thresholds, vary-by partitioning and the safety warnings.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r17","title":"Tutorial: Use Azure Managed Redis as a semantic cache","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/redis/tutorial-semantic-cache","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Partitioning, TTL and threshold tuning for an application-level semantic cache.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r18","title":"Model Context Protocol specification, Tools (2026-07-28)","url":"https://modelcontextprotocol.io/specification/2026-07-28/server/tools","type":"spec","publisher":"modelcontextprotocol.io","note":"Deterministic tool order, ttlMs and cacheScope on list and read results.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r19","title":"Fast Inference from Transformers via Speculative Decoding","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.17192","type":"paper","publisher":"Google Research (ICML 2023)","note":"Draft-and-verify decoding with unchanged output distribution.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r20","title":"KIVI: A Tuning-Free Asymmetric 2bit Quantization for KV Cache","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.02750","type":"paper","publisher":"Rice University and others (ICML 2024)","note":"Per-channel key and per-token value quantization of the KV cache.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r21","title":"FlashAttention: Fast and Memory-Efficient Exact Attention with IO-Awareness","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2205.14135","type":"paper","publisher":"Stanford (NeurIPS 2022)","note":"Exact attention that tiles to cut memory reads and writes.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r22","title":"Batch API (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/batch","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"50% discount, 24-hour window, file format and limits.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"r23","title":"Flex processing (Azure OpenAI in Foundry Models)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/flex-processing","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Preview tier at batch-like prices with synchronous calls and no SLA.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"preview"},{"id":"r24","title":"Large Transformer Model Inference Optimization","url":"https://lilianweng.github.io/posts/2023-01-10-inference-optimization/","type":"blog","publisher":"Lilian Weng","note":"Study aid: KV cache, quantization, MQA and GQA in one tutorial.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r25","title":"Efficiently Serving LLMs (short course)","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/efficiently-serving-llms","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI with Predibase","note":"Study aid: hands-on KV caching, continuous batching and quantization.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r26","title":"Semantic Caching for AI Agents (short course)","url":"https://www.deeplearning.ai/courses/semantic-caching-for-ai-agents","type":"course","publisher":"DeepLearning.AI with Redis","note":"Study aid: build and measure a semantic cache.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r27","title":"Meta-Llama-3-70B config.json","url":"https://huggingface.co/NousResearch/Meta-Llama-3-70B/blob/main/config.json","type":"repo","publisher":"Hugging Face","note":"Public layer, KV head and head dimension values used in the sizing examples.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r28","title":"DistServe: Disaggregating Prefill and Decoding for Goodput-optimized LLM Serving","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.09670","type":"paper","publisher":"Peking University and others (OSDI 2024)","note":"Separate prefill and decode machines to tune TTFT and TPOT independently.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r29","title":"Splitwise: Efficient Generative LLM Inference Using Phase Splitting","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2311.18677","type":"paper","publisher":"Microsoft Research and others (ISCA 2024)","note":"Phase splitting of prompt and token generation onto separate machines.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"r30","title":"Batch processing (Claude API)","url":"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/batch-processing","type":"doc","publisher":"Anthropic","note":"Message Batches: 50% cost cut, request and size limits, expiry and result retention.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr1","title":"Context caching (Gemini API)","url":"https://ai.google.dev/gemini-api/docs/caching","type":"doc","publisher":"Google AI for Developers","note":"Implicit caching is on by default for Gemini 2.5 and newer, with minimum token counts and the total_cached_tokens usage field.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr2","title":"Orca: A Distributed Serving System for Transformer-Based Generative Models (OSDI 2022)","url":"https://www.usenix.org/conference/osdi22/presentation/yu","type":"paper","publisher":"USENIX","note":"Origin of iteration-level (continuous) batching. The paper reports 36.9x throughput over FasterTransformer at the same latency on GPT-3 175B.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr3","title":"HTML Standard: Server-sent events","url":"https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/server-sent-events.html","type":"spec","publisher":"WHATWG","note":"The event stream format, id and retry fields, Last-Event-ID reconnection and the 15 second comment keepalive advice.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr4","title":"The Tail at Scale (Dean and Barroso, CACM 2013)","url":"https://research.google/pubs/the-tail-at-scale/","type":"paper","publisher":"Google Research","note":"Why rare slow calls dominate fan-out services and how hedged requests cut the tail. Figures checked against the article text.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr5","title":"Streaming API responses (OpenAI API guide)","url":"https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/streaming-responses","type":"doc","publisher":"OpenAI","note":"Responses stream events and the warning that streaming makes moderation harder.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr6","title":"Optimization and Tuning (vLLM)","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/configuration/optimization/","type":"doc","publisher":"vLLM project","note":"Preemption and recompute, chunked prefill defaults and the max_num_batched_tokens trade-off.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr7","title":"Quantization (vLLM)","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/features/quantization/","type":"doc","publisher":"vLLM project","note":"Supported formats (FP8, INT8, INT4) and the hardware support table.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr8","title":"Quantized KV Cache (vLLM)","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/features/quantization/quantized_kvcache/","type":"doc","publisher":"vLLM project","note":"FP8 KV cache, scale calibration and skipping sensitive layers.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr9","title":"EAGLE: Speculative Sampling Requires Rethinking Feature Uncertainty","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2401.15077","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Speculative sampling that drafts at the feature level instead of the token level.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr10","title":"Fast Transformer Decoding: One Write-Head is All You Need","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/1911.02150","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv (Shazeer)","note":"Introduces multi-query attention: shared keys and values cut memory bandwidth in incremental decoding.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr11","title":"What is provisioned throughput for Foundry Models?","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/concepts/provisioned-throughput","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"PTU model: held capacity, quota versus capacity, and cached tokens not consuming PTU capacity (page of 2026-07-15; GPT-6 models with normalized token accounting differ, see the sizing page).","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr12","title":"Meta-Llama-3-8B config.json","url":"https://huggingface.co/NousResearch/Meta-Llama-3-8B/blob/main/config.json","type":"repo","publisher":"Hugging Face (NousResearch mirror)","note":"Public config used to derive KV bytes per token: 32 layers, 8 KV heads, hidden size 4096, 32 attention heads, bfloat16.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr13","title":"Access Foundry Models and other language models through a gateway","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/generative-ai-gateway","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Gateway considerations: streaming responses need long-lived connections without premature timeouts or buffering, and the Responses API needs session affinity.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr14","title":"llm-token-limit policy (Azure API Management)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/llm-token-limit-policy","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Token-based rate limiting. Streaming always estimates tokens.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr15","title":"LLM.int8(): 8-bit Matrix Multiplication for Transformers at Scale","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2208.07339","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Int8 inference that halves memory by keeping emergent outlier features in 16-bit.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr16","title":"AWQ: Activation-aware Weight Quantization for LLM Compression and Acceleration","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.00978","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"Weight-only low-bit quantization that protects about 1% salient channels found from activation statistics.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr17","title":"GPTQ: Accurate Post-Training Quantization for Generative Pre-trained Transformers","url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2210.17323","type":"paper","publisher":"arXiv","note":"One-shot 3 and 4 bit weight quantization of 175B-parameter models in about four GPU hours.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr18","title":"Speculative Decoding (vLLM)","url":"https://docs.vllm.ai/en/latest/features/speculative_decoding/","type":"doc","publisher":"vLLM project","note":"Methods (EAGLE, MTP, draft model, n-gram, suffix), where each helps, and the speculative-config keys.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"n/a"},{"id":"tr19","title":"llm-emit-token-metric policy (Azure API Management)","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/api-management/llm-emit-token-metric-policy","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Token metrics to Application Insights. Streaming may need include_usage for accurate counts.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"},{"id":"tr20","title":"Determine PTU sizing for a workload","url":"https://learn.microsoft.com/azure/foundry/openai/how-to/provisioned-throughput-sizing","type":"doc","publisher":"Microsoft Learn","note":"Normalized TPM formula with the cache-rate term and a worked sizing example.","verified":"2026-10-13","status":"ga"}],"glossary":[{"term":"TTFT","def":"Time to first token. Queueing plus prefill plus network time until the first output token arrives. Grows with prompt length."},{"term":"TPOT / ITL","def":"Time per output token, also called inter-token latency. `(e2e latency - TTFT) / (output tokens - 1)`. Sets how smooth streaming feels."},{"term":"TTLT","def":"Time to last token, the total latency. Azure writes it as `TTFT + (TBT x tokens generated)`."},{"term":"Prefill","def":"The phase that reads the whole prompt in parallel and builds the KV cache. Compute-bound. Sets TTFT."},{"term":"Decode","def":"The phase that emits one token per request per step, reading weights and the KV cache each time. Memory-bandwidth bound. Sets TPOT."},{"term":"KV cache","def":"Stored keys and values of every earlier token, per layer and KV head, so each new token does not recompute them. Grows linearly with context and batch."},{"term":"GQA","def":"Grouped-query attention. Several query heads share one KV head, which shrinks the KV cache by `heads / kv_heads`. MQA is the extreme with one KV head."},{"term":"MLA","def":"Multi-head latent attention. Compresses keys and values into a low-dimensional latent vector before caching."},{"term":"PagedAttention","def":"Stores KV in fixed-size blocks mapped by a block table, like virtual memory. Near-zero fragmentation and shareable blocks. The basis of vLLM."},{"term":"Continuous batching","def":"Scheduling at iteration level: finished requests leave the batch and waiting ones join at the next step, instead of waiting for a whole batch."},{"term":"Chunked prefill","def":"Splitting a long prefill into chunks that share iterations with decodes, so other requests keep streaming."},{"term":"Speculative decoding","def":"A cheap draft model proposes several tokens and the large model verifies them in one pass. Same output distribution, fewer large-model steps."},{"term":"Prefix caching","def":"Reusing the KV state of an identical prompt prefix across requests. Hosted as provider prompt caching, self-hosted in vLLM or SGLang."},{"term":"Cache breakpoint","def":"A marker that says where a cacheable prefix ends. Explicit on Anthropic and on GPT-5.6 and later, implicit by default elsewhere."},{"term":"Semantic cache","def":"Returns a stored answer when a new prompt embeds close enough to an earlier one. Skips the model call and can return a wrong, stale or foreign answer."},{"term":"Cache write premium","def":"The extra charge for creating a cache entry, for example 1.25x the input rate on GPT-5.6 and later and on Anthropic 5-minute caching."},{"term":"Queueing delay","def":"Time a request waits for capacity before prefill starts. It grows steeply as utilization nears 100%, and it counts inside TTFT."},{"term":"Tail latency","def":"The slow end of the latency distribution, such as p95 or p99. Fan-out makes rare slow calls common at the request level."},{"term":"Server-sent events (SSE)","def":"A one-way HTTP streaming format (`text/event-stream`) used to deliver tokens as they are generated, with ids and reconnection support."},{"term":"MQA (multi-query attention)","def":"Attention where all query heads share one key and value head, which shrinks the KV cache and decode memory traffic at minor quality cost."},{"term":"Block table","def":"The mapping from a sequence's logical KV blocks to physical blocks in PagedAttention. Two tables can point to one shared block."},{"term":"RadixAttention","def":"SGLang's radix tree over token sequences that stores KV so prefixes are reused automatically across calls."},{"term":"Acceptance rate","def":"In speculative decoding, the chance that a drafted token is accepted by the target model. It drives the expected tokens per target pass."},{"term":"Quantization","def":"Storing weights, activations or the KV cache in fewer bits to save memory and bandwidth, at some quality risk that needs an eval gate."},{"term":"Cache hit ratio","def":"Cached tokens over prompt tokens for provider prompt caches. On Anthropic, cache reads over input plus reads plus creation tokens."},{"term":"prompt_cache_key","def":"An OpenAI and Azure request field that improves routing of requests sharing a prefix. It never pins a machine or guarantees a hit."},{"term":"Cache lifetime (TTL)","def":"How long an entry stays eligible after its last write or reuse: at least 30 minutes on GPT-5.6+, 5 minutes or 1 hour on Anthropic."},{"term":"cache_salt","def":"A per-request vLLM value mixed into the first block hash so only requests with the same salt share prefix blocks, which isolates tenants."},{"term":"Exact-match response cache","def":"A cache keyed on a hash of the normalised request plus scope and versions. It returns a stored answer only for an identical request."},{"term":"Embedding cache","def":"A cache of vectors keyed on a content hash plus the embedding model and version, which saves embedding calls for unchanged text."},{"term":"Hedged request","def":"A duplicate request sent after about the p95 delay, with the first reply used and the other cancelled, to cut tail latency on idempotent calls."},{"term":"Critical path","def":"The longest chain of dependent stages in a request. Latency is the sum along it, so overlapping stages shorten the total."}],"levels":[{"id":"w02-l1","order":1,"title":"Read the stopwatch","kind":"concept","minutes":15,"brief":"A user says the assistant feels slow. Before you touch anything, decide what you are timing: the wait for the first word, the speed of the words after it, or the whole answer. Prompt length, output length and traffic shape are not given yet.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Four stages and three numbers","level":2,"id":"four-stages-and-three-numbers"},{"t":"p","md":"Every request passes through four stages: the prompt is sent, the request **queues**, the model runs **prefill** over the whole prompt, then **decode** generates one token at a time. TTFT (time to first token) covers queueing, prefill and network time, so it grows with prompt length. TPOT (time per output token, also called inter-token latency or ITL) sets how smooth a stream feels. TTLT (time to last token) is the total [[r13]].\n\nThe three numbers answer three different questions, and a fix for one rarely moves another. Decide which one the product is judged on before you pick a lever."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w02-l1-timeline","title":"One request on a timeline","alt":"Sequence diagram. The client sends a request, it waits in a scheduler queue, prefill reads the whole prompt and builds the KV cache, decode returns the first token and then one token per step until the last token.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant C as Client\n  participant Q as Scheduler queue\n  participant P as Prefill\n  participant D as Decode\n  C->>Q: Request with prompt\n  Q->>P: Admit when capacity frees\n  P->>P: Read whole prompt, build KV cache\n  P->>D: Hand over KV cache\n  D-->>C: First token (TTFT ends)\n  D-->>C: Token 2, 3, 4 ... one per step\n  D-->>C: Last token (TTLT ends)","caption":"Queueing, prefill and network sit before the first token. Everything after it is decode."},{"t":"table","caption":"The numbers, what moves them and who feels them","head":["Metric","Definition","Moves with","Who feels it"],"rows":[["**TTFT**","Queueing + prefill + network until the first token","Prompt length, queue depth, prefix caching, distance to the endpoint","Anyone watching a stream. Voice assistants most, because silence is audible"],["**TPOT or ITL**","`(e2e_latency - TTFT) / (output_tokens - 1)`","Model size, batch load, KV cache size, decode speed","Readers of a stream. A value above reading speed is wasted, below it feels stuck"],["**TTLT or e2e**","`TTFT + generation time`","Output token count above all, including hidden reasoning tokens","Non-streaming clients, batch jobs, and agents that need the whole tool call before the next step"],["**TPS per user**","Output length over e2e latency, tends to `1 / ITL`","Same drivers as TPOT","Capacity planners comparing hardware"],["**System TPS**","Total output tokens over the benchmark window","Concurrency. Rises to a saturation point while per-user TPS falls","Anyone dividing cost by throughput"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"formula","title":"Total latency from parts","expr":"TTLT = TTFT + (TBT x tokens_generated)","vars":[{"k":"TTFT","v":"time to the first token, including queueing and prefill"},{"k":"TBT","v":"time between tokens, the same quantity as TPOT or ITL"},{"k":"tokens_generated","v":"all generated tokens, including hidden reasoning tokens that count in completion tokens"}],"example":"TTFT 0.6 s, TBT 25 ms and 400 tokens give 0.6 + 0.025 x 400 = **10.6 s**. Halve the output and TTLT falls to 5.6 s. Halve the prompt and TTFT might fall by 0.1 s. Azure's guidance says to check output token counts before declaring a latency regression [[r14]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Prefill and decode are different kinds of work","head":["Phase","Work per iteration","Bottleneck","Per-iteration latency","What helps"],"rows":[["**Prefill**","Whole prompt in parallel","Compute: it saturates the GPU","High","Cache the prefix, shorten the prompt, chunk long prompts, scale compute"],["**Decode**","One token per request","Memory bandwidth: weights and KV are streamed for each token","Low","Batch many decodes together, shrink KV, use GQA or MLA, quantize, speculate"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"failure","title":"Latency symptoms and what they usually mean","items":[{"symptom":"TTLT doubled after a release, TTFT is flat","cause":"Output tokens grew: a prompt change made answers longer, or a reasoning model is spending more hidden tokens","fix":"Bucket TTLT by output tokens and compare `reasoning_tokens` in the usage block before blaming the platform"},{"symptom":"TTFT p95 jumps at the same hour daily while prompts are unchanged","cause":"Queueing on a deployment shared with a batch job or another workload","fix":"Give each workload its own deployment, or buy a priority or provisioned tier. Check the queue-time share of TTFT"},{"symptom":"The page looks frozen but the answer arrives eventually","cause":"The client waits for the whole response, so there is no perceived progress and long answers hit client timeouts","fix":"Stream with `stream: true`. Total time stays the same, time to first visible word drops"},{"symptom":"Two benchmark tools report different ITL for the same server","cause":"One includes TTFT in the average and the other excludes it","fix":"Pin the definition in your SLO and compute it from raw timestamps yourself"}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"What a senior answer sounds like","md":"Ask which number the user feels. A streaming chat is judged on TTFT p95 and a smooth TPOT. Voice is judged on TTFT and on staying ahead of speech. A batch summary is judged on TTLT and cost. An agent step is judged on TTLT, because a half-streamed tool call cannot run. Then say that streaming changes perception, not total time, and that cutting output tokens is usually the biggest lever [[r15]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-l1-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A streaming assistant starts answering about 6 s after the question on weekday mornings, then streams smoothly. A retrieval change grew the prompt from 2,000 to 30,000 tokens. Output length did not change. Which stage grew?","options":["Decode, because the model emits tokens more slowly after the change","Prefill, because the model reads 15 times more input first","Network, because longer prompts need much more upload bandwidth","Output formatting, because streaming adds overhead on every chunk"],"answer":1,"explain":"A smooth stream after a late start points at TTFT, and TTFT grows with prompt length because prefill must read the whole prompt first. Decode speed would show as slow tokens after the start. Text prompts are small on the wire, so upload time is rarely the cause. Chunk overhead does not scale with prompt length.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r13"},{"id":"w02-l1-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"TTFT is 0.8 s, time between tokens is 20 ms and the model generates 500 tokens. What is the total time to last token?","answer":10.8,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"seconds","hint":"TTLT = TTFT + TBT x tokens generated","explain":"0.8 + 0.020 x 500 = 0.8 + 10 = 10.8 s. Note that output generation is 93% of the total, which is why cutting output tokens beats trimming the prompt for total time.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r14"},{"id":"w02-l1-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select every statement that is true.","options":["Streaming does not change total generation time but lowers the time to the first visible response.","Halving output tokens can cut latency by about half, while halving the prompt may gain only 1% to 5%.","Batching decode requests slows each request in proportion to batch size because decode is compute-bound.","Azure's normalized TTFT metric divides first-byte time by prompt tokens and is only for comparing across prompt sizes."],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Streaming and the output-versus-input rule of thumb come from the OpenAI latency guide and Azure's guidance. The normalized TTFT metric exists for cross-prompt comparison. The third statement is wrong: decode is memory-bandwidth bound, so batching amortizes weight reads and per-request slowdown is well below proportional until the GPU saturates.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r15"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r13","r14","r15","r9"],"takeaways":["TTFT, TPOT and TTLT answer different questions, so name the one the product is judged on","TTFT grows with prompt length and queueing. TTLT is dominated by output tokens, including hidden reasoning tokens","Prefill is compute-bound and decode is memory-bound, which is why the fixes differ","Streaming changes perception, not total time","Pin metric definitions in your SLO, because tools disagree on ITL"]},{"id":"w02-l2","order":2,"title":"Keep the GPU busy without stalling users","kind":"problem","minutes":15,"brief":"Many users share one model. Long prompts stall short ones, single streams crawl, and the GPU idles between requests. The model size, GPU count, traffic mix and whether you host the model yourself are all open.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Why decode batches well and prefill does not","level":2,"id":"why-decode-batches-well-and-prefill-does-not"},{"t":"p","md":"Prefill runs the whole prompt in parallel, so one prefill already saturates compute and has high latency per iteration. Decode emits one token per request per iteration, so it uses little compute and is limited by memory bandwidth. That makes batching many decodes together very effective. Mixing a long prefill into a running decode batch is the throughput-latency tension: the prefill makes every stream wait [[r9]].\n\nThe five levers below each attack a different symptom. Only the first is a baseline you assume. The rest you add when the symptom appears."},{"t":"compare","title":"Serving levers and what each one fixes","intro":"These are engine and architecture choices, so they apply when you run the model yourself. On a hosted API your levers are in the table after the board.","options":[{"name":"Continuous batching","tag":"Baseline","summary":"Schedule at iteration level. Finished requests leave and waiting ones join at the next step, instead of holding the batch until its slowest request ends.","pros":["Keeps the GPU full as requests come and go","Short requests do not wait behind long ones"],"cons":["Does not remove prefill and decode interference by itself","Needs paged KV memory to admit and evict cheaply"],"bestWhen":"Any multi-user serving. It is the default in modern engines.","avoidWhen":"A single local user, where there is nothing to batch.","meta":[{"k":"Fixes","v":"idle GPU between requests"},{"k":"Cost","v":"none"}]},{"name":"Chunked prefill","tag":"Engine flag","summary":"Split a long prompt into near-equal chunks that share iterations with decodes, so streams keep moving while the prompt is read.","pros":["Stall-free schedules and steadier inter-token latency","Sarathi-Serve reported 2.6x capacity on Mistral-7B with one A100 and up to 3.7x on Yi-34B with two, under tail-latency limits [[r9]]"],"cons":["The chunked request itself sees a higher TTFT","Chunk size needs tuning against your latency targets"],"bestWhen":"Mixed prompt lengths and a TPOT target that long prompts keep breaking.","avoidWhen":"Uniformly short prompts, where there is little to chunk.","meta":[{"k":"Fixes","v":"decode stalls"},{"k":"Cost","v":"some TTFT on long prompts"}]},{"name":"Prefill and decode disaggregation","tag":"Architecture","summary":"Run prefill and decode on separate machine pools so each can be sized and tuned to its own target [[r28]] [[r29]].","pros":["Removes interference between phases","TTFT and TPOT targets are tuned independently"],"cons":["KV cache must move between machines","Two pools and a more complex scheduler to run"],"bestWhen":"A large fleet with separate TTFT and TPOT objectives and saturated load.","avoidWhen":"One node or low load, where two pools sit half idle.","meta":[{"k":"Fixes","v":"phase interference at scale"},{"k":"Cost","v":"KV transfer, complexity"}]},{"name":"Speculative decoding","tag":"Latency","summary":"A cheap draft proposes several tokens and the large model verifies them in parallel. The output distribution is unchanged.","pros":["2x to 3x faster on T5-XXL in the original paper, with identical outputs [[r19]]","Helps the single-stream experience the most"],"cons":["Needs a draft that agrees often with the large model","Gains shrink once batches are compute-bound (general reasoning, not a figure from the paper)"],"bestWhen":"Light or moderate load and a per-stream speed target.","avoidWhen":"Saturated batches, where verification competes for the compute that batching already uses.","meta":[{"k":"Fixes","v":"slow single streams"},{"k":"Cost","v":"draft model and memory"}]},{"name":"FlashAttention","tag":"Kernel","summary":"Exact attention that tiles the computation to cut reads and writes to high-bandwidth memory. Same result as standard attention [[r21]].","pros":["No quality trade, it is exact","Cuts memory traffic that long contexts would otherwise multiply"],"cons":["A kernel choice tied to hardware generation, not a scheduling lever","Does not change KV cache size"],"bestWhen":"Long contexts on supported GPUs. Treat it as a default to confirm, not a decision.","avoidWhen":"You expect it to fix queueing or batching problems. It does not.","meta":[{"k":"Fixes","v":"attention cost at long context"},{"k":"Cost","v":"hardware support"}]}]},{"t":"dials","id":"w02-l2-serving","title":"Pick the serving lever","prompt":"Flip the symptom, the load and the fleet shape. KV quantization and prefix caching are covered in the next levels.","dials":[{"id":"symptom","label":"Symptom","values":[{"id":"stall","label":"Streams stall when a long prompt arrives"},{"id":"slow","label":"Each stream is slow even when idle"},{"id":"mem","label":"Batch size is capped by KV memory"}]},{"id":"load","label":"Load","values":[{"id":"light","label":"Light, few concurrent requests"},{"id":"heavy","label":"Heavy, batches saturated"}]},{"id":"fleet","label":"Fleet","values":[{"id":"one","label":"One GPU node"},{"id":"many","label":"Many nodes, separate TTFT and TPOT targets"}]}],"options":[{"id":"chunk","name":"Chunked prefill","summary":"Split long prefills so decodes keep running."},{"id":"spec","name":"Speculative decoding","summary":"Draft and verify to speed one stream."},{"id":"kvq","name":"KV cache quantization","summary":"Store keys and values in fewer bits."},{"id":"disagg","name":"Prefill and decode disaggregation","summary":"Separate machine pools per phase."},{"id":"prefix","name":"Engine prefix caching","summary":"Reuse KV for shared prompt prefixes."}],"scores":{"chunk":{"symptom":{"stall":{"s":2,"why":"It exists to stop long prefills from freezing running decodes."},"slow":{"s":0,"why":"A lone stream has no prefill to interleave with."},"mem":{"s":0,"why":"It does not change how much KV memory is used."}},"load":{"light":{"s":0,"why":"At low load few prefills collide with decodes."},"heavy":{"s":2,"why":"Interference peaks when batches are saturated."}},"fleet":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"A scheduler setting on the one engine you have."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"Still useful, but it does not isolate TTFT from TPOT targets."}}},"spec":{"symptom":{"stall":{"s":0,"why":"It does not touch prefill interference."},"slow":{"s":2,"why":"Verifying several tokens per large-model pass speeds a single stream."},"mem":{"s":-1,"why":"A draft model and extra verification add memory pressure."}},"load":{"light":{"s":2,"why":"Spare compute absorbs the verification work."},"heavy":{"s":-1,"why":"Gains shrink once batches already use the compute."}},"fleet":{"one":{"s":0,"why":"Per-node feature with no fleet effect."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"Per-node feature with no fleet effect."}}},"kvq":{"symptom":{"stall":{"s":0,"why":"It does not change scheduling."},"slow":{"s":1,"why":"Less KV traffic per token helps decode somewhat."},"mem":{"s":2,"why":"KIVI at 2 bits cut peak memory 2.6x and allowed up to 4x batch size."}},"load":{"light":{"s":-1,"why":"Accuracy risk with no capacity you need."},"heavy":{"s":2,"why":"Bigger batches turn the saved memory into throughput."}},"fleet":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"Fits more sequences on the node you have."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"Same gain per node, no extra fleet benefit."}}},"disagg":{"symptom":{"stall":{"s":2,"why":"Prefill runs elsewhere, so decodes are never interrupted."},"slow":{"s":0,"why":"Decode machines still run the model at the same speed."},"mem":{"s":0,"why":"KV still has to live somewhere, and it also has to move between pools."}},"load":{"light":{"s":-2,"why":"Two pools and KV transfer waste machines at low load."},"heavy":{"s":1,"why":"Pays off once both phases are saturated."}},"fleet":{"one":{"s":-2,"why":"Needs at least two pools to exist."},"many":{"s":2,"why":"Each pool is sized for its own TTFT or TPOT target."}}},"prefix":{"symptom":{"stall":{"s":1,"why":"Skipping a shared prefix shortens the prefill that causes stalls."},"slow":{"s":0,"why":"It does not speed decode."},"mem":{"s":1,"why":"Shared prefix blocks are stored once."}},"load":{"light":{"s":0,"why":"Helps only if prefixes repeat, regardless of load."},"heavy":{"s":1,"why":"Saved prefill compute matters most when compute is scarce."}},"fleet":{"one":{"s":1,"why":"All repeats land on the same cache."},"many":{"s":0,"why":"Needs prefix-aware routing so repeats reach the node that holds the blocks."}}}},"takeaway":"Pick by symptom first, then check load and fleet shape. A lever that wins at light load can lose at saturation, and the reverse."},{"t":"table","caption":"On a hosted API you do not own the scheduler. These are your levers","head":["You want to fix","Self-hosted lever","Hosted API lever"],"rows":[["Slow TTFT on long prompts","Prefix caching, chunked prefill","Prompt caching, shorter prompts, a smaller model"],["Slow or jerky streaming","Speculative decoding, GQA or MLA model, quantization","A faster model, a faster tier, less load on a shared deployment"],["Queueing at peak","More replicas, disaggregation","A priority or provisioned tier, separate deployments per workload"],["Total time too long","Shorter outputs","Lower `max_tokens`, stop sequences, concise output format"]]},{"t":"accordion","items":[{"title":"Go deeper: why speculative decoding loses at high load","blocks":[{"t":"p","md":"Speculation trades extra compute for fewer sequential large-model steps. At low batch sizes decode leaves compute idle, so the extra verification is nearly free. When the batch is large enough to use that compute, every wasted draft token costs real throughput. This is engineering reasoning, not a figure from the cited paper. The safe habit is to measure on your own traffic at your own batch sizes [[r19]]."}]}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-l2-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Inter-token latency spikes for every active stream each time one request with a 40,000-token prompt arrives. Average load is moderate. Which change targets this?","options":["Quantize the KV cache to 8 bits so more requests fit in memory","Add a draft model for speculative decoding so each active stream decodes faster","Enable chunked prefill so the long prompt shares iterations with decodes","Raise the maximum batch size so more requests decode at the same time"],"answer":2,"explain":"The stall happens because a long prefill occupies whole iterations and running decodes wait. Chunked prefill splits it so decodes keep moving. KV quantization changes memory, not scheduling. Speculative decoding speeds decode but does not stop prefill from blocking it. A larger batch adds more work to each iteration and makes the interference worse.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r9"},{"id":"w02-l2-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each technique to what it does.","pairs":[{"l":"Continuous batching","r":"Finished requests leave and waiting ones join at every iteration"},{"l":"Chunked prefill","r":"Splits a long prompt so decodes keep running between chunks"},{"l":"Disaggregation","r":"Runs prefill and decode on separate machines tuned to TTFT and TPOT"},{"l":"Speculative decoding","r":"A draft proposes tokens and the large model verifies them in one pass"}],"explain":"Each technique answers one problem: idle GPU, decode stalls, phase interference at scale, and slow single streams. The pairs are not interchangeable, which is why the symptom comes first.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r9"},{"id":"w02-l2-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Speculative decoding gave a clear single-stream speedup on a lightly loaded test box but little on the saturated production cluster. Which explanation fits best?","options":["At high batch sizes the GPU is already busy, so verification passes compete for compute that batching already uses","Speculative decoding changes the output distribution, so the production quality gate rejects most of the drafted tokens","The draft model cannot run while more than one request is active, so production silently falls back to normal decoding","Production prompts are longer, which disables the verification step and forces the large model to decode every token alone"],"answer":0,"explain":"Decode normally leaves compute idle, which is what speculation spends. A saturated batch has no idle compute left, so gains shrink. Speculative decoding keeps the output distribution unchanged, drafts can run alongside many requests, and long prompts do not disable verification.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r19"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r9","r19","r21","r28","r29","r20","r25"],"takeaways":["Prefill is compute-bound and decode is memory-bound, so mixing them creates stalls","Match the lever to the symptom: chunked prefill for stalls, speculation for slow streams, KV quantization for memory caps","Disaggregation pays only at fleet scale with saturated, separate TTFT and TPOT targets","Speculative decoding keeps output identical but its gains shrink as batches saturate","On a hosted API your levers are prompt size, output size, model, caching and capacity tier"]},{"id":"w02-l3","order":3,"title":"Size the KV cache before it sizes you","kind":"lab","minutes":20,"brief":"A model must serve many long conversations on a fixed GPU. The architecture, KV precision, context length and concurrency are all open. Find which dial caps concurrency, and which of your options can still move.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"The formula that sizes everything","level":2,"id":"the-formula-that-sizes-everything"},{"t":"formula","title":"KV cache size","expr":"kv_bytes = 2 x n_layers x n_kv_heads x head_dim x bytes_per_element x tokens x batch","vars":[{"k":"2","v":"one key vector and one value vector per token"},{"k":"n_kv_heads","v":"KV heads, not query heads. This is where GQA and MQA save memory"},{"k":"bytes_per_element","v":"2 for fp16 or bf16, 1 for fp8, 0.5 for 4-bit"},{"k":"tokens x batch","v":"context length of each sequence times concurrent sequences"}],"example":"A Llama 3 8B shape (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head dim 128, bf16) stores 2 x 32 x 8 x 128 x 2 = **131,072 bytes, 128 KiB per token**, so 1 GiB at 8,192 tokens. A Llama 3 70B shape (80 layers, 8 KV heads, head dim 128) stores **320 KiB per token**, 2.5 GiB at 8,192 tokens. These sizes are arithmetic from the published configs [[r27]]. Every decode step reads this cache, so at long context KV bytes cap concurrency before the weights do."},{"t":"lab","id":"kv-cache","intro":"Start with the 7B-class MHA preset, then switch to the GQA preset and compare KV per sequence. Lower KV precision, then stretch the context. Watch the three paging rows: contiguous reservation, paged blocks, and paged blocks with a shared prefix. The shapes are illustrative, not vendor models."},{"t":"compare","title":"Attention variants decide KV size at model selection","intro":"You choose these when you pick a model, not later with a serving flag.","options":[{"name":"MHA","tag":"Baseline","summary":"One KV head per query head. The largest cache.","pros":["Highest quality reference","Simple kernels everywhere"],"cons":["KV memory and bandwidth scale with every head","Concurrency collapses first at long context"],"bestWhen":"Small models or short contexts where KV is not the limit.","avoidWhen":"Long context with high concurrency.","meta":[{"k":"KV vs MHA","v":"1x"}]},{"name":"MQA","summary":"All query heads share a single KV head.","pros":["Smallest KV and least memory traffic","Fast decode"],"cons":["Minor quality loss reported in the GQA paper [[r10]]","Hard to retrofit"],"bestWhen":"Decode speed and memory dominate and a small quality cost is acceptable.","avoidWhen":"Quality-critical tasks where the loss shows up in your evals.","meta":[{"k":"KV vs MHA","v":"1 / n_heads"}]},{"name":"GQA","tag":"Common choice","summary":"A group of query heads shares each KV head, with more than one but fewer KV heads than query heads.","pros":["Quality near MHA at speed near MQA after uptraining with about 5% of pre-training compute [[r10]]","KV shrinks by `heads / kv_heads`"],"cons":["Still scales linearly with context and batch"],"bestWhen":"General serving of dense models. It is the usual default.","avoidWhen":"You need the largest possible cut and can adopt a latent design.","meta":[{"k":"KV vs MHA","v":"kv_heads / n_heads"}]},{"name":"MLA","tag":"Model design","summary":"Compress keys and values into a latent vector and cache that instead.","pros":["DeepSeek-V2 reports a 93.3% smaller KV cache and 5.76x peak generation throughput versus DeepSeek 67B [[r11]]"],"cons":["Chosen when the model is trained, not a serving flag","Needs engine support for the attention variant"],"bestWhen":"You can select the model and KV memory is the binding limit.","avoidWhen":"You must stay on an existing checkpoint with a different design.","meta":[{"k":"KV vs MHA","v":"93.3% smaller (paper)"}]}]},{"t":"h","text":"Paging, sharing and quantization","level":2,"id":"paging-sharing-and-quantization"},{"t":"diagram","id":"w02-l3-paged","title":"Two requests sharing a prefix in paged memory","alt":"Two block tables, one for request A and one for request B, both point to the same physical block holding the shared prefix. Each also points to its own private block. All blocks live in one GPU memory pool.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Request A block table] --> S1[Block 1 shared prefix]\n  B[Request B block table] --> S1\n  A --> A2[Block 2 only A]\n  B --> B2[Block 2 only B]\n  S1 --> M[GPU block pool]\n  A2 --> M\n  B2 --> M","caption":"Fixed-size blocks mean only the last block of a sequence can be partly empty, and identical prefix blocks are stored once [[r6]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"KV memory levers, where you set them and what limits them","head":["Lever","Effect","Set at","Evidence and limits"],"rows":[["GQA or MLA model","Fewer or compressed KV entries per token","Model selection","Shrinks by `heads / kv_heads`. MLA reported 93.3% [[r10]] [[r11]]"],["KV quantization","Fewer bytes per element","Serving config","KIVI at 2 bits: tuning-free, 2.6x less peak memory, up to 4x batch, 2.35x to 3.47x throughput, near-equal quality. Validate on your own long-context evals [[r20]]"],["Paged blocks","Near-zero waste from over-reservation","Engine","PagedAttention reported 2x to 4x throughput at equal latency versus 2023 systems [[r6]]"],["Prefix sharing","Shared blocks stored once, prefill skipped","Engine config","vLLM caches only full blocks, hashed over tokens, parent hash and extras such as LoRA id and `cache_salt`. SGLang keeps a radix tree and reported up to 6.4x throughput on structured programs [[r7]] [[r8]]"],["Shorter or compacted context","Fewer tokens per sequence","Application","Linear: half the tokens, half the KV"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"failure","title":"KV failure modes","items":[{"symptom":"Out of memory on long conversations although the weights loaded fine","cause":"KV grows with context times concurrent sequences, and capacity was sized from short prompts","fix":"Compute the KV budget as GPU memory minus reserve minus weights, then cap concurrency or context, or pick GQA or MLA, or quantize"},{"symptom":"Capacity plan is off by 4x to 8x for a GQA model","cause":"The estimate used query heads instead of KV heads","fix":"Read `num_key_value_heads` from the model config, not `num_attention_heads`"},{"symptom":"Throughput improved after KV quantization but long-document answers got worse","cause":"Low-bit KV adds error that accumulates over long contexts","fix":"Gate the change on long-context evals, keep 8-bit as the fallback, and watch quality by context length"},{"symptom":"One tenant can infer another's prompts by timing responses on a shared prefix cache","cause":"Prefix blocks are shared across tenants without isolation","fix":"Set a per-request `cache_salt` in vLLM. Treat the faster `xxhash` option with care because the doc warns about collisions and cross-tenant leakage [[r7]]"}]}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-l3-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A model has 40 layers, 8 KV heads and head dimension 128, with fp16 KV. How many GiB of KV cache do 16 concurrent sequences of 8,192 tokens need?","answer":20,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"GiB","hint":"bytes per token = 2 x layers x kv_heads x head_dim x bytes","explain":"Per token: 2 x 40 x 8 x 128 x 2 = 163,840 bytes (160 KiB). Per sequence: 160 KiB x 8,192 = 1.25 GiB. For 16 sequences: 1.25 x 16 = 20 GiB. Using 32 query heads instead of 8 KV heads would give 80 GiB, a common mistake.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r27"},{"id":"w02-l3-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"What is wrong with this capacity estimate for a Llama-style GQA model with 80 layers, 64 query heads, 8 KV heads and head dimension 128?","code":{"lang":"python","code":"def kv_bytes(n_layers, n_heads, head_dim, tokens, batch, dtype_bytes=2):\n    return 2 * n_layers * n_heads * head_dim * dtype_bytes * tokens * batch\n\nprint(kv_bytes(80, 64, 128, tokens=8192, batch=8) / 2**30)"},"options":["Nothing is wrong, because the formula uses the head count the model reports","dtype_bytes should be 4, because the KV cache is stored in fp32","It multiplies by batch, but KV is shared across the whole batch","It passes query heads, so it overestimates a GQA model by 64 / 8 = 8x"],"answer":3,"explain":"KV size depends on KV heads. With 8 KV heads the answer is 20 GiB, but the code prints 160 GiB. The KV cache is usually fp16 or bf16, not fp32, and each sequence has its own KV so batch multiplies the total.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r10"},{"id":"w02-l3-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Select the changes that reduce the KV bytes stored per token of a single sequence.","options":["Use a model with 8 KV heads instead of 32","Store KV in fp8 instead of fp16","Switch the engine to PagedAttention blocks","Cache a shared system prompt with prefix caching"],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"Fewer KV heads and fewer bytes per element both shrink each token's entry. PagedAttention removes waste from over-reservation but each token still needs the same bytes. Prefix caching stores shared blocks once across sequences, which saves memory overall, but does not change the size of a token's entry.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r6"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r6","r7","r8","r10","r11","r20","r24","r27"],"takeaways":["KV bytes per token = 2 x layers x KV heads x head dim x bytes. Use KV heads, not query heads","At long context the KV cache, not the weights, caps concurrency","GQA and MLA are model choices. Quantization, paging and prefix sharing are serving choices","KV quantization needs an eval gate because errors grow with context length","Shared prefix caches need per-tenant isolation or they leak through timing"]},{"id":"w02-l4","order":4,"title":"Rent the prefix: how providers sell caching","kind":"problem","minutes":15,"brief":"You call a hosted model with a long, mostly repeated prompt. Vendors cache the prefix differently, charge differently for writes and expire entries on different clocks. The model family, request spacing and retention rules are all open.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"What a provider cache stores, and what it charges","level":2,"id":"what-a-provider-cache-stores-and-what-it-charges"},{"t":"p","md":"A provider cache stores the **KV tensors** for a prompt prefix, not the text. A request hits only if its prompt starts with the same tokens as a stored entry, so it saves prefill time and prefill billing but never changes the answer [[r1]]. On GPT-5.6 and later, a token is billed at exactly one of three rates: uncached, cached or cache write. Prefix order matters. Anthropic builds the prefix as `tools`, then `system`, then `messages` [[r3]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Provider rules as of 2026-10-13. Check the linked pages before you commit to a number","head":["Rule","OpenAI and Azure, GPT-5.6 and later","OpenAI and Azure, before GPT-5.6","Anthropic"],"rows":[["**Minimum prefix**","1,024 visible input tokens. Hidden system tokens do not count","1,024 tokens. On Azure, hits then count in 128-token steps","512, 1,024, 2,048 or 4,096 by model. Shorter prompts run uncached with no error"],["**How it starts**","On by default (`implicit`) or `explicit` breakpoints you mark","Implicit only","One top-level `cache_control` (automatic) or `cache_control` on blocks, up to 4 breakpoints"],["**Write charge**","1.25x the uncached input rate","None","1.25x for 5 minutes, 2x for 1 hour"],["**Read charge**","0.1x, and 0.05x on GPT-6.1 Sol","Discounted, up to 95% per the OpenAI guide","0.1x on most models, 0.05x on Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5, 0.025x on Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1"],["**Lifetime**","`ttl` of `30m`, the default and only value. At least 30 minutes after the latest write or reuse","`in_memory` 5 to 10 minutes idle and up to 1 hour, or `24h`, depending on model and retention policy","5 minutes by default, 1 hour optional. A hit refreshes the entry at no charge"],["**Usage fields**","`cached_tokens`. Azure adds `cache_write_tokens`","`cached_tokens`, rounded down to a multiple of 128","`cache_read_input_tokens` and `cache_creation_input_tokens`"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"OpenAI or Azure, GPT-5.6+","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"Responses API request with an explicit breakpoint","code":"{\n  \"model\": \"<gpt-5.6 or later deployment>\",\n  \"prompt_cache_key\": \"tenant-42:support-v7\",\n  \"prompt_cache_options\": { \"mode\": \"explicit\", \"ttl\": \"30m\" },\n  \"input\": [\n    { \"role\": \"developer\", \"content\": [\n      { \"type\": \"input_text\",\n        \"text\": \"<long policy, tool guidance, reference material>\",\n        \"prompt_cache_breakpoint\": { \"mode\": \"explicit\" } } ] },\n    { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [ { \"type\": \"input_text\", \"text\": \"<the question>\" } ] }\n  ]\n}","caption":"Illustrative shape from the 2026-10-13 docs [[r1]]. Content after the last explicit breakpoint is billed at the uncached rate with no write charge. Top-level `instructions` cannot hold a breakpoint, so put reusable instructions in a developer message."}]},{"label":"Anthropic","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"Messages request with a 1-hour breakpoint","code":"{\n  \"model\": \"<model>\",\n  \"max_tokens\": 1024,\n  \"system\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\",\n      \"text\": \"<long instructions and reference material>\",\n      \"cache_control\": { \"type\": \"ephemeral\", \"ttl\": \"1h\" } }\n  ],\n  \"messages\": [ { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"<the question>\" } ]\n}","caption":"Omit `ttl` for the 5-minute default. A top-level `cache_control` instead enables automatic caching, which moves the breakpoint to the last cacheable block as the conversation grows and uses one of the 4 breakpoint slots [[r3]]."}]},{"label":"Read the usage","blocks":[{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"Anthropic usage block","code":"{ \"usage\": {\n    \"input_tokens\": 2048,\n    \"cache_read_input_tokens\": 1800,\n    \"cache_creation_input_tokens\": 248,\n    \"output_tokens\": 503,\n    \"cache_creation\": { \"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens\": 148,\n                        \"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens\": 100 } } }","caption":"Zero in both cache fields means the request was not cached. On Azure, Chat Completions reports `prompt_tokens_details.cached_tokens`. Track hits as cached tokens divided by prompt tokens and alert when the ratio drops."}]}]},{"t":"formula","title":"Break-even for a write premium","expr":"cost_cached(N) = w + r x (N - 1)   versus   cost_plain(N) = N","vars":[{"k":"w","v":"write multiplier, for example 1.25 for OpenAI GPT-5.6+ and Anthropic 5-minute, 2 for Anthropic 1-hour"},{"k":"r","v":"read multiplier, for example 0.1"},{"k":"N","v":"requests that use the prefix inside one entry lifetime"}],"example":"With w = 1.25 and r = 0.1: one request costs 1.25 versus 1, a 25% loss. Two requests cost 1.35 versus 2, already a win. Ten cost 2.15 versus 10, the example in OpenAI's guide [[r1]]. With Anthropic's 1-hour write at w = 2, two requests cost 2.1 versus 2 and three cost 2.2 versus 3. A steady stream spaced under 5 minutes refreshes the cheap entry for free, so the 1-hour TTL pays only for gaps between 5 and 60 minutes [[r3]]."},{"t":"compare","title":"Four ways to configure provider caching","intro":"Dials: how often the prefix repeats inside the lifetime, how stable it is, the write premium, and your data retention policy.","options":[{"name":"Implicit default","tag":"GA","summary":"Let the provider place breakpoints. On GPT-5.6 and later it puts one at the end of the latest eligible message.","pros":["No code change","Fits append-only chat history"],"cons":["Writes content that may never be reused, at 1.25x","Dynamic text early in the prompt defeats it"],"bestWhen":"Append-only conversations with a stable prefix first.","avoidWhen":"Segments change at different rates, or the tail is rarely reused.","meta":[{"k":"Write slots","v":"uses 1 of 4"}]},{"name":"Explicit breakpoints","tag":"GA","summary":"Mark where each reusable segment ends: tools, system, a reference file, history. Up to 4 cache writes per request, 3 once the implicit one is used.","pros":["You choose what is written, so changing tails cost no write premium","Segments with different change rates keep separate entries"],"cons":["Slots are limited, and Anthropic only looks back 20 block positions for an earlier write","Explicit mode with no breakpoints means no caching at all"],"bestWhen":"Several segments with different change rates, or content you know is never reused.","avoidWhen":"A simple chat, where the extra control buys nothing.","meta":[{"k":"Lookup","v":"first 2 and latest 50 explicit breakpoints"}]},{"name":"Prewarm","tag":"GPT-5.6+","summary":"Send a Responses request with `prompt_cache_options.prewarm: true` to fill the cache without generating output.","pros":["The first real user request already hits","Good for startup of shared instructions and tool definitions"],"cons":["Prewarmed tokens are billed at the write rate","Wasted if no request follows inside the lifetime"],"bestWhen":"First-token latency of the first request matters.","avoidWhen":"Low reuse, where the write is never read.","meta":[{"k":"Billing","v":"write rate"}]},{"name":"Longer retention","summary":"Anthropic 1-hour TTL, or `24h` retention on models before GPT-5.6. On GPT-5.6 and later the only `ttl` is `30m`.","pros":["Survives gaps longer than 5 to 10 minutes","Fewer repeat writes for low-traffic prefixes"],"cons":["Anthropic 1-hour writes cost 2x","Extended retention stores KV on GPU-local storage, so check data retention and residency rules"],"bestWhen":"Gaps between requests of 5 to 60 minutes on the same prefix.","avoidWhen":"A steady stream under 5 minutes, which keeps the cheap entry alive for free.","meta":[{"k":"Anthropic write","v":"2x for 1 hour"}]}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Changed with GPT-5.6, checked 2026-10-13","md":"GPT-5.6 and later added write charges, explicit breakpoints and `prompt_cache_options`, removed the 128-token rounding and deprecated `prompt_cache_retention` on Azure. The model version is dated 2026-07-09. The date the docs changed was not stated. On Azure, models before GPT-5.6 return a 400 error if sent `prompt_cache_options` or `prompt_cache_breakpoint` [[r2]]. Gemini and Bedrock follow the same idea with their own minimums and discounts, and Bedrock does not support caching with batch inference [[r4]] [[r5]]."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-l4-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"On Anthropic with a 5-minute write at 1.25x and reads at 0.1x, a prefix is used by 4 requests inside one entry lifetime. What does the prefix cost, as a multiple of one uncached read of it? (Uncached, the four requests cost 4.0.)","answer":1.55,"tolerancePct":2,"hint":"w + r x (N - 1)","explain":"One write plus three reads: 1.25 + 0.1 x 3 = 1.55, about 39% of the 4.0 uncached cost. With a single request the same prefix would cost 1.25, a loss versus 1.0.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r3"},{"id":"w02-l4-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"On Azure with a model before GPT-5.6, a 3,000-token prompt shows `cached_tokens: 0` on every call. The system prompt starts with the line below. Which cause fits?","code":{"lang":"text","code":"System: You are a support assistant. Current time: 2026-10-13T09:41:07Z\n<2,900 tokens of fixed policy text>"},"options":["The timestamp sits in the first 1,024 tokens, so the prefix never matches","Prompts under 4,096 tokens are never cached on Azure, whatever their content","The cache only activates after 5 requests per minute from the same user account","`cached_tokens` is only reported when `stream` is false in the request"],"answer":0,"explain":"Azure needs an identical first 1,024 tokens, and one differing character gives zero cached tokens. Move the timestamp to the end of the prompt. The 4,096 figure is an Anthropic minimum for some models, not an Azure rule. There is no per-user rate gate, and streaming does not hide the field.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w02-l4-q3","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A team enables Anthropic caching but sees only cache writes and no reads. What is wrong with the request?","code":{"lang":"json","code":"\"system\": [\n  { \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Instructions v7 ...\" },\n  { \"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Request time: 2026-10-13T09:41:07Z. User: u-8123\",\n    \"cache_control\": { \"type\": \"ephemeral\" } }\n]"},"options":["`cache_control` must sit on the first system block, never on a later block","`ephemeral` is not a valid `cache_control` type, so the request is not cached","The breakpoint sits on content that changes every request, so it only writes","System blocks cannot be cached at all, so only message content can carry a breakpoint"],"answer":2,"explain":"A breakpoint on a block containing a timestamp and a user id writes a new entry every time and never matches. Put the breakpoint on the stable block, and keep the changing text after it. `ephemeral` is the valid type, position of the first block is irrelevant, and system blocks are cacheable.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r3"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r4","r5"],"takeaways":["A provider cache stores KV tensors for a prefix. It changes cost and TTFT, never the answer","From GPT-5.6 and on Anthropic, writes cost 1.25x or more, so a prefix needs at least two uses to win","Static first, dynamic last. One changed character inside the cached region voids the entry","Explicit breakpoints let you choose what is written. Explicit mode with none turns caching off","Longer retention only pays for gaps between 5 and 60 minutes. A steady stream refreshes the short entry for free"]},{"id":"w02-l5","order":5,"title":"Engineer the hit rate","kind":"lab","minutes":20,"brief":"The dashboard says caching is on, yet the bill barely moved. Hit rate depends on prefix stability, request spacing, routing and lifetime, and none of them is fixed. Find what to change first, and how you would notice a silent regression.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Hit rate is a product of four conditions","level":2,"id":"hit-rate-is-a-product-of-four-conditions"},{"t":"p","md":"A request hits only when **all four** hold: the prefix is above the minimum size, the prompt is identical up to the cached boundary, the request reaches the machine that holds the entry, and it arrives before the entry expires. Miss any one and you pay the full price, and on GPT-5.6 and later or Anthropic you also pay the write premium for an entry nobody reads [[r1]] [[r3]]. The lab below models these four conditions with illustrative prices and times, not vendor benchmarks."},{"t":"lab","id":"prompt-cache","intro":"Start with the 'Many tenants, low traffic' preset: about 0.2 requests per minute per tenant leaves gaps that run into a 5-minute lifetime. Then load 'One hot prefix', where 120 requests per minute on one key exceed the roughly 15 per minute documented before misses begin, and spread it across keys. Finally break the prefix on 70% of requests and watch caching cost more than no caching."},{"t":"failure","title":"What silently breaks a prefix cache","items":[{"symptom":"Hit ratio is near zero on every request","cause":"A timestamp, request id or user name sits early in the prompt, so the first differing token comes before the cached boundary","fix":"Put static content first and dynamic content last. Diff the first 1,024 tokens of two consecutive requests"},{"symptom":"Hit ratio fell after a deploy that changed nothing visible","cause":"Tool definitions were reordered or edited, or `tool_choice`, `parallel_tool_calls`, `text.format`, `reasoning.effort` or `text.verbosity` changed. On Anthropic, tool changes invalidate everything and thinking or effort changes invalidate messages","fix":"Version and freeze tool lists. Use `tool_choice: \"none\"` or `allowed_tools` instead of removing tools. Alert on the ratio, not on errors"},{"symptom":"Long conversations stop hitting after a summarization step","cause":"Summarization, compaction and truncation rewrite the prefix, so reuse resets","fix":"Compact rarely and in large steps, then append. Expect one write after each compaction"},{"symptom":"Misses climb at peak although the prefix is stable","cause":"One prefix and key above about 15 requests per minute overflows to other machines that do not hold the entry","fix":"Shard across several stable keys with a stable mapping. A key never pins a machine or guarantees a hit"},{"symptom":"Parallel fan-out requests all write and none read","cause":"On Anthropic an entry exists only after the first response begins","fix":"Send one request, wait for its response to begin, then fan out the rest"},{"symptom":"A hit vanishes after a turn that added many content blocks","cause":"An Anthropic breakpoint looks back only 20 block positions for an earlier write","fix":"Add a second breakpoint when a turn adds 20 or more blocks"},{"symptom":"Cost jumps after a regional failover","cause":"Caches are not shared across regional processing boundaries, so the standby region starts cold","fix":"Prewarm the standby where the API allows it and budget the cold-start write cost"},{"symptom":"Low hit rate behind a model router","cause":"Consecutive requests reach different underlying models, and each model keeps its own cache","fix":"Pin a model subset, use session affinity where offered (preview for Chat Completions, no guarantee), and measure before and after routing"}]},{"t":"diagram","id":"w02-l5-debug","title":"Debug a falling hit ratio","alt":"Flowchart. Start with a dropped hit ratio. Check the prefix is above the minimum size, then whether the first differing token is early, then whether the per-key rate exceeds 15 requests per minute, then whether gaps exceed the lifetime, and finally check tools, effort and compaction.","src":"flowchart TB\n  A[Hit ratio dropped] --> B{Prefix above minimum?}\n  B -- no --> B1[Pad or merge to reach it]\n  B -- yes --> C{First difference early?}\n  C -- yes --> C1[Move dynamic text last]\n  C -- no --> D{Over 15 rpm per key?}\n  D -- yes --> D1[Shard across stable keys]\n  D -- no --> E{Gaps longer than lifetime?}\n  E -- yes --> E1[Longer TTL or prewarm]\n  E -- no --> F[Check tools, effort, compaction]","caption":"Ordered from the cheapest check to the most invasive fix."},{"t":"formula","title":"Key sharding and the padding threshold","expr":"keys >= ceil(requests_per_minute / 15)","vars":[{"k":"requests_per_minute","v":"traffic for one shared prefix"},{"k":"15","v":"the roughly documented per-prefix, per-key rate above which requests can overflow to other machines"}],"example":"1,000 requests per minute on one prefix needs at least **67 keys**. Padding is a related decision: padding a short prefix up to the minimum M tokens pays off when its length exceeds `M x (r + (w - r) / N)`. With M = 1,024, r = 0.1, w = 1.25 and N = 10 total requests, the threshold is 1,024 x 0.215, about **220 tokens**. A 300-token prefix padded to 1,024 and shared by 10 requests costs less than leaving it uncached [[r1]]. The sharding rate and the padding rule come from OpenAI's guide, and Azure documents the same 15 per minute figure [[r2]]."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Per-key rate before misses","value":"about 15 rpm","note":"Azure: per prefix and key. OpenAI: per key across prefixes (advice for models before GPT-5.6)"},{"label":"Write and read multipliers","value":"1.25x and 0.1x","note":"GPT-5.6 and later, Anthropic 5-minute"},{"label":"Anthropic breakpoints","value":"4, lookback 20","note":"blocks searched for an earlier write"},{"label":"Minimum prefix","value":"1,024 tokens","note":"OpenAI and Azure; Anthropic 512 to 4,096 by model"}]},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"What the key does and does not do","md":"On GPT-5.6 and later, OpenAI's guide says `prompt_cache_key` is optional. It gives per-customer cache accounting and blocks cache-hit probing, although the Azure page still advises setting one for shared prefixes [[r2]]. Before GPT-5.6, a stable key per shared prefix improves routing. In both cases a key never pins a machine and never guarantees a hit [[r1]]. OpenAI also provides a Prompt Caching Dashboard and a Prompt Cache Diagnostics tool. Use them, and alert on cached tokens divided by prompt tokens, because a regression raises no error."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-l5-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"One shared prefix receives 1,000 requests per minute. About 15 requests per minute per key is the documented rate before misses. What is the minimum number of keys to shard across?","answer":67,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"keys","hint":"ceil(1000 / 15)","explain":"1,000 / 15 = 66.7, so at least 67 keys. Fewer keys leave some above 15 per minute, where requests can overflow to machines without the entry. The mapping from request to key must be stable, or a request lands on a key whose entry it never wrote.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w02-l5-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Put the life of a cached Anthropic request in order.","items":["The prompt prefix is matched against stored entries from the first token","The longest matching entry is reused, so those tokens skip prefill","The remaining tokens go through prefill as normal","Once the response begins, an entry for the new prefix is written","The entry lifetime is refreshed each time it is read again"],"explain":"Matching starts at the first token, which is why an early difference ruins everything after it. The entry for a new prefix only exists once the first response begins, which is why parallel requests miss. The refresh on each read is why a steady stream keeps a 5-minute entry alive.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r3"},{"id":"w02-l5-q3","type":"multi","tag":"diagnose","q":"A team reports a falling hit ratio. Select every change that can reset or break prefix reuse.","options":["Appending the next user turn to the end of the history","Reordering the `tools` array between two deployments","Summarizing the oldest turns to save tokens","Changing `reasoning.effort` between requests on a model without `configuration_update`"],"answers":[1,2,3],"explain":"Tools, summarization and the effort setting all alter the prefix or the settings tied to it. Appending a new turn at the end is exactly the pattern that caching is designed for, because the earlier prefix stays identical.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r1","r2","r3"],"takeaways":["A hit needs four things at once: size, identical prefix, the right machine, and arrival inside the lifetime","Dynamic text early, reordered tools, changed effort and summarization are the usual silent breakers","Above about 15 requests per minute per key, shard across stable keys. A key never guarantees a hit","On Anthropic wait for the first response to begin before fanning out, and add a breakpoint every 20 blocks","Alert on cached tokens over prompt tokens. A regression raises no error"]},{"id":"w02-l6","order":6,"title":"Beyond the prefix: caches that change the answer","kind":"problem","minutes":20,"brief":"Some caches skip the model altogether. They save the most and carry the most risk: stale answers, near-miss matches and one user's data surfacing for another. How often questions repeat, who shares the cache and how costly a wrong answer is are all open.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Six cache layers, one rule: the key carries the scope","level":2,"id":"six-cache-layers-one-rule-the-key-carries-the-scope"},{"t":"table","caption":"Only the provider and engine prefix caches leave the answer untouched","head":["Layer","What it saves","Changes the answer?","Key must include","Main risk"],"rows":[["**Provider prefix cache**","Prefill compute and input cost","No","Nothing for correctness. Use `prompt_cache_key` for per-customer accounting and to block probing on GPT-5.6+","Silent misses and the write premium"],["**Exact response cache**","The whole call","Replays an earlier answer","Normalized request, identity or permission scope, model, template and tool versions","Stale answers. Useless when inputs vary"],["**Semantic cache**","The whole call for paraphrases","Yes, and it can be wrong","Tenant or user scope, model id, embedding model id, template, tool configuration and safety-policy versions [[r17]]","Wrong, stale, unsafe or foreign answers. Every miss also pays an embedding and a lookup"],["**Embedding cache**","Embedding calls on re-ingest and repeated queries","No, vectors are deterministic","Content hash plus embedding model id and version (author inference from the partition rule above)","Stale vectors after a model change"],["**MCP list and read cache**","Round trips, and a stable tool order that protects the prompt cache","No","`cacheScope: private` results must not cross authorization contexts. `ttlMs` absent means 0 [[r18]]","Serving one user's list to another"],["**Engine prefix cache** (vLLM, SGLang)","Same as the provider cache, on self-hosted models","No","A per-tenant `cache_salt` [[r7]]","Cross-tenant timing leaks"]],"highlightCol":0},{"t":"dials","id":"w02-l6-cache","title":"Pick the cache layer","prompt":"Flip how questions repeat, who shares the cache and how costly a wrong answer is.","dials":[{"id":"repeat","label":"Repetition","values":[{"id":"exact","label":"Same normalized questions repeat"},{"id":"para","label":"Paraphrases of a few questions"},{"id":"unique","label":"A long tail of unique questions"}]},{"id":"tenancy","label":"Tenancy","values":[{"id":"shared","label":"Public data, one audience"},{"id":"multi","label":"Permissioned data, many tenants or users"}]},{"id":"risk","label":"Cost of a wrong answer","values":[{"id":"low","label":"Low, stale is tolerable"},{"id":"high","label":"High, wrong or stale hurts"}]}],"options":[{"id":"prefix","name":"Provider prefix cache","summary":"Reuse prefill state for a stable prefix."},{"id":"exact","name":"Exact response cache","summary":"Replay the answer for an identical request."},{"id":"semfree","name":"Semantic cache, shared","summary":"One cache for every caller."},{"id":"sempart","name":"Semantic cache, partitioned","summary":"Partition by tenant, user and versions."},{"id":"embed","name":"Embedding cache","summary":"Skip repeat embedding calls."}],"scores":{"prefix":{"repeat":{"exact":{"s":1,"why":"Saves prefill for any repeat, but still calls the model."},"para":{"s":1,"why":"Works for any wording once the prefix is stable."},"unique":{"s":2,"why":"Helps whenever a long stable prefix exists, even if questions are unique."}},"tenancy":{"shared":{"s":1,"why":"No answer is shared, so no leak path."},"multi":{"s":1,"why":"Per-organization cache. Use per-customer keys for accounting."}},"risk":{"low":{"s":0,"why":"Safe but saves less than a whole-call cache."},"high":{"s":2,"why":"The output is unchanged, so correctness is untouched."}}},"exact":{"repeat":{"exact":{"s":2,"why":"Skips the whole call for identical normalized requests."},"para":{"s":-1,"why":"A different wording is a different key, so it misses."},"unique":{"s":-2,"why":"No repeats, no hits."}},"tenancy":{"shared":{"s":1,"why":"One key space is fine for public data."},"multi":{"s":-1,"why":"The key must carry identity and permission scope or it leaks."}},"risk":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Stale answers are tolerable until the TTL."},"high":{"s":-1,"why":"Stale until TTL unless you invalidate on source change."}}},"semfree":{"repeat":{"exact":{"s":1,"why":"Hits, but pays the embedding and lookup first."},"para":{"s":2,"why":"Matches paraphrases by embedding distance."},"unique":{"s":-2,"why":"Every miss pays an embedding and a lookup for nothing."}},"tenancy":{"shared":{"s":1,"why":"Acceptable for public content."},"multi":{"s":-2,"why":"One user's answer can surface for another."}},"risk":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"A near-miss match costs little."},"high":{"s":-2,"why":"Microsoft warns results can be incorrect, outdated or unsafe."}}},"sempart":{"repeat":{"exact":{"s":1,"why":"Hits, with the same overhead as any semantic cache."},"para":{"s":2,"why":"Matches paraphrases inside each partition."},"unique":{"s":-2,"why":"Partitioning also shrinks the pool of possible hits."}},"tenancy":{"shared":{"s":0,"why":"Partitioning loses hit rate and buys no safety here."},"multi":{"s":2,"why":"Scope, model, template and safety versions keep answers apart."}},"risk":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Safer than shared at a modest cost in hit rate."},"high":{"s":-1,"why":"Still returns a stored answer, so a wrong match remains possible."}}},"embed":{"repeat":{"exact":{"s":1,"why":"Skips the embedding call for repeated text."},"para":{"s":0,"why":"Different text is a different key."},"unique":{"s":0,"why":"Little to reuse, little to lose."}},"tenancy":{"shared":{"s":1,"why":"Content-hash keys carry no answers."},"multi":{"s":1,"why":"Vectors hold no permissioned answer, but key on the model version."}},"risk":{"low":{"s":1,"why":"Deterministic, so nothing wrong can be replayed."},"high":{"s":1,"why":"No answer is replayed, so the risk stays low."}}}},"takeaway":"The prefix cache is the only layer that never changes the answer. Every layer above it needs a key that carries identity, permissions and versions."},{"t":"code","lang":"xml","title":"APIM semantic cache with a partition and a rate limit","code":"<policies>\n  <inbound>\n    <base />\n    <llm-semantic-cache-lookup\n        score-threshold=\"0.05\"\n        embeddings-backend-id=\"llm-backend\"\n        embeddings-backend-auth=\"system-assigned\"\n        ignore-system-messages=\"true\"\n        max-message-count=\"10\">\n      <vary-by>@(context.Subscription.Id)</vary-by>\n    </llm-semantic-cache-lookup>\n    <rate-limit calls=\"20\" renewal-period=\"90\" />\n  </inbound>\n  <outbound>\n    <llm-semantic-cache-store duration=\"60\" />\n    <base />\n  </outbound>\n</policies>","caption":"Lookup and store must be paired. A lower `score-threshold` is stricter. Microsoft suggests starting near 0.05 and says values above 0.2 may cause mismatches [[r16]]. The `vary-by` expression partitions by caller subscription. For per-user data, partition by user or tenant. RedisVL uses `distance_threshold` in cosine distance from 0 to 2 (tutorial sample 0.15), a different scale [[r17]]."},{"t":"failure","title":"Cache failure modes and how you would see them","items":[{"symptom":"User B receives an answer that names User A's documents","cause":"The cache key is only the question embedding, with no tenant or user scope","fix":"Partition by tenant or user, or do not semantic-cache answers built from permissioned data. Re-check access on every hit"},{"symptom":"Answers cite a policy that was updated last week","cause":"The TTL is longer than the source changes and entries are not tied to a source version","fix":"Add the index or source version to the key, keep TTLs short and invalidate on source change"},{"symptom":"Two questions that differ by one number get the same answer","cause":"The threshold is too loose, and embeddings treat near-identical questions as the same","fix":"Tighten the threshold, gate on numbers and entities, and measure the false-hit rate on a labelled pair set"},{"symptom":"Latency and cost got worse after enabling the cache","cause":"Hit rate is low, and every miss still pays the embedding, the lookup and the normal call","fix":"Measure net savings and switch the cache off below break-even"},{"symptom":"Old answers appear after a model or prompt release","cause":"Model, template, tool and safety-policy versions are not part of the partition","fix":"Put every version that shapes the answer into the key"},{"symptom":"Response timing reveals that another user's prompt was cached","cause":"Gu et al. detected global cache sharing across users in seven providers, including OpenAI [[r12]]","fix":"Scope caches per customer. Use per-customer `prompt_cache_key` on GPT-5.6+ and `cache_salt` on self-hosted engines"}]},{"t":"h","text":"Not a cache, but cheaper tokens: batch and flex","level":2,"id":"not-a-cache-but-cheaper-tokens-batch-and-flex"},{"t":"table","caption":"Asynchronous and delay-tolerant modes, as of 2026-10-13. Cost and capacity decisions continue in the cost world","head":["Mode","Discount","Latency contract","Limits and notes"],"rows":[["**OpenAI Batch API**","50%","Completes within 24 hours","`.jsonl` file with a unique `custom_id` per request, one model per file, 200 MB upload limit, separate higher limits [[r22]]"],["**Azure Global and Data Zone Batch**","50% less than Global Standard","24-hour target, no real-time SLA","Separate enqueued-token quota"],["**Anthropic Message Batches**","50%","Most finish within 1 hour, expire after 24 hours","Up to 100,000 requests or 256 MB. Results kept 29 days [[r30]]"],["**Flex processing** (Azure: preview)","50%, and cached-token discounts also apply","No latency or service SLA","Per-request `service_tier: \"flex\"`. Capacity is preemptible, so 429 can mean no capacity and 408 means raise the timeout. Unsupported models return 400, a rule Microsoft's priority-processing page dates to 2026-09-25 [[r23]]"]],"highlightCol":0}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-l6-q1","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"In a multi-tenant RAG assistant, User B received an answer that quoted User A's contract. The assistant uses a semantic cache in front of the model. Which cause fits best?","options":["The embedding model is too small, so a larger one would separate the two questions clearly","The partition has no tenant or user scope, so an answer from A's documents matched B's question","The score threshold is too loose in general, so only a much stricter value would have prevented it","The provider prefix cache leaked KV tensors between the two organizations that share the endpoint"],"answer":1,"explain":"A semantic cache returns a stored answer, and without a scope in the key any similar question hits it, whatever data built the answer. A bigger embedding model does not add scope. A stricter threshold reduces matches but does not remove the leak path. Provider prefix caches are per organization and never store answers.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16"},{"id":"w02-l6-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Select everything that belongs in the partition key of a semantic cache for a multi-tenant assistant that uses tools.","options":["Tenant or user scope","Embedding model id","The request's trace id","Prompt template and safety-policy versions","The response length"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Scope keeps answers apart, the embedding model id keeps vectors comparable, and template and policy versions stop old behavior from being replayed after a release. A trace id is unique per request, so nothing would ever hit. Response length is an output, not an input that defines which answers are interchangeable.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r17"},{"id":"w02-l6-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"A public product FAQ bot gets many paraphrases of about 200 questions. The content is public and a day-old answer is acceptable. Which choice fits these dials best?","options":["Only an exact response cache, because semantic caches are never safe to run for any kind of workload","Only the provider prefix cache, because it never changes the answer and so carries no risk at all","No cache at all, because any staleness is unacceptable for a bot that answers customers","Semantic cache with a strict threshold, short TTL and versioned partitions, plus prefix caching"],"answer":3,"explain":"Paraphrase-heavy public traffic with a tolerance for staleness is the semantic cache's best case. Keep the threshold strict, partition by versions and keep the TTL short. An exact cache misses paraphrases. The prefix cache alone leaves most of the cost. The stem says staleness is acceptable, so refusing all caching is the wrong trade.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16"},{"id":"w02-l6-q4","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A semantic cache adds an embedding and lookup that cost 3% of one model call, on every request. The hit rate is 20%. A hit costs only that overhead. A miss pays the overhead and the full call. What does an average request cost, as a percentage of a call with no cache?","answer":83,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"percent","hint":"overhead + (1 - hit rate) x 1.0","explain":"Every request pays 0.03. Misses, 80% of requests, also pay 1.0: 0.03 + 0.8 = 0.83, so 83% of baseline, a 17% saving. Break-even is where 0.03 + (1 - h) = 1, so h = 3%. Below that hit rate the cache costs more than it saves, before counting the risk of wrong answers.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r17"}],"defend":null,"refs":["r7","r12","r16","r17","r18","r22","r23","r26","r30"],"takeaways":["Only prefix caches leave the answer untouched. Response and semantic caches replace the model's answer","Every answer cache needs a key with scope, model, template, tool and safety versions","Semantic caches pay embedding and lookup on every miss, so compute the break-even hit rate","APIM's `score-threshold` is stricter when lower, and RedisVL uses a different scale","Timing can leak that another user's prompt was cached, so scope caches per customer"]},{"id":"w02-l7","order":7,"title":"Boss: make it feel instant","kind":"boss","minutes":25,"brief":"An interviewer says: the assistant feels slow and costs too much, so make it feel instant without breaking correctness or isolation between users. Nothing else is given. Build a budget, rank the levers, then defend your plan.","blocks":[{"t":"h","text":"Warm-up: build the latency budget","level":2,"id":"warm-up-build-the-latency-budget"},{"t":"lab","id":"latency-budget","intro":"Load the Interactive chat preset and find where first-token time goes. Turn on the parallel guard, then streaming, then a prompt cache hit (it cuts model TTFT by an assumed 50%), then the smaller-model toggle. Finally set every p95 to 3 times its p50 and compare the typical path, the simulated p95 and the sum of p95s. All defaults are illustrative, not product measurements."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w02-l7-path","title":"Request path with the input guard beside retrieval","alt":"Flowchart. After authentication the input guard and the retrieval branch of rewrite, embed, retrieve and rerank run in parallel. Both feed prompt assembly, then model time to first token, then streamed tokens, then the output guard.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Auth] --> B[Input guard]\n  A --> R[Rewrite]\n  R --> E[Embed]\n  E --> V[Retrieve]\n  V --> K[Rerank]\n  B --> M[Assemble prompt]\n  K --> M\n  M --> T[Model TTFT]\n  T --> S[Stream tokens]\n  S --> O[Output guard]","caption":"Time to first token is auth plus the longer of the guard and the retrieval branch, then assembly and model TTFT. If the guard is the shorter branch, running it in parallel is free."},{"t":"dials","id":"w02-l7-lever","title":"Which latency lever for which bottleneck","prompt":"Flip where the time goes, who is waiting and how strict the quality bar is.","dials":[{"id":"neck","label":"Bottleneck","values":[{"id":"out","label":"Long answers dominate"},{"id":"pre","label":"Long prompts dominate TTFT"},{"id":"ser","label":"Serial stages before the first token"},{"id":"tail","label":"p95 spikes from queueing"}]},{"id":"who","label":"Audience","values":[{"id":"live","label":"A user is watching"},{"id":"async","label":"Nobody is waiting"}]},{"id":"bar","label":"Quality bar","values":[{"id":"strict","label":"Strict, regressions are costly"},{"id":"relaxed","label":"Relaxed, small losses are fine"}]}],"options":[{"id":"outcap","name":"Shorten the output","summary":"Cap tokens, add stop sequences, ask for concise formats."},{"id":"cache","name":"Cache the prefix","summary":"Static first, provider prompt caching."},{"id":"small","name":"Smaller or routed model","summary":"Trade capability for speed and cost."},{"id":"par","name":"Parallelize stages","summary":"Run the guard beside retrieval, drop needless steps."},{"id":"capacity","name":"Priority or provisioned capacity","summary":"Buy steadier timing."}],"scores":{"outcap":{"neck":{"out":{"s":2,"why":"Output tokens drive total time, and 50% fewer can cut about 50% of latency."},"pre":{"s":0,"why":"Output length does not change prefill."},"ser":{"s":0,"why":"It does not touch stages before the model."},"tail":{"s":1,"why":"Shorter outputs free batch slots sooner."}},"who":{"live":{"s":1,"why":"Short answers feel quick."},"async":{"s":0,"why":"Nobody is watching the clock."}},"bar":{"strict":{"s":-1,"why":"Truncation can drop required content."},"relaxed":{"s":1,"why":"Terse answers are acceptable."}}},"cache":{"neck":{"out":{"s":0,"why":"It skips prefill only."},"pre":{"s":2,"why":"It skips prefill of the stable prefix, which sets TTFT."},"ser":{"s":0,"why":"Rewrite, retrieval and guards are untouched."},"tail":{"s":0,"why":"It does not reduce queueing."}},"who":{"live":{"s":1,"why":"A lower TTFT is felt directly."},"async":{"s":1,"why":"Still cuts cost for background work."}},"bar":{"strict":{"s":2,"why":"The output is unchanged."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"The gain is the same whatever the bar."}}},"small":{"neck":{"out":{"s":1,"why":"Faster decode shortens long answers."},"pre":{"s":1,"why":"Smaller models also prefill faster."},"ser":{"s":0,"why":"It does not remove serial stages."},"tail":{"s":0,"why":"Smaller models can still queue."}},"who":{"live":{"s":0,"why":"Faster, but it must still pass the quality bar."},"async":{"s":1,"why":"Cheaper bulk work."}},"bar":{"strict":{"s":-2,"why":"It can miss the bar, so it needs an eval gate."},"relaxed":{"s":2,"why":"You can trade quality for speed and cost."}}},"par":{"neck":{"out":{"s":0,"why":"Generation is serial."},"pre":{"s":0,"why":"Prefill is one call."},"ser":{"s":2,"why":"The path costs the longer of the guard and retrieval, not the sum."},"tail":{"s":-1,"why":"Branches run together, so the slowest one sets the pace and widens the tail."}},"who":{"live":{"s":1,"why":"It cuts time before the first token."},"async":{"s":-1,"why":"Extra complexity for no user benefit."}},"bar":{"strict":{"s":1,"why":"Same models, same output."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"The gain does not depend on the bar."}}},"capacity":{"neck":{"out":{"s":0,"why":"Capacity does not shorten answers."},"pre":{"s":0,"why":"It does not shorten prefill."},"ser":{"s":0,"why":"It does not touch serial stages."},"tail":{"s":2,"why":"Priority or provisioned capacity cuts queueing variance."}},"who":{"live":{"s":1,"why":"A steadier first token."},"async":{"s":-2,"why":"A premium for latency nobody needs. Use batch."}},"bar":{"strict":{"s":1,"why":"Same models, steadier timing."},"relaxed":{"s":0,"why":"Neutral."}}}},"takeaway":"The lever follows the bottleneck. Caching wins on prefill, output caps on long answers, parallelism on serial stages, capacity on queueing, and a smaller model only where the quality bar allows."},{"t":"callout","kind":"interview","title":"How this round is scored","md":"Use the five steps of Dial-and-Defend: clarify the dials, list options including the boring one, pick and say what flips it, name failure modes and how you would detect them, then name metrics and the rollout. Say the isolation rule out loud before you propose any cache that stores answers."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-l7-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Stage times in milliseconds: auth 20, rewrite 400, embed 40, retrieve 80, rerank 120, input guard 300, prompt assembly 10, model TTFT 600. Ignore network. The input guard starts at the same time as the rewrite stage and the retrieval branch (rewrite, embed, retrieve, rerank) runs serially beside it. What is the time to first token?","answer":1270,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"ms","hint":"auth + max(guard, retrieval branch) + assembly + model TTFT","explain":"The retrieval branch takes 400 + 40 + 80 + 120 = 640 ms, longer than the 300 ms guard, so the guard is hidden: 20 + 640 + 10 + 600 = 1,270 ms. Run serially it would be 1,570 ms. If the guard were the longer branch, it would set the pace instead.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r14"},{"id":"w02-l7-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"On weekdays at 09:00, p95 TTFT rises and stays high. The cached-token ratio is flat, TPOT is flat, and prompt and output lengths are unchanged. Which cause fits best?","options":["Prefill got slower because the prompt cache lifetime expired overnight and reset at 09:00","Queueing on a deployment shared with another workload adds wait before prefill","Decode got slower because the KV cache is full and sequences are being preempted","The client library started buffering the stream, which delays the first visible token"],"answer":1,"explain":"With prompt length, cache ratio and TPOT all flat, the extra time is before prefill starts: queueing, usually from a workload sharing the deployment. Azure advises separate deployments per workload. A cache expiry would show in the cached-token ratio. A full KV cache would slow TPOT. Buffering affects what the client sees, not the server-side TTFT metric.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r14"},{"id":"w02-l7-q3","type":"single","tag":"flip","q":"Suppose revocation of a document permission must take effect immediately, and answers are built from permissioned documents. Which previously acceptable choice becomes unacceptable?","options":["Provider prefix caching of the system prompt and tool definitions","Streaming the answer to the user token by token as the model generates it","A semantic cache keyed only on the question text, with a one-hour TTL","Running the input guard in parallel with the retrieval branch to hide its latency"],"answer":2,"explain":"A cached answer outlives the permission it was built under, and a key on question text alone ignores who asks. Prefix caching of static instructions stores no answers. Streaming and parallel guards do not change what a user may see.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16"}],"defend":{"prompt":"Our RAG assistant feels slow and costs too much. Make it feel instant without breaking correctness or isolation between users. Walk me through your approach.","timebox":"5 min","clarify":["Which number feels slow: time to first word, stream speed or total time, and at which percentile?","Where does the time go today: queue, rewrite, retrieval, guards, prefill or decode? Do we have per-stage traces bucketed by output tokens?","What may never be shared between users, and how quickly must a permission revocation take effect?","How stable is the prompt prefix, how many requests per minute hit it, and what is the gap between requests?","Is there an eval set, so a smaller model or a shorter output can be tested instead of guessed?"],"options":[{"name":"Boring first: stream, cap output, static-first prompt","when":"Always. It costs almost nothing and removes the common causes.","tradeoff":"Streaming changes perception, not total time. Output caps can truncate required content."},{"name":"Provider prompt caching with per-tenant keys","when":"A long stable prefix, enough requests inside the lifetime, and prefill-dominated TTFT.","tradeoff":"Write premium of 1.25x on GPT-5.6+ and Anthropic, silent misses from prefix drift, and a cold cache after failover."},{"name":"Parallelize and trim the pipeline","when":"Serial rewrite, retrieval and guard stages before the first token.","tradeoff":"More concurrent work and a wider tail. A blocked request wastes the retrieval it started."},{"name":"Semantic or response cache","when":"Public content, paraphrase-heavy traffic and tolerance for staleness.","tradeoff":"Can return wrong, stale or foreign answers. Unsafe for permissioned data without a scoped key."},{"name":"Smaller or routed model, or a capacity tier","when":"Output-dominated latency with a relaxed quality bar, or p95 spikes from queueing.","tradeoff":"Quality risk without an eval gate, a split prompt cache across models, and a premium for priority or provisioned capacity."}],"modelAnswer":[{"t":"p","md":"**Clarify.** I would first ask which number is slow, at which percentile, and where the time goes. I want per-stage traces: queue, rewrite, retrieval, rerank, guards, prefill and decode, bucketed by output tokens. I would also ask what must never be shared between users, how stable the prompt prefix is, and whether an eval set exists."},{"t":"p","md":"**Options.** The boring set is streaming, an output cap and a static-first prompt. Then provider prompt caching, parallel stages, a smaller or routed model, a capacity tier, and last the answer caches that skip the model. I would rank them by how little they risk correctness: caches that only reuse prefill state never change the answer, so they come before anything that stores answers."},{"t":"p","md":"**Pick, and what flips it.** For an interactive RAG chat over permissioned data with a long stable prefix I would stream, restructure the prompt so static content comes first, turn on prompt caching with a per-tenant key, run the input guard beside retrieval, skip query rewrite where the eval set shows no loss, and cap output length. Only then would I test a smaller or routed model against the eval set. I would add a semantic cache only for public, paraphrase-heavy content, partitioned by scope and versions."},{"t":"table","head":["If this dial changes","The choice changes to"],"rows":[["Answers come from permissioned data and revocation is immediate","No answer caches. Prefix caching only, with per-tenant keys"],["Prefix is below the minimum, or gaps exceed the lifetime","Caching will not hit. Prewarm, extend the lifetime, or skip it"],["Output tokens dominate","Output cap or a smaller model. Caching helps little"],["Prefill dominates","Prompt caching and a shorter retrieved context"],["p95 spikes come from queueing","Separate deployments, then priority or provisioned capacity"],["The quality bar is strict","No smaller model without an eval gate"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Failure modes and detection.** A prompt-cache regression raises no error, so I alert on cached tokens divided by prompt tokens. A semantic cache can return near-miss answers, so I measure the false-hit rate on a labelled pair set and tag partitions with versions. A parallel guard that finishes after tokens have streamed can leave unsafe text on screen, so I hold the first chunks or design a retraction. Per-stage tracing shows whether the parallel path really hid the shorter branch."},{"t":"p","md":"**Metrics and rollout.** I track TTFT p95, TPOT p95, TTLT p95 bucketed by output tokens, cache hit ratio, cost per successful task, quality on the eval set and, for semantic caching, the false-hit rate. I roll changes out behind flags, compare on the same traffic, and set rollback thresholds before the first percent of users sees a change."}],"rubric":[{"pt":"Asks which latency number is slow and requests per-stage traces before choosing a lever","w":3},{"pt":"Explains that TTFT is prefill and queueing while total time is dominated by output tokens","w":2},{"pt":"Offers at least three options including the boring one (stream, cap output, static-first prompt)","w":2},{"pt":"Ranks prompt caching ahead of answer caches because it never changes the answer","w":2},{"pt":"Protects isolation: no shared answer caches over permissioned data, scoped keys, per-tenant cache keys","w":3},{"pt":"Parallelizes independent stages and notes the guard and streaming retraction risk","w":2},{"pt":"States a flip condition for each lever tied to a dial","w":2},{"pt":"Names detection and metrics: cache ratio alert, p95 by output bucket, eval gate, false-hit rate, rollout and rollback","w":2}],"followUps":[{"q":"One prefix receives 400 requests per minute and the hit ratio is poor. What do you do?","a":"OpenAI and Azure document about 15 requests per minute per prefix and key before overflow to other machines, so shard across at least 27 stable keys (400 / 15 is 26.7), keep the mapping stable and watch the ratio. A key never guarantees a hit."},{"q":"Security worries that timing could reveal whether a competitor asked a question. What changes?","a":"Gu et al. found global cache sharing across users at several providers. Use a per-customer `prompt_cache_key` on GPT-5.6 and later, keep tenant text out of shared prefixes, avoid cross-tenant answer caches, and set `cache_salt` on self-hosted engines."},{"q":"Finance wants a smaller model everywhere. How do you respond?","a":"Test it on the eval set by task type. Cutting output tokens often beats a model swap for latency. A router splits the prompt cache across models, so measure the combined effect on cost and hit rate before rolling it out."}],"redFlags":["Proposes a shared semantic cache across tenants without a partition key","Says streaming makes the model faster","Puts a timestamp or the user question at the top of the prompt","Names a tool or capacity tier before asking where the time goes"]},"refs":["r14","r15","r1","r12","r16","r2"],"takeaways":["Build the budget by stage, then attack the stage that dominates","Run independent stages in parallel and watch for the guard and streaming interaction","Rank levers by risk to correctness: prefix caching before answer caching","State the isolation rule before proposing any cache that stores answers","Every lever has a dial where it loses, so say what would flip your pick"]}],"topics":[{"id":"w02-t01","order":1,"group":"Latency fundamentals","title":"TTFT, TPOT, throughput and queueing","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Four numbers describe an LLM call: time to first token, time per output token, total latency and throughput. Each has a different driver, so name the one the product is judged on before you fix anything.","useIf":"Someone says the assistant feels slow and you must decide what to measure and which stage to attack.","avoidIf":"You already have per-stage traces with token counts and a metric the product is judged on. Go straight to the lever.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A request passes through four stages: **queue** (waiting for capacity), **prefill** (the model reads the whole prompt), **decode** (it emits one token at a time) and the **network** back to the client. Three numbers fall out of that. **TTFT** (time to first token) covers queueing, prefill and network time, and grows with prompt length. **TPOT**, also called **ITL** (inter-token latency), is the average gap between output tokens. **TTLT** (total latency) is TTFT plus the generation time. Azure writes it as `TTLT = TTFT + (TBT x tokens generated)` [[r13]] [[r14]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"Latency from parts","expr":"e2e = TTFT + TPOT x (output_tokens - 1)","vars":[{"k":"TTFT","v":"queue + prefill + network, in seconds"},{"k":"TPOT","v":"(e2e - TTFT) / (output_tokens - 1), the same quantity as ITL"},{"k":"output_tokens","v":"includes hidden reasoning tokens on reasoning models"}],"example":"A 4.2 s call with 0.6 s TTFT and 181 output tokens has TPOT = (4.2 - 0.6) / 180 = 20 ms, about 50 tokens per second per user."},{"t":"table","caption":"Which number answers which question","head":["Metric","Question it answers","Main driver","Typical SLO form"],"rows":[["TTFT","How long until the first word?","Prompt length, queueing, cache hits","p95 under 1.5 s"],["TPOT or ITL","How smooth is the stream?","Model size, batch load, KV size","p95 under 50 ms"],["TTLT","How long for the whole answer?","Output tokens first, then TPOT","p95 per output-length bucket"],["System TPS","How much work per GPU or deployment?","Batching, saturation","Cost per million tokens"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Throughput** has two meanings. System tokens per second is total output tokens over the benchmark window. Per-user tokens per second is output length over e2e latency and tends to `1/ITL`. As concurrency rises, system TPS climbs to a saturation point while per-user TPS falls [[r13]]. A fleet run hot is cheap per token and slow per user."},{"t":"p","md":"**Queueing** is where load becomes latency. Textbook M/M/1 arithmetic (general queueing theory, not from the vendor docs) puts the mean wait at about `rho / (1 - rho)` service times at utilization rho: 1 at 50%, 9 at 90%, 19 at 95%. Batching changes the constants but not the shape, so p99 breaks first as utilization nears 100%."},{"t":"p","md":"**Percentiles** matter because users meet the tail. If each of 100 parallel calls is slow 1% of the time, 63% of user requests hit at least one slow call [[tr4]]. Track p50 and p95, add p99 for fan-out paths, and always pair latency with output token counts, because a longer answer is not a regression [[r14]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Tools define ITL differently","md":"GenAI-Perf leaves TTFT out of its ITL average and LLMPerf includes it, so numbers from the two do not compare [[r13]]. Pin the definition in your SLO."}],"whereToUse":["Setting an SLO for a chat or copilot UI: TTFT p95 plus TPOT p95","Reading a vendor or benchmark claim: check which ITL definition and which prompt and output lengths it used","Triage after a release that feels slower: compare TTFT, TPOT and output token counts before and after","Capacity planning: find the concurrency where per-user TPS still meets the target","Batch pipelines, where system TPS and cost per token matter more than TTFT"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The product is interactive and users watch for the first word (lead with TTFT)","Answers are long, so TPOT and output length dominate total time","Load is spiky and you need to see where queueing starts (percentiles under load)"],"avoid":["Do not average latency across short and long outputs. Bucket by output tokens","Do not hold a job nobody watches to a TTFT target. Use throughput, cost and success rate","Do not compare numbers from two tools without checking how each defines ITL"],"flips":["Output tokens double (reasoning model, longer format): TTLT moves and TTFT does not, so TTLT becomes the headline metric","Utilization passes roughly 80%: p95 TTFT grows faster than the mean, so alert on queue depth, not averages","The consumer is a program, not a person: drop TTFT and target TTLT and success rate"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["TTFT p95","Interactive, streaming UI","Output-dominated jobs or non-streaming callers","Needs prompt length alongside it"],["TPOT or ITL","Judging stream smoothness and long answers","Short answers where TTFT dominates","Definitions differ between tools"],["TTLT p95 by output bucket","Non-streaming or programmatic callers","You care about the first word","Needs token counts on every request"],["System TPS and cost per token","Batch and offline work","Interactive users, where it hides slow streams","Rewards running hot"],["Average latency","A first dashboard","Any skewed distribution","Hides the tail users feel"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Latency 'regressed' after a release","cause":"Answers got longer or reasoning tokens grew. TTFT did not change","fix":"Chart TTLT against output tokens before you touch infrastructure [[r14]]"},{"symptom":"Two benchmark tools disagree on ITL for the same run","cause":"One includes TTFT in the average and the other excludes it","fix":"Pin one definition in the SLO and use one tool for comparisons [[r13]]"},{"symptom":"p50 looks fine and users still complain","cause":"Queueing under load or a fan-out tail","fix":"Alert on p95 and p99, queue depth and utilization, not on the mean"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t01-s1","title":"The chat that feels slow","situation":"Users of an interactive assistant say it feels slow. Dashboards show one number: average end-to-end latency, flat for a month. Answers stream to the screen.","constraints":["Interactive, streamed UI","Only average e2e latency is collected today","No capacity change is planned"],"unknowns":["Did typical output length change?","Is the complaint about the first word or the pace of the text?","What is p95 TTFT at peak?"],"question":"What do you measure first?","options":[{"name":"TTFT p95 and ITL p95, charted with output token counts","verdict":"best","why":"It separates the wait for the first word from the pace of the stream and rules out longer answers as the cause.","tradeoff":"You must add per-request token counts and percentile collection."},{"name":"Average e2e latency by hour","verdict":"poor","why":"It is the number that already hides the problem: it mixes queueing, prefill and answer length.","tradeoff":"Fine for a first glance at a batch system where only the total matters."},{"name":"System tokens per second per GPU","verdict":"good","why":"It shows whether the fleet is saturated, which explains a TTFT rise.","tradeoff":"It is a capacity number. It cannot tell you what an individual user waits for."}],"flips":[{"if":"The product is not streamed and a program consumes the answer","then":"TTLT p95 by output bucket becomes the primary metric."},{"if":"Users say text appears fast but the answer arrives late","then":"Output length is the cause, so chart output tokens first."}],"takeaway":"Pick the metric that matches what the user perceives, then break the total into parts."},{"id":"w02-t01-s2","title":"p99 explodes at peak","situation":"Load tests show a flat p50 and p95 up to 70% of capacity. Past 90% the p99 grows tenfold. Marketing plans a campaign that will push traffic to 95% of current capacity.","constraints":["Fixed capacity today","A campaign with a known date","Timeouts are currently 30 s"],"unknowns":["Can capacity be added before the date?","Can low-priority traffic be shed or delayed?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Add capacity or shed low-priority traffic so peak utilization stays below the knee","verdict":"best","why":"Queueing delay rises steeply near saturation. Moving off the knee is the only fix that removes the cause.","tradeoff":"It costs money or drops some work."},{"name":"Raise client timeouts","verdict":"poor","why":"It hides failures while users wait longer in the same queue.","tradeoff":"Right only for background jobs that tolerate delay."},{"name":"Cap max output tokens","verdict":"good","why":"Shorter answers free capacity sooner and lower per-request time.","tradeoff":"It changes the product, and may truncate answers."}],"flips":[{"if":"The campaign is cancelled and traffic stays near 60%","then":"No change is needed. Keep an alert on queue depth."},{"if":"Traffic is bursty, not sustained","then":"A queue with admission control may be cheaper than permanent capacity."}],"takeaway":"Latency is a function of utilization. Plan below the knee."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t01-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A streamed call finishes in 4.2 s. TTFT was 0.6 s and the model produced 181 output tokens. What is the TPOT in milliseconds?","answer":20,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"ms","hint":"(e2e - TTFT) / (tokens - 1)","explain":"(4.2 - 0.6) / (181 - 1) = 3.6 / 180 = 0.02 s, which is 20 ms. Dividing by 181 instead of 180 gives 19.9 ms, which the tolerance still accepts.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r13"},{"id":"w02-t01-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Two tools report ITL for the same benchmark run and the numbers differ by 15%. What is the most likely cause?","options":["One tool uses a different tokenizer for the system prompt","ITL is random and varies by run","One tool includes TTFT in the ITL average and the other excludes it","One tool measures network latency only"],"answer":2,"explain":"GenAI-Perf excludes TTFT from the ITL average and LLMPerf includes it. A tokenizer difference would change counts, not definitions, ITL is deterministic given a trace, and network-only latency is a different metric.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r13"},{"id":"w02-t01-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the stages of one streamed request from first to last.","items":["Queue for capacity","Prefill the prompt","Emit the first token","Decode the remaining tokens"],"explain":"The request waits, then the whole prompt is processed, which yields the first token, and only then does the decode loop emit tokens one at a time.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r13"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does TTFT include and what grows it?","back":"Queueing, prefill and network time. It grows with prompt length and with load."},{"front":"Write ITL in terms of e2e latency and TTFT.","back":"ITL = (e2e latency - TTFT) / (total output tokens - 1). It is the same as TPOT."},{"front":"Why can raising concurrency hurt users while lowering cost?","back":"System TPS rises to a saturation point while per-user TPS falls, and queueing grows near full utilization."}],"related":["w02-t02","w02-t03","w02-t17","w02-l1"],"refs":["r13","r14","r15","tr4"]},{"id":"w02-t02","order":2,"group":"Latency fundamentals","title":"Streaming and perceived latency","minutes":7,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Streaming sends tokens to the client as they are generated. Total time does not change, but the first word appears sooner and long calls stop timing out.","useIf":"A person reads the answer as it arrives and the answer is longer than a sentence or two.","avoidIf":"A program consumes the whole answer, or a check must see the full text before anyone may display it.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"With `stream: true` the server returns the answer as a series of small events instead of one body. Streaming does not change total time. It lowers the time to the first visible response and avoids client timeouts on long outputs [[r14]]. Perceived latency tracks TTFT, not TTLT, so a 12 second answer that starts in 700 ms feels fast."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w02-t02-d1","title":"One streamed call","alt":"The client posts a request, the server sends a series of data events, then a completed event.","src":"sequenceDiagram\n  participant C as Client\n  participant S as Server\n  C->>S: POST with stream true\n  S-->>C: data: delta 1\n  S-->>C: data: delta 2\n  S-->>C: data: delta n\n  S-->>C: completed event"},{"t":"p","md":"Most APIs use **server-sent events** (SSE). The response type is `text/event-stream`, each event is one or more `data:` lines ended by a blank line, `id:` and `retry:` support reconnection through the `Last-Event-ID` header, and a line starting with `:` is a comment. Servers can send a comment about every 15 seconds so that legacy proxies do not drop an idle connection [[tr3]]. On the Responses API the events to handle are `response.created`, `response.output_text.delta`, `response.completed` and `error`; Chat Completions puts text in a `delta` field [[tr5]]."},{"t":"h","text":"UI patterns that make it feel fast","level":3,"id":"ui-patterns-that-make-it-feel-fast"},{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Show a status line while retrieval or tools run (searching, reading, writing), then switch to text","Render incrementally, batched to animation frames, and keep the scroll position stable","Offer a stop button that cancels the request, so the server stops spending tokens","Show sources or tool results as they arrive, ahead of the final prose"]},{"t":"p","md":"**When streaming hurts.** Streaming makes moderation harder, because partial completions are hard to evaluate and moderation scores arrive only after the full output [[tr5]]. A structured output cannot be validated until it is complete. Buffering proxies hold events until the end, and a gateway needs long-lived unbuffered connections [[tr13]]. Token limits cannot count streamed tokens exactly, so APIM `llm-token-limit` always estimates when streaming [[tr14]]. Once text is on screen you cannot silently retry or retract it."}],"whereToUse":["Chat and copilot UIs where users read as text appears","Long outputs (reports, code, explanations) that would otherwise risk client or gateway timeouts","Agent UIs that show progress: tool calls, retrieved sources, partial plans","Voice or text-to-speech pipelines that start speaking on the first sentence","Server-to-server calls where an idle-timeout would otherwise kill a long non-streamed request"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A human reads the output and TTFT is the felt delay","Answers are long enough that TTLT exceeds a few seconds","You can handle events in the client and the whole path allows unbuffered responses"],"avoid":["A downstream program needs complete, validated JSON before any step may start","Policy requires a full-text safety check before display, and a chunk-level check is not acceptable","A proxy or CDN buffers the response and you cannot change it"],"flips":["Output guard needs the whole answer: stream a status line, then release the text after the check, or guard in sentence-sized chunks","A job only needs to avoid an idle timeout: stream to a server-side buffer and never show partial text","Network is unreliable: add event ids and a resume path, or use background runs and polling"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Stream raw tokens","Low-risk text for people","Strict pre-display moderation","Cannot retract shown text"],["Stream in guarded chunks","Moderation and responsiveness both matter","You need exact token-level display","First chunk waits for the check"],["Non-streaming","Program consumers, schema validation","Long answers shown to people","Whole-answer wait, timeout risk"],["Background run and polling","Very long jobs, flaky clients","Interactive feel","More moving parts"],["Status updates only","Agent steps before any text exists","The answer itself is slow","Does not shorten TTFT"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Nothing appears until the answer completes","cause":"A proxy, CDN or gateway buffers the response","fix":"Disable response buffering on the path, flush each event, and test through the real route [[tr13]]"},{"symptom":"Streamed text could not be filtered before users saw it","cause":"Moderation ran on the full output after display","fix":"Check in chunks before release, or hold text for high-risk flows [[tr5]]"},{"symptom":"Token metrics or limits are off for streamed calls","cause":"Gateways estimate streamed tokens, and some OpenAI models need `include_usage: true` for accurate counts","fix":"Request usage in the stream where supported and reconcile gateway counts with provider usage [[tr19]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t02-s1","title":"Public chat with a safety policy","situation":"A consumer assistant answers questions for the public. Policy says unsafe text must never be displayed. Typical answers run 400 tokens. Users already complain about the wait.","constraints":["High risk of harm if shown","Human reader","Answers take about 8 seconds in full"],"unknowns":["Can a check run per sentence in under 100 ms?","Is a 1 to 2 second hold on the first sentence acceptable?"],"question":"How do you deliver the answer?","options":[{"name":"Stream through a chunk-level guard that releases each checked sentence","verdict":"best","why":"Users see the first sentence soon and nothing unchecked is shown.","tradeoff":"TTFT rises by one chunk plus guard time, and a late unsafe sentence must end the stream."},{"name":"Stream raw tokens and moderate afterwards","verdict":"poor","why":"Unsafe text is already visible when the check finishes.","tradeoff":"Acceptable for an internal tool with low harm potential."},{"name":"Do not stream and check the full answer","verdict":"good","why":"It is the safest, with one simple check.","tradeoff":"Users wait the full 8 seconds and may leave."}],"flips":[{"if":"The audience is internal staff and the harm potential is low","then":"Stream raw and log for review."},{"if":"Guard latency per chunk exceeds the gap between chunks","then":"Hold the full answer, show a status line and release after the check."}],"takeaway":"Streaming and moderation can coexist when the check works on chunks."},{"id":"w02-t02-s2","title":"Extraction job behind a gateway","situation":"A service extracts a long JSON object from a document and passes it to an order system. No human watches. Calls sometimes take 90 seconds and the gateway closes idle connections at 60 seconds.","constraints":["Program consumer","Needs complete valid JSON","Gateway idle timeout is fixed"],"unknowns":["Can the gateway timeout be raised?","Is the call idempotent if retried?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Stream, accumulate on the server, validate, then pass on","verdict":"best","why":"Bytes flow, so the idle timeout does not fire, and the consumer still sees only complete JSON.","tradeoff":"No latency gain for the end consumer, plus a little event handling code."},{"name":"Stream partial JSON to the order system","verdict":"poor","why":"The downstream step would act on incomplete data.","tradeoff":"Reasonable only for a consumer that parses incrementally and tolerates partial objects."},{"name":"Keep the non-streamed call and retry on timeout","verdict":"good","why":"It is simple and works if calls are idempotent.","tradeoff":"Each retry repeats the full cost and delay."}],"flips":[{"if":"The timeout can be raised above worst-case latency","then":"A plain non-streamed call is the simplest choice."},{"if":"The run takes many minutes","then":"Use background mode and poll for the result."}],"takeaway":"Streaming also fixes timeouts, even when no one reads the partial text."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t02-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does turning on streaming change for a 10 second answer?","options":["Total generation time drops by half","The first words appear sooner, but total generation time is about the same","Output tokens are billed at a lower rate","The model skips the prefill phase"],"answer":1,"explain":"Streaming lowers time to first response and avoids client timeouts, but it does not change total time. Billing and prefill are unaffected.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r14"},{"id":"w02-t02-q2","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which situations make streaming a poor fit?","options":["A program must validate a full JSON object before use","A response buffering proxy sits in the path and cannot be changed","A person reads a long answer in a chat window","Users want a stop button to cancel generation"],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"Full-object validation and unbuffered delivery are the two blockers. A human reading and a stop button are reasons to stream.","difficulty":2,"ref":"tr5"},{"id":"w02-t02-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each SSE element to its role.","pairs":[{"l":"data: line","r":"Carries the event payload"},{"l":"id: field","r":"Sets the Last-Event-ID sent on reconnect"},{"l":"retry: field","r":"Sets the reconnection time in milliseconds"},{"l":"Line starting with a colon","r":"Comment, usable as a keepalive"}],"explain":"These are the four stream elements defined in the HTML living standard. The colon comment is what servers send about every 15 seconds to keep legacy proxies from dropping the connection.","difficulty":2,"ref":"tr3"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does streaming change and not change?","back":"It lowers time to the first visible token and avoids client timeouts. Total generation time stays about the same."},{"front":"Name two reasons streaming can hurt.","back":"Moderation of partial text is harder, and full-object validation or buffering proxies defeat it."},{"front":"What keeps an SSE connection alive through legacy proxies?","back":"A comment line (starting with a colon) sent about every 15 seconds."}],"related":["w02-t01","w02-t17","w01-t13","w02-l1"],"refs":["r14","tr3","tr5","tr13","tr14","tr19"]},{"id":"w02-t03","order":3,"group":"Latency fundamentals","title":"Prefill vs decode","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Prefill reads the whole prompt in parallel and is compute-bound. Decode emits one token per step and is memory-bandwidth-bound. Prompt length drives the first, output length drives the second.","useIf":"You need to decide whether a slow call is a prompt problem or an output problem, and which lever fits.","avoidIf":"You only call a hosted model and the slowness is clearly queueing or rate limiting, not model time.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Generation has two phases with different physics. **Prefill** processes every prompt token in one parallel pass. It saturates GPU compute, takes a long time per iteration, and builds the KV cache. It sets TTFT. **Decode** then emits one token per request per iteration. Each step does little arithmetic but must read the model weights and the KV cache from memory, so it is **memory-bandwidth-bound** and low on compute use. Because each step is light, batching many decodes together is very effective [[r9]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Two phases, two sets of levers","head":["Property","Prefill","Decode"],"rows":[["Work per step","All prompt tokens at once","One token per request"],["Bound by","GPU compute","Memory bandwidth"],["Sets","TTFT (with queueing)","TPOT and most of TTLT"],["Grows with","Prompt length","Output length and KV size"],["Levers","Prefix caching, shorter prompt, chunking","Fewer output tokens, smaller model, speculation, KV quantization"]]},{"t":"formula","title":"Why decode speed has a ceiling","expr":"tokens per second per stream <= memory bandwidth / bytes read per token","vars":[{"k":"bytes read per token","v":"model weights plus the KV cache of that sequence"},{"k":"batching","v":"shares one weight read across many requests, so system TPS scales while per-user speed does not"}],"example":"Derived and illustrative, not a measurement: an 8B model in bf16 is about 16 GB. On a GPU with 2,000 GB/s of memory bandwidth, one stream reads about 16 GB per token, so the ceiling is about 125 tokens per second before KV reads."},{"t":"p","md":"The levers are asymmetric. OpenAI's latency guide gives the rule of thumb that cutting 50% of output tokens may cut about 50% of latency, while cutting 50% of the prompt may give only 1% to 5% [[r15]]. Azure adds that output tokens are the main driver of total latency, so check token counts before declaring a regression [[r14]]. Long prompts still matter for TTFT, and at very long contexts the share grows."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"The phases fight when they share a batch","md":"A long prefill in the same batch as running decodes stalls them, which shows up as ITL spikes for other users. That is the throughput-latency tension that chunked prefill and prefill/decode disaggregation address [[r9]] [[r28]] [[r29]]."}],"whereToUse":["Diagnosing a slow call: a long prompt points to prefill, a long answer points to decode","Choosing between prompt caching (prefill) and output limits (decode)","Sizing a self-hosted fleet, where prefill and decode need different tuning","Reading why a reasoning model is slow: hidden reasoning tokens extend decode","Explaining to a stakeholder why trimming the prompt rarely fixes a slow answer"],"whenToUse":{"use":["TTFT is high and prompts are long or retrieval-heavy (prefill levers)","TTLT is high and answers are long or reasoning is on (decode levers)","You host the model and see stalls when long prompts arrive"],"avoid":["Do not trim input to fix a decode-dominated delay: 50% fewer input tokens may give 1% to 5%","Do not tune batch or chunk settings on a hosted API where you cannot reach them","Do not read a single average as proof of health: TTFT and TPOT can move in opposite directions"],"flips":["Context reaches tens of thousands of tokens: prefill becomes a large share, so caching and trimming pay off","Reasoning effort rises: hidden tokens extend decode, so cap effort or output before touching the prompt","Concurrency grows on your own GPUs: decode batches well, but long prefills start to stall streams"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Lever","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Prefix caching","Long stable prefix, TTFT bound","Short prompts or unique prefixes","Write premium on some providers"],["Cut output tokens","Output-dominated latency","Answers need the length","May reduce completeness"],["Smaller or routed model","Easy tasks dominate","Hard tasks need the large model","Quality risk, eval needed"],["Trim input","Context is padded with irrelevant text","Prompt is already tight","Can drop needed evidence"],["Chunked prefill or disaggregation","Self-hosted, mixed long and short traffic","Hosted API, low load","Operational complexity"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Trimming the prompt by half barely moves total latency","cause":"The call is decode-dominated: long output or hidden reasoning tokens","fix":"Cap output, lower reasoning effort, or use a smaller model first [[r15]]"},{"symptom":"Other users' streams stutter when one long prompt arrives","cause":"A large prefill shares a batch with decodes","fix":"Enable chunked prefill or separate prefill and decode capacity [[r9]]"},{"symptom":"TTFT scales with prompt length although output is short","cause":"Prefill grows with prompt tokens","fix":"Cache the stable prefix and cut retrieved context"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t03-s1","title":"Retrieval-heavy answers on a hosted model","situation":"A support assistant sends about 30,000 tokens of policy text and retrieved passages to a hosted model and gets back about 200 tokens. Users complain about a 6 second wait before text starts.","constraints":["Hosted API","Short output","Most of the 30,000 tokens are the same policy text on every call"],"unknowns":["How much of the prompt is identical across calls?","How often do calls arrive relative to cache lifetime?"],"question":"Which lever first?","options":[{"name":"Cache the stable policy prefix and keep retrieved text after it","verdict":"best","why":"TTFT is dominated by prefill of a long, mostly repeated prompt. Reusing the prefill state attacks exactly that.","tradeoff":"Hits need an identical prefix, enough traffic inside the cache lifetime and, on some providers, a write premium."},{"name":"Cut max output tokens","verdict":"poor","why":"Output is only 200 tokens, so there is almost nothing to cut.","tradeoff":"Right when long answers dominate total time."},{"name":"Switch to a smaller model","verdict":"good","why":"Prefill is cheaper on a smaller model.","tradeoff":"Quality risk on policy questions, and caching often gives more for less risk."}],"flips":[{"if":"Each call has unique 30,000-token documents","then":"Caching cannot help. Retrieve less, rerank harder or use a smaller model."},{"if":"Answers grow to 2,000 tokens","then":"Decode dominates, so output length becomes the first lever."}],"takeaway":"Long prompt and short answer means attack prefill."},{"id":"w02-t03-s2","title":"Reasoning-heavy analyst tool","situation":"An analysis tool sends a 1,500-token prompt and waits 40 seconds for an answer. Usage data shows large hidden reasoning token counts. A teammate proposes shortening the prompt.","constraints":["Small prompt","Reasoning model","Accuracy matters but 40 seconds is too slow"],"unknowns":["Does accuracy hold at lower reasoning effort?","Is there an eval set to test that?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Lower reasoning effort or cap output, checked against an eval set","verdict":"best","why":"Latency is decode time over many hidden tokens, so cutting generated tokens attacks the cause.","tradeoff":"Accuracy may fall on the hardest questions, so measure it."},{"name":"Shorten the prompt","verdict":"poor","why":"A 1,500-token prompt is a small share of a 40 second call.","tradeoff":"Helps when prompts are tens of thousands of tokens."},{"name":"Add prompt caching","verdict":"good","why":"It trims TTFT a little and lowers cost.","tradeoff":"It does not touch the long decode phase."}],"flips":[{"if":"Easy and hard questions can be told apart cheaply","then":"Route easy ones to low effort and keep high effort for hard ones."},{"if":"The result is not needed interactively","then":"Run it as a background or batch job."}],"takeaway":"Hidden reasoning tokens make a call decode-bound."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t03-q1","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"Why is batching decode requests so effective?","options":["Decode is compute-bound and batching adds more compute","Batching shortens the prompt of each request","Batching removes the KV cache","Each decode step is memory-bandwidth-bound, so one weight read can serve many requests"],"answer":3,"explain":"Decode does little arithmetic per step and is limited by reading weights and KV from memory, so sharing one weight read across a batch raises throughput. Prefill, not decode, is the compute-bound phase.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r9"},{"id":"w02-t03-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A 7B model in bf16 is about 14 GB. On a GPU with 2,000 GB/s of memory bandwidth, what is the upper bound on tokens per second for one stream, ignoring KV reads?","answer":143,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"tokens per second","hint":"bandwidth / bytes per token","explain":"Each token reads about 14 GB, so 2,000 / 14 is about 143 tokens per second. Real systems are lower because KV reads, overheads and kernels add cost. The point is that decode speed is set by bytes moved, not by FLOPs.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r9"},{"id":"w02-t03-q3","type":"match","tag":"diagnose","q":"Match each symptom to its dominant phase.","pairs":[{"l":"TTFT grows with a 30,000-token prompt","r":"Prefill"},{"l":"Streams crawl at 15 tokens per second on a short prompt","r":"Decode"},{"l":"A long prompt stalls other users' streams","r":"Prefill and decode sharing a batch"}],"explain":"Long prompts lengthen prefill and TTFT. A slow stream on a short prompt is decode speed. Stalls for others come from mixing a large prefill into a batch of decodes.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r9"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Why is prefill compute-bound and decode memory-bound?","back":"Prefill processes all prompt tokens in parallel and saturates compute. Decode emits one token per step and mostly reads weights and KV from memory."},{"front":"Which cuts latency more: halving output tokens or halving input tokens?","back":"Halving output may cut about 50% of latency. Halving input may give only 1% to 5%, more at very long contexts."},{"front":"What do chunked prefill and disaggregation fix?","back":"Stalls and interference when long prefills share capacity with decodes."}],"related":["w02-t01","w02-t07","w02-t09","w02-l2"],"refs":["r9","r14","r15","r28","r29"]},{"id":"w02-t04","order":4,"group":"KV cache and serving","title":"The KV cache and how big it gets","minutes":10,"difficulty":"core","summary":"The KV cache stores the keys and values of every token already read so decode never recomputes them. It grows with context length and concurrency and, at long context, caps how many users fit on a GPU.","useIf":"You host a model and must size concurrency, or you must explain why long context makes serving expensive.","avoidIf":"You only call a hosted API. The provider manages the KV cache, and your levers are prompt size, output size and prompt caching.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"In each attention layer every token produces a key vector and a value vector. Decode needs those of all earlier tokens at every step, so engines keep them in GPU memory instead of recomputing. That store is the **KV cache**. Its size per token depends on the model's shape, and the total scales with the number of tokens in flight [[r24]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"KV cache size","expr":"bytes per token = 2 x n_layers x n_kv_heads x head_dim x bytes_per_element","vars":[{"k":"2","v":"one key and one value per head"},{"k":"n_kv_heads","v":"KV heads, not query heads. GQA models have fewer"},{"k":"total","v":"bytes per token x tokens per sequence x concurrent sequences"}],"example":"Llama 3 8B (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128, bf16): 2 x 32 x 8 x 128 x 2 = 131,072 bytes, which is 128 KiB per token and 1 GiB at 8,192 tokens [[tr12]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Derived from public model configs, bf16","head":["Model shape","KV per token","At 8,192 tokens","Source"],"rows":[["Llama 3 8B (32 layers, 8 KV heads)","128 KiB","1 GiB","[[tr12]]"],["Llama 3 70B (80 layers, 8 KV heads)","320 KiB","2.5 GiB","[[r27]]"],["Same 8B body with 32 KV heads (MHA, hypothetical)","512 KiB","4 GiB","Derived"]]},{"t":"lab","id":"kv-cache","intro":"Start with the MHA preset and read KV per sequence. Switch to the GQA preset and compare. Lower KV precision, then stretch the context and watch how many sequences fit. Check the three paging rows: contiguous reservation, paged blocks and paged blocks with a shared prefix. Shapes are illustrative, not vendor models."},{"t":"p","md":"**Concurrency limit.** Memory left for KV is GPU memory minus weights and overhead. Illustrative arithmetic: an 80 GiB GPU holding 8B weights in bf16 (about 15 GiB) with some headroom leaves roughly 56 GiB, which is 56 sequences at 8,192 tokens or 14 at 32,768. At long context the KV cache, not the weights, caps concurrency."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"What spills when KV runs out","md":"vLLM preempts requests to free KV space and recomputes them later. In V1 the default preemption mode is `RECOMPUTE`, which costs latency. The documented fixes are a higher `gpu_memory_utilization`, lower `max_num_seqs` or `max_num_batched_tokens`, or more `tensor_parallel_size` or `pipeline_parallel_size` [[tr6]]."}],"whereToUse":["Capacity planning for a self-hosted model: sequences per GPU at a target context","Comparing candidate models: KV heads and layers matter as much as parameter count","Explaining why a 128K context window is costly to serve, not only to bill","Deciding whether KV quantization, paging or a GQA model is the right fix for a memory cap","Reading a preemption or out-of-memory event on a serving engine"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You run the model and context is long or concurrency is high","You compare architectures and need KV bytes per token","Latency spikes line up with preemption counts"],"avoid":["Hosted API only: tune prompt size and caching instead","Short contexts with few users, where weights dominate memory","Planning from average context: size for the long tail or you will preempt"],"flips":["Context grows from 8K to 64K: per-sequence KV grows 8x, so concurrency drops 8x unless you shrink KV","Weights shrink through quantization: more memory is left for KV, so concurrency rises","Traffic shares a long prefix: paged sharing or prefix caching saves KV for every tenant that shares it"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Ways to fit more sequences","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["GQA or MLA model","You choose the model","The model is fixed","Chosen at selection time, not tunable later"],["KV quantization (FP8, lower)","Memory-bound at long context","Quality is sensitive at long context","Needs an eval gate"],["Paged allocation and prefix sharing","Variable lengths, shared prompts","Unique prompts","Per-tenant isolation needed"],["More GPUs or tensor parallelism","Budget exists","Synchronization overhead dominates","Cost and complexity"],["Shorter context or compaction","Answers need less history","Tasks need the full document","May drop needed evidence"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Out-of-memory or preemption at long context although weights fit comfortably","cause":"KV per sequence was budgeted at the average, not the maximum, context","fix":"Size for the p95 context and set `max_num_seqs` to match [[tr6]]"},{"symptom":"KV estimate is four times too high for a GQA model","cause":"Query heads were used in the formula instead of KV heads","fix":"Read `num_key_value_heads` from the model config"},{"symptom":"Latency spikes under load with no error","cause":"Preempted requests are being recomputed","fix":"Watch the preemption metric and add KV headroom or lower concurrency [[tr6]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t04-s1","title":"Concurrency short on a 70B deployment","situation":"A team serves a 70B GQA model (80 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128) on four 80 GiB GPUs. Typical context is 16,384 tokens and 40 users are active at peak. After weights and overhead about 150 GiB remains for KV.","constraints":["320 KiB of KV per token in bf16","40 concurrent sequences needed","Budget allows a quality check, not new hardware this quarter"],"unknowns":["Is there a long-context quality eval?","Can the engine store KV in FP8 on this hardware?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Add GPUs","verdict":"good","why":"More memory raises the limit without changing numerics.","tradeoff":"Costs money, adds parallelism overhead, and is not available this quarter."},{"name":"Raise memory utilization to the maximum","verdict":"poor","why":"It frees a few GiB, which is one or two sequences, and risks out-of-memory failures.","tradeoff":"Right when the reserved headroom is much larger than needed."},{"name":"Store KV in FP8 and gate it with a long-context eval","verdict":"best","why":"Each sequence needs 5 GiB in bf16, so 150 GiB fits 30. FP8 halves that to 2.5 GiB and fits 60, covering the 40 needed.","tradeoff":"Quality can degrade at long context, so the eval gate is mandatory."}],"flips":[{"if":"The eval shows an unacceptable drop at 16K tokens","then":"Add capacity or shorten context through compaction instead."},{"if":"Most sessions share a long system prompt","then":"Prefix sharing cuts KV per user before you quantize anything."}],"takeaway":"Do the sequence arithmetic first, then pick the cheapest lever that closes the gap."},{"id":"w02-t04-s2","title":"Two candidate models, equal quality","situation":"A team picks an open model for a long-document service. Candidate A has 32 KV heads (MHA). Candidate B has 8 KV heads (GQA). Same layers and head size. Evals on the team's tasks are tied.","constraints":["Self-hosted","Context is long and concurrency is the cost driver","Tied quality on the team's own eval"],"unknowns":["Does the eval cover long-context retrieval?","Is the license the same?"],"question":"Which model do you pick?","options":[{"name":"Candidate A with FP8 KV","verdict":"good","why":"It halves KV, closing part of the gap.","tradeoff":"It is still twice the size of B's KV and adds a quantization risk."},{"name":"Candidate A because more KV heads means better recall","verdict":"poor","why":"The head count is a design choice, not a quality guarantee, and the evals are tied.","tradeoff":"Right if an eval of long-context retrieval showed A is better."},{"name":"Candidate B (GQA)","verdict":"best","why":"It needs a quarter of the KV bytes per token, so the same GPU serves about four times the sequences.","tradeoff":"Confirm no loss on long-context tasks before committing."}],"flips":[{"if":"A long-context eval favours A by a margin that matters","then":"Pay the memory cost or quantize KV and re-test."},{"if":"Context is short and traffic is small","then":"Pick on quality and licence, since KV is not the limit."}],"takeaway":"KV heads are a capacity decision made at model selection."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t04-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A model has 40 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128 and stores KV in fp16 (2 bytes). How many KiB of KV does one token need?","answer":160,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"KiB","hint":"2 x layers x kv heads x head_dim x bytes","explain":"2 x 40 x 8 x 128 x 2 = 163,840 bytes, which is 160 KiB per token. At 8,192 tokens that is 1.25 GiB per sequence.","difficulty":2,"ref":"tr12"},{"id":"w02-t04-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"A model config shows 64 attention heads and 8 key-value heads. Which number goes into the KV size formula?","options":["64, the number of attention heads","8, the number of KV heads","72, the sum of both","Neither, KV size does not depend on heads"],"answer":1,"explain":"Only KV heads are stored. Using 64 would overstate memory by eight times. The cache does depend on head count through KV heads, and the sum has no meaning.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r27"},{"id":"w02-t04-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which choices reduce KV memory per sequence?","options":["Choosing a GQA model over an MHA model","Storing KV in FP8 instead of bf16","Quantizing weights to INT4","Halving the context length"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"GQA lowers KV heads, FP8 halves bytes per element and a shorter context stores fewer tokens. INT4 weights free memory for KV overall but do not shrink the per-sequence KV size.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r24"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Write the KV cache bytes per token formula.","back":"2 x n_layers x n_kv_heads x head_dim x bytes_per_element. Multiply by tokens and by concurrent sequences for the total."},{"front":"How much KV does one token need on Llama 3 8B in bf16?","back":"128 KiB per token, which is 1 GiB at 8,192 tokens (32 layers, 8 KV heads, head_dim 128)."},{"front":"What happens when a serving engine runs out of KV space?","back":"It preempts requests and recomputes them later. In vLLM V1 the default mode is recompute, which costs latency."}],"related":["w02-t05","w02-t06","w02-t10","w02-l3"],"refs":["r24","r27","tr6","tr12","r6"]},{"id":"w02-t05","order":5,"group":"KV cache and serving","title":"MHA, GQA, MQA and MLA","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Attention variants decide how many key and value heads a model stores. Fewer KV heads, or a compressed latent, shrink the KV cache and the memory traffic of decode, at some quality risk.","useIf":"You compare open models for serving cost, or you must explain why two models of equal size need different GPU memory.","avoidIf":"You call a hosted API and cannot see or change the attention design.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"In **multi-head attention (MHA)** every query head has its own key and value head. **Multi-query attention (MQA)** shares one KV head across all query heads, which greatly reduces the size of keys and values and the memory bandwidth of incremental decoding, at only minor quality loss [[tr10]]. **Grouped-query attention (GQA)** sits between them: more than one KV head but fewer than query heads. MHA checkpoints can be uptrained to GQA with about 5% of the original pre-training compute and reach quality close to MHA at a speed close to MQA [[r10]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Multi-head latent attention (MLA)**, used in DeepSeek-V2, takes a different route: it compresses keys and values into a latent vector. DeepSeek reports a 93.3% smaller KV cache and 5.76 times the maximum generation throughput compared with DeepSeek 67B, for a model with 236B total and 21B active parameters and a 128K context [[r11]]. That figure compares two different models, so it is not a like-for-like benchmark."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"MHA, 32 KV heads","value":"100%","note":"KV per token, baseline (derived)"},{"label":"GQA, 8 KV heads","value":"25%","note":"4x smaller than MHA with 32 query heads (derived)"},{"label":"MQA, 1 KV head","value":"3.1%","note":"32x smaller (derived)"},{"label":"MLA, DeepSeek-V2","value":"-93.3%","note":"KV cache versus DeepSeek 67B [[r11]]"}]},{"t":"table","caption":"Same query heads, different KV storage","head":["Variant","KV heads per layer","KV size versus MHA","Trade-off"],"rows":[["MHA","= query heads","1x","Best quality baseline, largest cache"],["GQA","More than 1, fewer than query heads","KV heads / query heads","Near MHA quality with a small cache after uptraining"],["MQA","1","1 / query heads","Smallest cache, minor quality loss"],["MLA","Latent vector, not per-head KV","Large reduction, design specific","Needs model and kernel support, different architecture"]]},{"t":"callout","kind":"why","title":"Why fewer KV heads speed up decode","md":"Decode is memory-bandwidth-bound and re-reads the KV cache for every generated token. A smaller cache means fewer bytes to move per step and more sequences that fit in memory, so both TPOT and concurrency improve [[tr10]]."}],"whereToUse":["Comparing open models for long-context serving: read `num_key_value_heads` in the model config","Deciding whether an in-house MHA model should be uptrained to GQA","Explaining why a larger model can be cheaper to serve than a smaller one with more KV heads","Estimating KV memory before benchmarking, using the formula in the KV cache topic","Judging a vendor claim about long-context throughput"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Long context and high concurrency make KV memory the cost driver","You control model selection or training and can test long-context quality","You want a smaller cache without changing precision or the serving engine"],"avoid":["Do not choose on KV size alone when the task needs the best long-range recall: test it","Do not expect to retrofit MLA onto an existing model. It is an architecture choice","Do not treat a paper's headline reduction as a prediction for your own candidates"],"flips":["Short contexts and few users: weights dominate memory, so KV design matters little","A quality-sensitive task shows a drop with fewer KV heads: take a larger GQA group count or MHA","Your engine lacks MLA kernels: choose a GQA model or wait for support"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with other ways to shrink KV","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["GQA model","Mature engine support, near-MHA quality","You need the smallest possible cache","Choose at model selection"],["MQA model","Maximum cache cut","Quality is sensitive","Minor quality loss, as reported"],["MLA model","Very long context at high concurrency","Engine or tooling support is thin","Different architecture, not a drop-in"],["KV quantization on any model","Model is fixed","Long-context quality is fragile","Needs an eval gate"],["Paging and prefix sharing","Variable lengths, shared prompts","Unique long prompts","Isolation work"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Two models of the same parameter count need very different GPU memory at 32K context","cause":"Different KV head counts or layers change KV bytes per token","fix":"Compute KV per token from each config before you size hardware [[r27]]"},{"symptom":"A migration to MQA lowers long-context answer quality","cause":"Sharing a single KV head removes capacity that the task used","fix":"Use GQA with a moderate group count, evaluate at your context lengths [[r10]]"},{"symptom":"An MLA model is slower than expected on your stack","cause":"The engine runs a fallback attention path without MLA kernels","fix":"Check the engine version and benchmark before committing"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t05-s1","title":"Own MHA checkpoint, memory-bound serving","situation":"A team owns a trained MHA model and serves it on its own GPUs. KV memory caps concurrency. Retraining from scratch is out of the question, but a small training budget exists.","constraints":["Existing MHA checkpoint","Small training budget","Quality must stay near the current level"],"unknowns":["How much compute is 5% of pre-training for this model?","Is there a long-context eval?"],"question":"What do you do?","options":[{"name":"Uptrain to MQA","verdict":"good","why":"It gives the largest KV cut.","tradeoff":"More quality risk than GQA on tasks that use long-range detail."},{"name":"Drop KV heads at inference with no retraining","verdict":"poor","why":"The recovery of quality in the paper comes from uptraining, so skipping it is an untested risk.","tradeoff":"Only acceptable as a throwaway experiment to see the memory effect."},{"name":"Uptrain the checkpoint to GQA with a moderate group count","verdict":"best","why":"The published recipe reaches near-MHA quality at near-MQA speed for about 5% of the original compute.","tradeoff":"It still costs training compute and needs an eval to confirm long-context quality."}],"flips":[{"if":"No training budget exists","then":"Quantize KV with an eval gate, or add GPUs."},{"if":"Quality drops on the long-context eval after uptraining","then":"Raise the number of KV groups."}],"takeaway":"GQA by uptraining is the cheap path from an MHA checkpoint."},{"id":"w02-t05-s2","title":"128K context, GQA or MLA","situation":"A team must serve a 128K-token document workload at high concurrency. One candidate is a GQA model. The other uses MLA and advertises a very small cache.","constraints":["Very long context","High concurrency","Serving engine is fixed and supports both"],"unknowns":["Quality on your documents at 128K","KV bytes per token measured on your engine"],"question":"How do you decide?","options":[{"name":"Choose the GQA model for mature support","verdict":"good","why":"It is the lower-risk path with predictable tooling.","tradeoff":"You may leave memory and throughput on the table."},{"name":"Choose MLA because it advertises the largest cache cut","verdict":"poor","why":"A headline reduction against another model does not predict your quality or throughput.","tradeoff":"Right once your own tests confirm the gain and the engine support."},{"name":"Benchmark both at your context lengths for quality, KV bytes and throughput on your engine","verdict":"best","why":"The 93.3% figure compares DeepSeek-V2 with a different model, so only your own measurement answers the question.","tradeoff":"It takes a day of testing."}],"flips":[{"if":"The engine has no efficient MLA path","then":"Choose GQA."},{"if":"Quality ties and MLA memory use is far lower in your test","then":"Choose MLA for concurrency."}],"takeaway":"Treat paper headlines as hypotheses. Measure on your own context lengths."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t05-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A model has 64 query heads and 8 KV heads. By what factor is its KV cache per token smaller than the same model with MHA?","answer":8,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"x smaller","hint":"query heads / KV heads","explain":"KV bytes scale with the number of KV heads. MHA would store 64, GQA stores 8, so the cache is 64 / 8 = 8 times smaller.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r10"},{"id":"w02-t05-q2","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each attention variant to its defining idea.","pairs":[{"l":"MHA","r":"Every query head has its own K and V"},{"l":"MQA","r":"One KV head shared by all query heads"},{"l":"GQA","r":"Groups of query heads share a KV head"},{"l":"MLA","r":"KV compressed into a latent vector"}],"explain":"These are the standard definitions. GQA sits between MHA and MQA, and MLA compresses rather than shares.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r10"},{"id":"w02-t05-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"A paper reports a 93.3% KV cache reduction for MLA. What is the correct way to use that number?","options":["Assume any model with MLA has a cache 93.3% smaller than any GQA model","Apply it to a GQA model by changing the config","Treat it as a lead to test, since it compares DeepSeek-V2 with a different model","Ignore MLA because a 93.3% cut is impossible"],"answer":2,"explain":"The figure is against DeepSeek 67B, a different design. It shows what is possible but does not predict your candidates. It cannot be applied to GQA by config, and the paper does report it, so it is not impossible.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r11"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does GQA differ from MHA and MQA?","back":"MHA gives every query head its own KV head, MQA shares one KV head across all, and GQA shares one KV head per group of query heads."},{"front":"What did the GQA paper show about converting MHA checkpoints?","back":"Uptraining with about 5% of pre-training compute reached quality near MHA at speed near MQA."},{"front":"What does MLA do differently?","back":"It compresses keys and values into a latent vector. DeepSeek-V2 reports a 93.3% smaller KV cache versus DeepSeek 67B."}],"related":["w02-t04","w02-t06","w02-t10","w02-l3"],"refs":["r10","r11","r27","tr10","r24"]},{"id":"w02-t06","order":6,"group":"KV cache and serving","title":"PagedAttention and fragmentation","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"PagedAttention stores the KV cache in fixed-size blocks mapped through a block table, like virtual memory pages. It removes most reserved-but-unused memory and lets requests share blocks.","useIf":"You run your own inference engine and want more concurrent sequences per GPU, or you need to understand how engines share KV.","avoidIf":"You use a hosted API. The provider's engine already does this, and you cannot tune it.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A simple engine reserves one contiguous KV region per request, sized for the longest answer it might produce. Most requests end earlier, so much of that memory is reserved and never used, and gaps between regions are too small to reuse. The vLLM paper shows that this waste limits batch size, and with it throughput [[r6]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**PagedAttention** splits each sequence's KV cache into fixed-size blocks and keeps a **block table** that maps the sequence's logical blocks to physical blocks anywhere in GPU memory. Blocks are allocated on demand as tokens are generated, so waste is confined to the last, partly filled block. Because a block table is only a mapping, several sequences can point to the same physical block, which enables sharing within a request (parallel samples) and across requests (a common prefix). The paper reports near-zero waste and 2x to 4x higher throughput at the same latency than the state of the art in 2023 [[r6]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w02-t06-d1","title":"Two requests sharing a prefix block","alt":"Two block tables point to one shared physical block for the common prefix and to separate physical blocks for their own tokens.","src":"flowchart LR\n  A[Block table A] --> P1[(Physical 7: shared prefix)]\n  B[Block table B] --> P1\n  A --> P2[(Physical 3: A tokens)]\n  B --> P3[(Physical 9: B tokens)]"},{"t":"p","md":"In vLLM each block carries a reference count, so a shared block is freed only when no request uses it. Prefix caching builds on the same blocks: it hashes full blocks and reuses them, and it caches only full blocks [[r7]]. The multimodal example in the vLLM design doc assumes blocks of 16 tokens (its other examples use 4) [[r7]]. Smaller blocks cut last-block waste but increase table size and lookup work, which is a general trade-off to test on your engine."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"Paging does not remove the capacity limit","md":"Blocks are a pool. When the pool is empty the engine preempts requests and, in vLLM V1, recomputes them later by default [[tr6]]. Paging raises how much of the pool is useful. It does not enlarge the pool."}],"whereToUse":["Self-hosted serving of open models with vLLM, SGLang or similar engines","Workloads with highly variable output length, where contiguous reservation wastes memory","Parallel sampling or beam search, where requests share a long prompt","Multi-turn chat where each turn extends a shared history","Reading engine metrics such as block usage and preemption counts"],"whenToUse":{"use":["You host models and sequence lengths vary widely","Many requests share a prefix and you want to store it once","You see low batch sizes although GPU memory looks full"],"avoid":["Hosted API only, where the engine is not yours","A tiny deployment with fixed, short sequences, where waste is already small","Low, steady traffic where GPUs sit idle anyway and the engine is not the bottleneck"],"flips":["Traffic is bursty with long outputs: paging helps most, so prefer an engine that pages by default","Tenants must not share cache state: keep paging but salt prefix hashes per tenant","Hardware is unusual and no paged engine supports it: you may need a custom allocator"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with other KV memory strategies","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Paged KV (vLLM, SGLang)","Variable lengths, shared prefixes","Hardware without engine support","Block table overhead, isolation to design"],["Contiguous max-length reservation","Fixed, short sequences","Variable or long outputs","Large reserved-but-unused memory"],["Lower max sequence length","Few requests need long outputs","Long outputs are common","Truncates legitimate requests"],["KV quantization","Memory-bound at long context","Quality-sensitive long tasks","Eval gate needed"],["More GPUs","Budget exists","Cost-sensitive","Parallelism overhead"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Batch size stays low while memory looks full","cause":"Contiguous reservation holds space for maximum lengths","fix":"Use an engine with paged KV allocation [[r6]]"},{"symptom":"Latency spikes once the block pool is exhausted","cause":"Requests are preempted and recomputed","fix":"Add KV headroom, lower `max_num_seqs`, or shorten contexts [[tr6]]"},{"symptom":"Prefix sharing fails to help although prompts look alike","cause":"Only full blocks are cached, or a token in the first block differs","fix":"Keep the shared prefix byte-identical and long enough to fill blocks [[r7]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t06-s1","title":"Reserved memory starves the batch","situation":"A team serves an open model with a stack that reserves KV for the maximum sequence length per request. Outputs range from 50 to 4,000 tokens. GPUs are memory-full at a batch of 12, and utilization is low.","constraints":["Self-hosted","Highly variable output length","Switching engines is possible with a week of work"],"unknowns":["Is the model supported by a paged engine?","Do requests share long prefixes?"],"question":"What is the best change?","options":[{"name":"Lower the maximum sequence length cap","verdict":"good","why":"It reduces reservation immediately.","tradeoff":"It truncates or rejects legitimate long requests."},{"name":"Write a custom block allocator","verdict":"poor","why":"It duplicates mature engine work and carries correctness risk.","tradeoff":"Only right for hardware or models that no engine supports."},{"name":"Move to an engine with paged KV allocation","verdict":"best","why":"Blocks are allocated as tokens appear, so the memory that was reserved for unused length becomes usable batch capacity.","tradeoff":"A migration with testing, and new operational knobs."}],"flips":[{"if":"All requests are short and uniform","then":"The gain is small, so keep the current stack."},{"if":"The model is unsupported by paged engines","then":"A custom allocator or a different model becomes the choice."}],"takeaway":"Adopt a paged engine before inventing a memory manager."},{"id":"w02-t06-s2","title":"Self-host to get PagedAttention?","situation":"A product calls a hosted model and users complain about latency. A teammate proposes self-hosting an open model to benefit from PagedAttention. Traffic is about 20 requests per minute.","constraints":["Low, steady traffic","Hosted model quality is sufficient","No residency constraint"],"unknowns":["Where does time go: prefill, decode or queueing?","What would idle GPUs cost?"],"question":"What do you do first?","options":[{"name":"Benchmark self-hosting at projected sustained volume","verdict":"good","why":"It tests the cost and latency claim with real numbers.","tradeoff":"It takes time and may confirm that hosted is cheaper."},{"name":"Self-host at this volume","verdict":"poor","why":"At 20 requests per minute GPUs sit idle, and you take on operations without evidence that engine memory is the bottleneck.","tradeoff":"Right when volume is sustained and high, or when residency or model control requires it."},{"name":"Stay hosted and tune prompt size, output length and prompt caching","verdict":"best","why":"At low volume paging gains are small, and the hosted provider already runs an optimised engine. Your levers are on the request.","tradeoff":"You depend on the provider's capacity and cannot tune the engine."}],"flips":[{"if":"Volume grows to sustained high utilization","then":"Self-hosting with a paged engine can win on cost."},{"if":"Data may not leave your boundary","then":"Self-host regardless of volume."}],"takeaway":"Engine internals matter when you own the GPUs and keep them busy."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t06-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A request ends at 500 tokens but contiguous allocation reserved 8,192 tokens at 128 KiB per token. How many MiB were reserved and never used?","answer":961.5,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"MiB","hint":"(8,192 - 500) x 128 KiB, then divide by 1,024","explain":"7,692 unused tokens x 128 KiB is 984,576 KiB, which is 961.5 MiB. With paging, only the unused tail of the last block (at most one block) would be wasted.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r6"},{"id":"w02-t06-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What lets two requests share a common prompt prefix under PagedAttention?","options":["They copy the prefix into one larger contiguous region","The model weights are shared","Their block tables point to the same physical blocks","The tokenizer merges the requests"],"answer":2,"explain":"A block table is a mapping, so two tables can reference one physical block, with a reference count. Copying defeats the saving, and weight sharing and tokenizers are unrelated.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r6"},{"id":"w02-t06-q3","type":"multi","tag":"tradeoff","q":"Which statements about paged KV and prefix caching in vLLM are correct?","options":["Only full blocks are cached","Paging enlarges the total KV pool","A shared block is freed only when no request uses it","Prefix caching hashes each block using the parent hash and block tokens"],"answers":[0,2,3],"explain":"The design doc says only full blocks are cached and blocks carry reference counts. Block hashes include the parent hash and tokens. Paging uses the pool more efficiently but does not make it larger.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r7"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What problem does PagedAttention solve?","back":"Reserved-but-unused and fragmented KV memory. Fixed-size blocks and a block table allow on-demand allocation and sharing."},{"front":"What did the vLLM paper report?","back":"Near-zero KV waste and 2x to 4x throughput at equal latency versus the state of the art in 2023."},{"front":"Does paging make the KV pool bigger?","back":"No. It makes more of the pool useful. When the pool is empty, requests are preempted and recomputed."}],"related":["w02-t04","w02-t07","w02-t08","w02-l3"],"refs":["r6","r7","tr6"]},{"id":"w02-t07","order":7,"group":"KV cache and serving","title":"Continuous batching and chunked prefill","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Continuous batching schedules the GPU one iteration at a time so finished requests leave and new ones join. Chunked prefill splits long prompts so they stop stalling everyone else's streams.","useIf":"You run an inference engine for many users with mixed prompt lengths and you see throughput gaps or ITL spikes.","avoidIf":"You only call a hosted API. Engine scheduling is not yours to tune, and the lever there is separating workloads.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"**Static batching** forms a batch and runs it until the slowest request finishes. Short requests leave idle slots, and new requests wait for the next batch. **Continuous batching**, introduced by Orca, schedules per iteration: a request that finishes leaves immediately and a waiting request takes its slot. On GPT-3 175B the Orca paper reports 36.9 times the throughput of FasterTransformer at the same level of latency [[tr2]]."},{"t":"p","md":"Continuous batching still has **head-of-line blocking**. If one iteration contains a long prefill, it runs for a long time and every decode in that batch waits, so ITL spikes for every active stream. **Chunked prefill** fixes this by splitting a long prompt into near-equal chunks and batching each chunk with decodes. Sarathi-Serve calls the result a stall-free schedule and reports 2.6 times the capacity on Mistral-7B (one A100) and up to 3.7 times on Yi-34B (two A100s) compared with vLLM under tail-latency limits [[r9]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"One scheduler iteration with chunked prefill","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Decodes first","md":"Every running request gets one token slot.","tag":"priority"},{"title":"Fill the budget","md":"Remaining tokens go to pending prefills, split into chunks if needed.","tag":"max_num_batched_tokens"},{"title":"Run one pass","md":"Decodes and the prefill chunk share one forward pass.","tag":"GPU"},{"title":"Retire and admit","md":"Finished requests free blocks and waiting requests join.","tag":"iteration level"}]},{"t":"p","md":"In vLLM V1 chunked prefill is on by default whenever possible. The scheduler batches all pending decodes first, then fills the remaining `max_num_batched_tokens` budget with prefills and chunks any that do not fit. Smaller values such as 2048 give better ITL because fewer prefill tokens slow each step. Larger values give better TTFT, and the docs recommend more than 8192 for throughput on smaller models and large GPUs [[tr6]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Chunking is a trade, not a free fix","md":"A chunked prompt finishes its prefill over several iterations, so its own TTFT rises somewhat. The gain is for everyone else's streams, and for capacity under a tail-latency target [[r9]]."}],"whereToUse":["Self-hosted chat and agent serving with vLLM or a similar engine","Mixed traffic where a few very long prompts share GPUs with many short ones","Choosing a token budget per step to trade ITL against TTFT","Capacity tests that must meet a p99 ITL target","Explaining why a hosted provider recommends separate deployments for interactive and bulk work [[r14]]"],"whenToUse":{"use":["p99 ITL spikes line up with long prompts arriving","You need more capacity under a tail-latency limit without adding GPUs","Traffic mixes short chat turns with long document prompts"],"avoid":["Hosted APIs where you cannot set scheduler budgets","A single-tenant offline job where only total throughput counts and ITL does not","Tiny prompts only: with no long prefills there is no stall to remove"],"flips":["TTFT for long prompts matters more than ITL: raise the token budget","ITL is the SLO: lower the token budget and accept slower long-prompt TTFT","Targets for TTFT and TPOT diverge sharply at fleet scale: consider prefill and decode disaggregation [[r28]] [[r29]]"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with other scheduling choices","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Static batching","Uniform, offline jobs","Mixed lengths or interactive traffic","Idle slots, queueing behind long requests"],["Continuous batching, no chunking","Short prompts, light load","Long prompts arrive among streams","ITL stalls from long prefills"],["Continuous batching with chunked prefill","Mixed traffic with a tail-latency target","You need the lowest TTFT for long prompts","Extra TTFT for chunked prompts"],["Prefill and decode disaggregation","Large fleets, separate TTFT and TPOT SLOs","Small deployments","KV transfer between machines, complex scheduling"],["Separate deployments per workload","Hosted API, interactive plus bulk traffic","Traffic is tiny","Capacity is split, so utilization falls"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Every user's stream pauses when one user pastes a long document","cause":"A large prefill shares the iteration with decodes","fix":"Enable chunked prefill and lower the per-step token budget [[tr6]]"},{"symptom":"Throughput is low on a large GPU running a small model","cause":"A small token budget leaves compute idle","fix":"Raise `max_num_batched_tokens` above 8192 and re-measure ITL [[tr6]]"},{"symptom":"Interactive latency is poor while a nightly job runs","cause":"Bulk and interactive traffic share batches and cache on one deployment","fix":"Use separate deployments per workload [[r14]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t07-s1","title":"ITL spikes when long prompts arrive","situation":"A chat service on a self-hosted engine streams at 30 ms per token. When a user submits a 20,000-token document, other users' streams freeze for about a second. The SLO is p99 ITL under 100 ms.","constraints":["Self-hosted","Mixed short and long prompts","p99 ITL is the SLO"],"unknowns":["Is chunked prefill already enabled?","What is the current per-step token budget?"],"question":"What do you change first?","options":[{"name":"Add GPUs and spread the load","verdict":"good","why":"More capacity dilutes the stalls.","tradeoff":"It costs money and leaves the interference in place."},{"name":"Enable chunked prefill and lower the per-step token budget","verdict":"best","why":"Decodes run in every iteration, so no stream waits for a whole 20,000-token prefill.","tradeoff":"Long-prompt TTFT rises, and throughput may fall a little at a small budget."},{"name":"Cap max output tokens","verdict":"poor","why":"The freeze comes from a long prefill, not from long answers.","tradeoff":"Right when long outputs hold slots and starve newcomers."}],"flips":[{"if":"Long prompts are rare and users accept the pause","then":"Leave the defaults."},{"if":"Fleet is large and TTFT and ITL targets conflict","then":"Disaggregate prefill and decode."}],"takeaway":"Stalls are a scheduling problem. Chunk the prefill so decodes keep running."},{"id":"w02-t07-s2","title":"Overnight bulk summarisation","situation":"A nightly job summarises 300,000 documents on a self-hosted small model on large GPUs. Nobody reads the output until morning. The job must finish in 6 hours.","constraints":["Offline","Throughput and cost are the goals","No ITL target"],"unknowns":["Is the 6 hour window at risk at today's volume?","Can the job run on dedicated GPUs?"],"question":"How do you tune scheduling?","options":[{"name":"Tune for ITL with a small token budget","verdict":"poor","why":"ITL is irrelevant here, and a small budget leaves compute idle.","tradeoff":"Right for interactive streaming where users feel every pause."},{"name":"Keep one deployment shared with chat and turn on priority","verdict":"good","why":"It saves hardware.","tradeoff":"Bulk traffic still shares batches and cache with chat, risking interactive latency."},{"name":"Run on dedicated capacity with a large token budget, above 8192","verdict":"best","why":"Large steps keep a big GPU busy and maximise tokens per hour.","tradeoff":"Individual request latency is poor, which is acceptable offline."}],"flips":[{"if":"The job must share GPUs with live chat","then":"Cap the bulk job's concurrency and tune for chat ITL first."},{"if":"The window is missed","then":"Raise concurrency or add GPUs before changing scheduling."}],"takeaway":"The right token budget depends on whether anyone waits for a single stream."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t07-q1","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Streams for all users pause for about a second whenever one long prompt arrives. What most likely causes this?","options":["The KV cache is quantized","Speculative decoding is rejecting tokens","A large prefill shares the iteration with the decodes","The tokenizer is slow on long inputs"],"answer":2,"explain":"A long prefill occupies the whole iteration, so every decode in the batch waits. Chunked prefill removes this. KV quantization, speculation and tokenization do not create synchronous pauses across all streams.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r9"},{"id":"w02-t07-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A scheduler has a 2,048 token budget per iteration and 10 running decodes, each using 1 token. How many iterations does a 20,000-token prompt need if every iteration gives it the remaining budget?","answer":10,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"iterations","hint":"Chunk size is 2,048 minus 10","explain":"Decodes use 10 tokens, leaving 2,038 for the prefill chunk. 20,000 / 2,038 is 9.81, so it needs 10 iterations. A larger budget finishes sooner, which is the TTFT side of the trade.","difficulty":2,"ref":"tr6"},{"id":"w02-t07-q3","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order the steps of one chunked-prefill scheduler iteration in vLLM V1.","items":["Schedule all pending decodes","Fill the remaining token budget with prefill chunks","Run one forward pass","Retire finished requests and admit waiting ones"],"explain":"Decodes are prioritised, prefill fills what is left, the pass runs, and finished requests leave so waiting ones can join on the next iteration.","difficulty":2,"ref":"tr6"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does continuous batching change versus static batching?","back":"It schedules per iteration, so finished requests leave and waiting ones join at once instead of waiting for the slowest request."},{"front":"What does chunked prefill fix, and what does it cost?","back":"It removes decode stalls from long prefills. The chunked prompt's own TTFT rises somewhat."},{"front":"How does max_num_batched_tokens trade ITL against TTFT in vLLM?","back":"Smaller values (such as 2048) give better ITL. Larger values give better TTFT and throughput."}],"related":["w02-t03","w02-t06","w02-t09","w02-l2"],"refs":["tr2","r9","tr6","r14","r28","r29"]},{"id":"w02-t08","order":8,"group":"KV cache and serving","title":"Server-side prefix caching","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Inference engines can keep the KV blocks of finished requests and reuse them when a new request starts with the same tokens. It is the self-hosted twin of provider prompt caching.","useIf":"You run your own engine and many requests share a long prefix such as a system prompt, tools or a document.","avoidIf":"Prompts are unique, or tenants must not infer each other's prompts and you have not set up isolation.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A prefix cache keeps the KV blocks computed for earlier requests. A new request whose first tokens match skips prefill for the shared part and starts at the first new token. The output does not change, because the same attention state is reused [[r7]]. Hosted prompt caching is the provider-run version of the same idea."},{"t":"p","md":"**vLLM** takes a hash-based approach. Each KV block is identified by a hash of its parent block's hash, its own tokens and any extra hashes (LoRA ID, multimodal input hash and `cache_salt`). Only full blocks are cached, and unused blocks are evicted in LRU order [[r7]]. **SGLang** keeps KV in a radix tree over token sequences (RadixAttention), which reuses prefixes automatically across calls and reports up to 6.4 times the throughput of earlier systems on structured LLM programs [[r8]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w02-t08-d1","title":"Hash chain of a cached prefix","alt":"The salt and extra hashes feed the first block hash. Each later block hash includes the parent hash, so a change in an early block changes every later hash.","src":"flowchart LR\n  S[cache_salt and extra hashes] --> H1[Block 1 hash]\n  H1 --> H2[Block 2 hash]\n  H2 --> H3[Block 3 hash]\n  H3 --> N[New tokens: prefill from here]"},{"t":"p","md":"**Hit conditions.** The tokens must match from the very first token through whole blocks, the extra hashes must match, and the blocks must not have been evicted. One changed token early in the prompt changes every later hash."},{"t":"p","md":"**Multi-tenant risk.** Cache hits are faster, and speed is observable. An audit of LLM APIs found global cache sharing across users in seven providers, including OpenAI, so timing can reveal whether someone else's prompt was cached [[r12]]. In vLLM, a per-request `cache_salt` is injected into the first block's hash so that only requests with the same salt share blocks, which blocks timing attacks [[r7]]. Hash choice matters too: `sha256` is the default since v0.11, `sha256_cbor` gives reproducible hashes, and `xxhash` is faster but not cryptographic, so the docs warn of collision risk and possible leakage in multi-tenant setups [[r7]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Isolation costs hits","md":"A salt per tenant stops cross-tenant sharing, including of a common system prompt. Choose the salt granularity to match your trust boundary: per tenant, per trust group or none."}],"whereToUse":["Self-hosted chat with a long shared system prompt and tool definitions","RAG or agent loops that resend a growing history each turn","Structured LLM programs and tree-of-thought style search that branch from a common prefix","Batch jobs that put the same instructions and examples in front of many items","Multi-tenant platforms that need per-tenant cache isolation"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A large share of tokens is an identical prefix across requests","Requests arrive often enough that blocks stay resident until reused","You can keep the prefix byte-identical and put variable text last"],"avoid":["Every prompt is unique or starts with variable text","Tenants must not be able to probe each other and no salting is in place","You use a non-cryptographic hash in a multi-tenant deployment without a risk review"],"flips":["Users share nothing confidential: keep one global cache to maximise hits","Compliance requires strict separation: salt per tenant and accept the lower hit rate","Memory is tight: the cache competes with active sequences, so evictions rise and hits fall"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Engine prefix cache, one global scope","Single trust domain, shared prompts","Tenants must be isolated","Timing side channel across users"],["Engine prefix cache with per-tenant salt","Multi-tenant platform","Tenants share nothing anyway","Lower hit rate, no cross-tenant reuse"],["No prefix caching","Unique prompts, strict isolation, tiny traffic","Long shared prefixes","Repeated prefill cost"],["Hosted provider prompt cache","You use a managed API","You need engine-level control","Provider-defined lifetime and pricing"],["Semantic cache","Repeating questions with the same answer","Answers differ per user","Changes the answer, not only the cost"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Prefix cache shows no hits although prompts look alike","cause":"Variable text such as a timestamp sits early in the prompt, or the shared part is shorter than one block","fix":"Move variable text last and keep the shared prefix identical and longer than a block [[r7]]"},{"symptom":"A user can tell whether another tenant used a prompt, by timing","cause":"Cache blocks are shared globally across tenants","fix":"Send a per-tenant `cache_salt` so hashes differ across tenants [[r7]] [[r12]]"},{"symptom":"A security review flags the `xxhash` prefix-cache setting on a multi-tenant fleet","cause":"A non-cryptographic hash theoretically raises collision risk, which the vLLM docs say can cause undefined behaviour or leak private information","fix":"Use `sha256` (default since v0.11) or `sha256_cbor` in multi-tenant deployments [[r7]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t08-s1","title":"Shared platform, confidential prompts","situation":"A SaaS platform serves many customer organisations from one vLLM fleet. Each organisation uses a confidential system prompt of about 4,000 tokens, reused across its users. Contracts forbid one customer inferring another's prompts.","constraints":["Multi-tenant","Confidential prompts","Each tenant has many users and steady traffic"],"unknowns":["Can the gateway add a salt per request?","Is a shared base prompt common to all tenants?"],"question":"How do you configure prefix caching?","options":[{"name":"One global cache, no salt","verdict":"poor","why":"Timing differences can reveal that another tenant's prompt was cached.","tradeoff":"Right for a single-tenant deployment or public prompts."},{"name":"Disable prefix caching","verdict":"good","why":"It removes the risk completely.","tradeoff":"You pay the full 4,000-token prefill on every request."},{"name":"Enable prefix caching with a per-tenant cache_salt","verdict":"best","why":"Users of one tenant share blocks, and other tenants cannot match them or time them.","tradeoff":"A common base prompt is no longer shared across tenants, so hit rate is lower than global."}],"flips":[{"if":"Several tenants form one trust group and agree to share","then":"Give that group a common salt so they share blocks. The salt covers the whole hash chain, so mixing salted and unsalted parts in one request is not possible."},{"if":"Each tenant has very low traffic","then":"Hits will be rare, so the benefit may not justify the memory."}],"takeaway":"Salt at the trust boundary, and accept the hit-rate cost."},{"id":"w02-t08-s2","title":"Internal assistant, one trust domain","situation":"An internal assistant for one company uses a 6,000-token system prompt and tool list for all staff. The platform team asks whether to salt the cache per user for safety.","constraints":["Single trust domain","Prompt is not confidential among staff","TTFT is the pain point"],"unknowns":["Do staff see different tools by role?","What is the threat model?"],"question":"Do you salt per user?","options":[{"name":"Salt per user","verdict":"poor","why":"It splits the cache into one copy per user and removes most reuse for no stated threat.","tradeoff":"Right when users must not be able to probe each other."},{"name":"Enable prefix caching with no salt and keep the prefix stable","verdict":"best","why":"All staff share one prefix, so each request skips most of the prefill.","tradeoff":"A timing signal exists inside the company, which is acceptable under this threat model."},{"name":"Salt per department","verdict":"good","why":"It keeps hits within a group while isolating groups.","tradeoff":"Fewer hits than global, and only worthwhile if departments must be separated."}],"flips":[{"if":"HR or legal data appears in prompts for some roles","then":"Salt by role or department."},{"if":"Tools differ by role","then":"Group by identical tool lists so each group shares a stable prefix."}],"takeaway":"Isolation is a dial set by the threat model, not a default."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t08-q1","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which inputs feed the hash of a KV block in vLLM prefix caching?","options":["The hash of the parent block","The tokens in this block","The request arrival time","Extra hashes such as a LoRA ID or cache_salt"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"The block hash combines the parent hash, the block's tokens and extra hashes. Arrival time is not part of it, which is why identical prefixes can match across requests.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r7"},{"id":"w02-t08-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A 1,000-token prompt matches a cached prefix exactly. vLLM caches only full blocks and the example block size is 16. How many tokens can be served from cache?","answer":992,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"tokens","hint":"floor(1,000 / 16) x 16","explain":"1,000 / 16 is 62.5, so 62 full blocks are cached, which is 992 tokens. The last 8 tokens fill a partial block and are recomputed.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r7"},{"id":"w02-t08-q3","type":"single","tag":"security","q":"Which setting best defends a shared vLLM fleet against timing probes of cached prompts?","options":["A faster non-cryptographic hash","A per-tenant cache_salt in each request","A larger block size","Lower max_num_seqs"],"answer":1,"explain":"The salt changes the first block's hash, so other tenants cannot reuse or time those blocks. A weaker hash raises collision risk, and block size or concurrency settings do not isolate tenants.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r7"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does vLLM identify a cached KV block?","back":"By a hash of the parent block hash, the block's tokens and extra hashes such as LoRA ID, multimodal hash and cache_salt. Only full blocks are cached."},{"front":"What does RadixAttention do?","back":"It keeps KV in a radix tree of token sequences so prefixes are reused automatically across calls. SGLang reports up to 6.4x throughput on structured programs."},{"front":"How do you stop cross-tenant timing probes in vLLM?","back":"Send a per-request cache_salt, which is injected into the first block hash, so only same-salt requests share blocks."}],"related":["w02-t06","w02-t11","w02-t14","w02-l3"],"refs":["r7","r8","r12"]},{"id":"w02-t09","order":9,"group":"KV cache and serving","title":"Speculative decoding","minutes":8,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A cheap draft proposes several tokens and the large model verifies them in one pass. Accepted tokens come at the price of one step, and the output distribution stays the same.","useIf":"You run your own model, single-stream decode speed matters, and load is low to medium so the GPU is memory-bound with spare compute.","avoidIf":"The GPU is saturated by large batches, or the draft agrees with the target too rarely to pay for itself.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Decode generates one token per step and is memory-bandwidth-bound, so compute sits idle. **Speculative decoding** uses that spare compute. A cheap **draft** proposes a few tokens. The large **target** model scores all of them in a single parallel pass, keeps the longest prefix it agrees with and samples one more token itself. The paper shows the output distribution is unchanged and reports 2x to 3x faster decoding on T5-XXL with identical outputs [[r19]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"One speculative step","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Draft","md":"The draft proposes k tokens cheaply.","tag":"small"},{"title":"Verify","md":"The target scores all k in one parallel pass.","tag":"large"},{"title":"Accept","md":"Keep the agreeing prefix, resample the first disagreement.","tag":"unchanged output"},{"title":"Repeat","md":"Continue from the new end of the text.","tag":"loop"}]},{"t":"formula","title":"Expected tokens per target pass","expr":"E[tokens] = (1 - a^(k+1)) / (1 - a)","vars":[{"k":"a","v":"acceptance rate, the chance a drafted token is accepted (assumed independent)"},{"k":"k","v":"number of drafted tokens per step"}],"example":"With a = 0.8 and k = 4: (1 - 0.8^5) / 0.2 = 3.36 tokens per target pass. With a = 0.5 it is 1.94. A drafter that is cheap but rarely right gains little. Formula from the speculative decoding paper [[r19]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Variants.** A separate draft model, EAGLE (which drafts at the feature level instead of the token level [[tr9]]), multi-token prediction heads on the target itself, and lightweight n-gram or suffix methods that need no extra model. vLLM enables them through `--speculative-config` with a `method` and `num_speculative_tokens`. Its documentation says speculation reduces inter-token latency under medium-to-low QPS, memory-bound workloads, that model-based methods give the best latency reduction, and that n-gram and suffix methods give modest speedups without adding workload during peak traffic [[tr18]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"note","title":"The hosted cousin","md":"OpenAI's latency guide suggests Predicted Outputs when most of the output is already known, for example a small edit to a large document [[r15]]."}],"whereToUse":["Interactive self-hosted assistants at low to medium load, where per-user speed is the goal","Code editing and document revision, where output copies much of the input","Large target models whose single-stream decode is slow","Workloads with a good small draft from the same model family","Edit-in-place tasks on hosted APIs through Predicted Outputs"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Decode is memory-bound and GPUs have spare compute at your load","Outputs are predictable enough for a draft to agree often","Exact output equivalence matters, since the distribution is unchanged"],"avoid":["GPUs run at high batch saturation, where verification competes for real compute","The draft model rarely agrees, as in highly creative, high-entropy text","You have no memory left for a draft model and no lightweight method fits"],"flips":["Traffic grows and batches saturate: gains shrink, so switch to a lighter method or turn it off","No good draft exists: use n-gram or suffix methods for repetitive outputs","You need maximum throughput, not per-user speed: spend effort on batching and quantization first"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with other ways to speed decode","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Speculative decoding (draft or EAGLE)","Low to medium load, memory-bound decode","Saturated batches, low acceptance","Extra model or heads, tuning k"],["N-gram or suffix speculation","Repetitive or copy-heavy outputs","Novel text","Modest gains, little downside"],["Smaller model","Task is easy","Task needs the large model","Quality drop"],["Weight quantization","Memory-bound and capacity-bound","Quality-sensitive tasks","Eval gate needed"],["Fewer output tokens","Output is longer than needed","Output length is required","May shorten answers"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Speculation made throughput worse at peak","cause":"Batches are saturated, so verifying drafted tokens costs real compute","fix":"Use a lighter method or disable it above a load threshold, and measure at peak traffic [[tr18]]"},{"symptom":"Little speedup although speculation is on","cause":"Low acceptance rate because the draft diverges from the target","fix":"Pick a better-matched draft or EAGLE head, lower k, and watch acceptance metrics [[r19]]"},{"symptom":"Out-of-memory after enabling a draft model","cause":"The draft weights and KV take memory from the target's cache","fix":"Account for draft memory in capacity planning, or choose a method without a separate model"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t09-s1","title":"Code assistant at modest load","situation":"A team self-hosts a large code model for an editor plugin. Load is moderate. Users edit existing files, so output copies a lot of input. Streams run at 20 tokens per second and users want faster.","constraints":["Self-hosted","Low to medium QPS","Output overlaps input heavily"],"unknowns":["Is a matched small draft or EAGLE head available?","How much GPU memory is free?"],"question":"Which lever do you try first?","options":[{"name":"Spend on larger batches and more GPUs","verdict":"good","why":"It raises capacity.","tradeoff":"It does not make a single stream faster, which is the complaint."},{"name":"Enable speculative decoding with a matched draft or EAGLE head","verdict":"best","why":"Decode is memory-bound at this load, copy-heavy output gives high acceptance, and the output is unchanged.","tradeoff":"Needs extra memory and tuning of the number of drafted tokens."},{"name":"Quantize KV to 2 bits","verdict":"poor","why":"KV quantization is for memory capacity, not single-stream speed, and carries quality risk.","tradeoff":"Right when KV memory caps concurrency."}],"flips":[{"if":"Load rises until batches saturate","then":"Expect smaller gains and test turning it off at peak."},{"if":"No suitable draft exists","then":"Try n-gram or suffix speculation, which suit copy-heavy output."}],"takeaway":"Speculation speeds single streams when decode has idle compute."},{"id":"w02-t09-s2","title":"Saturated fleet, cost pressure","situation":"A high-traffic service keeps its GPUs at near full batch occupancy all day. Per-user speed is acceptable. Finance wants a lower cost per token. A teammate proposes adding a draft model.","constraints":["Self-hosted","High QPS, saturated batches","Goal is cost per token, not single-stream speed"],"unknowns":["What is the batch occupancy at peak?","Is memory free for a draft model?"],"question":"What do you recommend?","options":[{"name":"Add a model-based draft for speed","verdict":"poor","why":"At saturation there is little spare compute, so verification competes with real work and a draft model takes memory.","tradeoff":"Right at low to medium load where decode leaves compute idle."},{"name":"Look at weight quantization and batching efficiency","verdict":"best","why":"These raise tokens per GPU hour, which is the cost metric, and they do not rely on idle compute.","tradeoff":"Quantization needs an eval gate for quality."},{"name":"Try n-gram or suffix speculation","verdict":"good","why":"The docs describe modest gains without extra workload at peak traffic.","tradeoff":"Gains are small, and only for repetitive output."}],"flips":[{"if":"Traffic drops overnight to low load","then":"Enable model-based speculation during the quiet window."},{"if":"A latency SLO for single streams is added","then":"Revisit speculation with a measured threshold."}],"takeaway":"Match the optimisation to the metric: speed for one user, or cost across many."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t09-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A drafter has acceptance rate 0.8 and proposes 4 tokens per step. Using E = (1 - a^(k+1)) / (1 - a), how many tokens does one target pass yield on average?","answer":3.36,"tolerancePct":3,"unit":"tokens","hint":"0.8^5 = 0.32768","explain":"(1 - 0.32768) / 0.2 = 3.36. At acceptance 0.5 the same formula gives about 1.94, so a draft that agrees less often gains much less.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r19"},{"id":"w02-t09-q2","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does speculative decoding do to the distribution of the generated text?","options":["It leaves it unchanged, because the target verifies every drafted token","It slightly shifts it toward the draft model","It makes sampling deterministic","It lowers quality in exchange for speed"],"answer":0,"explain":"Rejection-based verification makes the output distribution identical to sampling from the target alone. The speedup comes from computing several tokens in parallel, not from approximating.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r19"},{"id":"w02-t09-q3","type":"multi","tag":"flip","q":"Which conditions shrink the benefit of speculative decoding?","options":["GPUs saturated by large batches at high QPS","A low acceptance rate between draft and target","Low-QPS, memory-bound single streams","Copy-heavy output with an n-gram method"],"answers":[0,1],"explain":"Saturation removes the spare compute that verification uses, and low acceptance wastes drafted tokens. Memory-bound low load and copy-heavy output are where speculation helps.","difficulty":2,"ref":"tr18"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does speculative decoding keep outputs identical?","back":"The target verifies all drafted tokens in one pass, keeps the agreeing prefix and resamples the first disagreement, so the distribution is unchanged."},{"front":"When does speculation help most, and when does it fade?","back":"It helps at low to medium QPS in memory-bound decode. Gains shrink as batches saturate or acceptance falls."},{"front":"What is EAGLE?","back":"A speculative sampling method that drafts at the feature level (second-to-top-layer features) instead of the token level."}],"related":["w02-t03","w02-t07","w02-t10","w02-l2"],"refs":["r19","tr9","tr18","r15"]},{"id":"w02-t10","order":10,"group":"KV cache and serving","title":"Quantization: weights and KV cache","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Quantization stores numbers in fewer bits. Quantizing weights shrinks the model and speeds memory-bound decode. Quantizing the KV cache lets more sequences fit. Both trade quality risk for capacity.","useIf":"You host a model and weights or KV memory limit capacity, and you can run an eval before and after.","avoidIf":"You use a hosted API, or you cannot test quality on your own tasks, especially at long context.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Two different things get quantized, and they fix different limits. **Weight quantization** (and activation quantization) shrinks the model. Memory is roughly parameters times bytes per weight: an 8B model is about 16 GB in bf16, 8 GB at 8 bits and 4 GB at 4 bits (derived). **KV cache quantization** shrinks the per-sequence cache, so more sequences fit at long context."},{"t":"table","caption":"What each kind of quantization changes","head":["Target","Saves","Speeds up","Main risk"],"rows":[["Weights, 8-bit (FP8 or INT8)","About half of bf16 weight memory","Memory-bound decode, and prefill where hardware supports the format","Small accuracy loss, needs an eval"],["Weights, 4-bit (for example INT4 W4A16)","About a quarter of bf16 weight memory","Decode bandwidth","Larger quality risk, method-dependent"],["KV cache, FP8","Half of bf16 KV per token","Concurrency and long context","Error grows with context, some layers are sensitive"],["KV cache, 2-bit (KIVI)","About 2.6x less peak memory in the paper","Batch size, up to 4x","Research-grade, near-equal quality only as reported"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Formats and methods.** Names such as W8A8 and W4A16 give weight bits then activation bits. vLLM's LLM Compressor path supports FP8 W8A8, INT8 W8A8 and INT4 W4A16, besides AWQ, GPTQ and bitsandbytes. Its hardware table lists FP8 W8A8 on Ada, Hopper and AMD GPUs but not on Ampere, so check your GPU [[tr7]]. LLM.int8() halves inference memory by keeping outlier features in 16-bit [[tr15]]. GPTQ reaches 3 to 4 bits on 175B-parameter models with negligible accuracy loss as reported [[tr17]]. AWQ protects about 1% of salient weight channels, found from activation statistics [[tr16]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**KV cache.** vLLM supports `kv_cache_dtype=\"fp8\"`. With no calibration all scales are 1.0, and the docs recommend calibrating on a dataset with llm-compressor. Some layer types, such as sliding-window attention, are more sensitive, and `--kv-cache-dtype-skip-layers` keeps chosen layers in the native type [[tr8]]. KIVI quantizes keys per channel and values per token to 2 bits without tuning, reporting 2.6x less peak memory, up to 4x batch size and 2.35x to 3.47x throughput with near-equal quality [[r20]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Gate on your own tasks, at your own context length","md":"Aggregate benchmark scores hide losses on exact quotes, numbers, tool-call arguments and long-range recall. Test those at your real context lengths before and after."}],"whereToUse":["Fitting a larger model on fewer GPUs by quantizing weights","Raising concurrency on long-context services by quantizing the KV cache","Speeding up memory-bound decode on self-hosted models","Edge or cost-limited deployments where memory is the hard limit","Reading vendor claims about FP8 or INT4 serving: ask which tasks were tested"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Weights do not fit, or leave too little memory for KV","Concurrency at long context is capped by KV memory","You have an eval set that covers your hardest tasks and context lengths"],"avoid":["You cannot measure quality on your own data","Tasks need exact quotation, precise arithmetic or long-range recall and the eval shows a drop","Your GPU lacks hardware support for the format, so memory shrinks but speed does not"],"flips":["KV is the binding limit: quantize KV before weights, since weights are already fine","Weights are the binding limit: quantize weights, not KV","The eval shows a drop at long context: keep sensitive layers in native precision or use fewer bits only on the short-context path"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with other ways to fit and speed","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["8-bit weights (FP8 or INT8)","Weights bind, quality bar is high","Hardware lacks support","Small quality drop, needs eval"],["4-bit weights","Memory is tight, tasks tolerate noise","Exact tasks, strict quality","Larger drop, method-dependent"],["FP8 KV cache","KV binds at long context","Long-range recall is fragile","Calibration, layer sensitivity"],["2-bit KV (KIVI)","Throughput-bound, tolerant tasks","Exact quotation, high stakes","Research-grade, test carefully"],["GQA or MLA model, or a smaller model","You can choose the model","Model is fixed","Different model quality"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Answers degrade only on long documents after KV quantization","cause":"Quantization error accumulates with context length","fix":"Eval at the real maximum context, calibrate scales, skip sensitive layers [[tr8]]"},{"symptom":"Model fits but is no faster","cause":"The GPU lacks native support for the format, so it runs weight-only paths","fix":"Check the hardware support table before choosing the format [[tr7]]"},{"symptom":"Numbers or quoted spans are subtly wrong after a 4-bit move","cause":"Aggregate benchmarks hid a loss on precise tasks","fix":"Add exact-match and numeric checks to the eval gate"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t10-s1","title":"70B model must fit on two GPUs","situation":"A team wants to serve a 70B model on two 80 GB Hopper GPUs. In bf16 the weights are about 140 GB, leaving almost no room for KV. Tasks include numeric extraction where mistakes are costly.","constraints":["Hopper GPUs support FP8","Quality bar is strict on numbers","An eval set exists"],"unknowns":["How large is the long-context KV need?","How big is the eval gap at 8 bits versus 4 bits?"],"question":"What do you quantize first?","options":[{"name":"Quantize only the KV cache","verdict":"poor","why":"The 140 GB of weights alone do not fit well, and KV quantization does not shrink them.","tradeoff":"Right when weights already fit and KV is the binding limit."},{"name":"4-bit weight-only for the largest saving","verdict":"good","why":"It cuts weights to about a quarter and leaves ample room for KV.","tradeoff":"More quality risk on numeric tasks, so it needs a strong eval."},{"name":"8-bit FP8 weights, gated by the numeric eval","verdict":"best","why":"It halves weights to about 70 GB, supported on Hopper, with the smallest quality risk that still fits.","tradeoff":"Less memory saved than 4-bit, so KV room may still be tight."}],"flips":[{"if":"8-bit leaves too little KV room and the 4-bit eval passes","then":"Move to 4-bit weights."},{"if":"The GPUs are Ampere","then":"FP8 W8A8 is not supported there, so use INT8 or a weight-only format."}],"takeaway":"Quantize the thing that binds, in the smallest step that fits."},{"id":"w02-t10-s2","title":"Long contracts, concurrency capped by KV","situation":"A service answers questions over long contracts at 64K tokens. Weights fit comfortably on the GPUs, but KV caps concurrency at 6 users. Answers must quote clauses exactly.","constraints":["KV is the binding limit","Exact quotation required","Self-hosted engine supports FP8 KV"],"unknowns":["Is there a long-context exact-quote eval?","Which layers are sensitive in this model?"],"question":"How do you raise concurrency?","options":[{"name":"Calibrated FP8 KV, skip sensitive layers, gate on an exact-quote eval at 64K","verdict":"best","why":"It halves KV with the smallest quality risk and targets the real limit.","tradeoff":"Calibration and testing effort, and less than a 2-bit saving."},{"name":"Add GPUs","verdict":"good","why":"No quality risk.","tradeoff":"Cost and parallelism overhead."},{"name":"Switch to 2-bit KV for the biggest gain","verdict":"poor","why":"Research-grade quantization is risky for exact quotation at 64K tokens.","tradeoff":"Right for throughput-bound, error-tolerant tasks with an eval to back it."}],"flips":[{"if":"The FP8 eval fails on exact quotes","then":"Add GPUs or shorten the context with retrieval."},{"if":"Many users share the same contracts","then":"Prefix caching may raise capacity without touching numerics."}],"takeaway":"Exactness requirements set how aggressive the KV format can be."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t10-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A 70B-parameter model is stored at 4 bits per weight (0.5 bytes). Roughly how many GB do the weights take?","answer":35,"tolerancePct":3,"unit":"GB","hint":"70 billion x 0.5 bytes","explain":"70e9 x 0.5 bytes is 35 GB, versus 140 GB in bf16. Real files are slightly larger because of scales and unquantized layers.","difficulty":1,"ref":"tr7"},{"id":"w02-t10-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Weights fit comfortably, but concurrency at 64K context is capped. Which quantization targets that limit?","options":["INT4 weight-only quantization","A smaller tokenizer vocabulary","Higher temperature","FP8 KV cache"],"answer":3,"explain":"KV memory binds, so a lower-precision KV cache raises the number of sequences that fit. INT4 weights shrink the model but not the per-sequence cache, and vocabulary or temperature are unrelated.","difficulty":2,"ref":"tr8"},{"id":"w02-t10-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each method to its core idea.","pairs":[{"l":"LLM.int8()","r":"Int8 matmul with outlier features kept in 16-bit"},{"l":"GPTQ","r":"One-shot weight quantization using approximate second-order information"},{"l":"AWQ","r":"Protects salient weight channels found from activation statistics"},{"l":"KIVI","r":"2-bit KV cache, keys per channel and values per token"}],"explain":"Each is a named technique from its paper. LLM.int8 and GPTQ and AWQ target weights, while KIVI targets the KV cache.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r20"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Which limit does weight quantization fix, and which does KV quantization fix?","back":"Weight quantization shrinks model memory and speeds memory-bound decode. KV quantization lets more sequences fit at long context."},{"front":"What does W4A16 mean?","back":"4-bit weights with 16-bit activations. The first number is weight bits and the second is activation bits."},{"front":"What is the safe way to adopt KV quantization?","back":"Calibrate scales, skip sensitive layers and gate on an eval at your real maximum context length."}],"related":["w02-t04","w02-t05","w02-t09","w02-l3"],"refs":["r20","tr7","tr8","tr15","tr16","tr17","r24"]},{"id":"w02-t11","order":11,"group":"Prompt caching","title":"Prompt caching: what makes a hit","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A provider prompt cache reuses the stored KV state of a repeated prefix. It changes cost and time to first token, never the answer. A hit needs four things at once: enough tokens, an identical prefix, the right machine and a request inside the lifetime.","useIf":"Requests share a long, stable prefix such as a system prompt, tool list, reference text or growing chat history.","avoidIf":"Prompts are short or unique, or reuse is so sparse that writes cost more than reads save.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A **prompt cache** stores the KV tensors computed for a prefix, not the text or the answer. A later request that starts with the same tokens skips prefill for that part, so the first token arrives sooner and cached tokens are billed at a discount. Sampling still runs, so the output is unchanged [[r1]]. Caching is on by default on OpenAI, Azure OpenAI and Gemini 2.5 and later. Anthropic needs a `cache_control` marker, either one top-level marker for automatic mode or markers on blocks [[r1]] [[r2]] [[r3]] [[tr1]]."},{"t":"flow","title":"Four gates to a hit","direction":"h","steps":[{"title":"Long enough","md":"OpenAI and Azure GPT-5.6+: 1,024 visible tokens. Anthropic: 512 to 4,096 by model. Shorter prompts run uncached with no error.","tag":"size"},{"title":"Identical prefix","md":"One differing character inside the cached region voids the match. Order is tools, system, messages.","tag":"content"},{"title":"Right machine","md":"Caches live on single machines. Above about 15 requests per minute per prefix and key, traffic can overflow and miss.","tag":"routing"},{"title":"In lifetime","md":"OpenAI and Azure GPT-5.6+: at least 30 minutes. Anthropic: 5 minutes or 1 hour, refreshed on use.","tag":"time"}]},{"t":"formula","title":"Break-even for a write premium","expr":"cost(N) = w + r x (N - 1) versus N","vars":[{"k":"w","v":"write multiplier: 1.25 on OpenAI GPT-5.6+ and Anthropic 5-minute entries, 2 for the Anthropic 1-hour entry"},{"k":"r","v":"read multiplier: 0.1 for most models (0.05 on GPT-6.1 Sol and some Anthropic models)"},{"k":"N","v":"requests that reuse the prefix inside its lifetime"}],"example":"At w = 1.25 and r = 0.1, one request costs 1.25x, which is 25% worse than no caching. Two cost 1.35x versus 2x. Ten cost 2.15x versus 10x, the OpenAI documentation example [[r1]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Where to read hits","head":["Provider","Hit field","Write field"],"rows":[["OpenAI and Azure OpenAI","`cached_tokens` (`prompt_tokens_details` on Azure)","`cache_write_tokens` on Azure GPT-5.6+"],["Anthropic","`cache_read_input_tokens`","`cache_creation_input_tokens`"],["Gemini Developer API","`usage.total_cached_tokens`","Implicit, none"],["Gemini on Google Cloud","`cachedContentTokenCount`","Explicit caches bill storage"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Scope and retention.** Entries are not shared across OpenAI organisations or Azure subscriptions, and Google isolates caches by project. OpenAI organisations with Zero Data Retention default to in-memory entries, and Google advises scoping caches to end-user permission boundaries in agent workloads. Check data-handling terms before you cache a sensitive prefix [[r1]] [[r2]] [[r5]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"What changes the prefix without looking like content","md":"On OpenAI the `model`, `tools`, `parallel_tool_calls`, `text.format`, `reasoning.effort`, `text.verbosity` and `context_management` all affect the cached prefix, and summarisation or truncation of history resets reuse [[r1]]. On Anthropic, toggling thinking settings or `output_config.effort` invalidates the messages portion [[r3]]."}],"whereToUse":["Assistants with a long system prompt, policies and tool definitions on every call","RAG or document Q&A where the same reference text precedes each question","Multi-turn chat and agent loops that resend a growing history","Few-shot classification with a long fixed example block","Batch prompts that share one instruction block, when items arrive close together"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A large share of input tokens is identical across requests","Requests for one prefix arrive within the cache lifetime, ideally several per lifetime","You can keep the stable part first and the variable part last"],"avoid":["Prefixes are under the minimum, or change on nearly every request","A prefix is reused only once or twice per lifetime on a provider that charges for writes","Strict isolation forbids shared infrastructure state and the provider scope does not satisfy it"],"flips":["A write premium applies (GPT-5.6+, Anthropic) and reuse is sparse: turn caching off or lengthen retention only if reuse justifies it","Traffic for one prefix exceeds about 15 requests per minute: shard across stable keys","Gaps are 5 to 60 minutes on Anthropic: the 1-hour entry may pay, the 5-minute one will not"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with other caches","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Provider prompt cache","Long stable prefix, varied questions","Short or unique prompts","Write premium, silent misses"],["Engine prefix cache (self-hosted)","You run the engine","You use a hosted API","Timing leaks across tenants unless salted"],["Exact response cache","Identical requests repeat","Inputs vary","Stale answers"],["Semantic cache","Paraphrased repeats, same answer for all","Answers differ by user or change often","Wrong or leaked answers"],["No cache","Sparse reuse with a write premium","Steady reuse","Pays full prefill every time"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Cached tokens are zero on long prompts","cause":"A timestamp, user name or reordered tool list sits inside the first part of the prompt","fix":"Put dynamic text last, sort tool definitions, and compare consecutive prompts byte by byte [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"The cached count is lower than the prefix length you expected","cause":"Before GPT-5.6 counts round down to a multiple of 128, and hidden tokens are excluded","fix":"Expect rounding on older models and compare only to the visible prefix [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"Anthropic hit ratio looks tiny","cause":"`input_tokens` counts only tokens after the last breakpoint, so dividing by it misstates the ratio","fix":"Hit ratio is `cache_read_input_tokens` over the sum of input, read and creation tokens [[r3]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t11-s1","title":"Sparse reuse on GPT-5.6","situation":"A nightly job sends a 9,000-token instruction block with each of three documents, spaced several hours apart on a GPT-5.6 deployment. The default caching mode is on. The bill for input tokens is 20% above the no-cache estimate.","constraints":["Writes cost 1.25x on this model","Reuse gaps exceed the 30 minute lifetime","Latency does not matter"],"unknowns":["Can the job run in the batch tier?","Can the three calls be grouped inside 30 minutes?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Leave the default implicit caching on","verdict":"poor","why":"Every call writes an entry that expires before the next call, so you pay the premium and never read.","tradeoff":"Right when several calls for one prefix land inside the lifetime."},{"name":"Turn caching off with explicit mode and no breakpoints","verdict":"best","why":"Explicit mode with no breakpoints means no caching and no write charge, which matches reuse that never happens.","tradeoff":"You give up savings if the schedule later clusters the calls."},{"name":"Move the work to the batch tier","verdict":"good","why":"Batch gives a 50% discount for 24 hour work.","tradeoff":"Results arrive within 24 hours and queueing is outside your control."}],"flips":[{"if":"The three calls can be sent within a few minutes of each other","then":"Keep caching on. Two uses already beat no cache."},{"if":"The work is delay-tolerant and large","then":"Batch usually beats any caching tweak."}],"takeaway":"A write premium turns sparse reuse into a loss. Count reuses per lifetime."},{"id":"w02-t11-s2","title":"Tenant prefixes with 15 minute gaps","situation":"On Anthropic, each of 400 tenants has a 12,000-token prefix. A given tenant sends a request roughly every 15 minutes during business hours, four requests per hour.","constraints":["5-minute default lifetime, 1-hour option","Write is 1.25x (5-minute) or 2x (1-hour), read is 0.1x","Per-tenant prefixes"],"unknowns":["Is traffic steady across the business day?","Is the model's minimum size met?"],"question":"Which setting do you choose?","options":[{"name":"Default 5-minute entries","verdict":"poor","why":"Each 15 minute gap lets the entry expire, so every request writes at 1.25x and never reads.","tradeoff":"Right when requests for one prefix arrive more often than every 5 minutes."},{"name":"1-hour entries","verdict":"best","why":"Every use refreshes the entry, so with 15 minute gaps one 2x write covers the whole day: 32 requests in 8 hours cost 2 + 31 x 0.1 = 5.1x versus 32x uncached.","tradeoff":"The first request pays 2x, and a tenant that goes quiet for over an hour pays that write again for nothing."},{"name":"No caching","verdict":"good","why":"It is simple and safe.","tradeoff":"It forgoes the saving for tenants with steady traffic."}],"flips":[{"if":"A tenant is silent for more than an hour between requests","then":"Each request writes at 2x and never reads. Turn caching off for that tenant."},{"if":"A tenant sends a request every 2 minutes","then":"The 5-minute entry stays alive for free and is the cheaper choice."}],"takeaway":"Match the lifetime to the gap between requests, not to a default."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t11-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"On a model with a 1.25x write and a 0.1x read, ten requests reuse one prefix inside its lifetime. What is the total input cost for the prefix, in units of one uncached request (ten uncached requests would cost 10)?","answer":2.15,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"x of one uncached request","hint":"1.25 + 0.1 x 9","explain":"One write at 1.25 plus nine reads at 0.1 is 1.25 + 0.9 = 2.15, versus 10 uncached. The documentation uses this same example.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w02-t11-q2","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which are required for a prompt cache hit?","options":["A prefix at or above the minimum size","The same first tokens up to the cached region","The same user id on both requests","The next request arrives inside the cache lifetime"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Size, identical prefix and arrival inside the lifetime are required, along with reaching the machine that holds the entry. A user id is not part of the match.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w02-t11-q3","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Anthropic usage shows input_tokens: 40, cache_read_input_tokens: 11,900 and cache_creation_input_tokens: 0. What does it mean?","options":["Caching failed because input_tokens is only 40","The 11,900-token prefix was read from cache and only 40 tokens were uncached","A cache entry was written for 40 tokens","The prompt was shorter than the minimum size"],"answer":1,"explain":"input_tokens counts only tokens after the last breakpoint. A large cache_read value with zero creation is a clean hit. Failure would show zero in both cache fields.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r3"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What does a provider prompt cache store?","back":"The KV tensors for a prefix, not text or answers. Hits change cost and TTFT, never the output."},{"front":"What are the four conditions for a prompt cache hit?","back":"Enough tokens, an identical prefix, routing to the machine holding it, and arrival inside the lifetime."},{"front":"How many reuses does a 1.25x write need to break even?","back":"Two. One request costs 1.25x versus 1x, but two cost 1.35x versus 2x."}],"related":["w02-t12","w02-t13","w02-t14","w02-l4"],"refs":["r1","r2","r3","tr1","r5"]},{"id":"w02-t12","order":12,"group":"Prompt caching","title":"OpenAI and Azure OpenAI prompt caching","minutes":10,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"OpenAI and Azure OpenAI cache prefixes automatically. GPT-5.6 and later add explicit breakpoints, a write charge, a 30 minute lifetime and prewarming. Older models keep the implicit, free-to-write behaviour.","useIf":"You call OpenAI or Azure OpenAI with a long, stable prefix and need to choose a mode, a retention setting and where to put breakpoints.","avoidIf":"You use another provider (see the Anthropic topic) or you run your own engine (see server-side prefix caching).","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"One caching model, two regimes, split at GPT-5.6. Neither the OpenAI nor the Azure page carries a preview label, and both were checked on 2026-10-13. The GPT-5.6 model version date is 2026-07-09. The date the caching change shipped is UNVERIFIED. Models before GPT-5.6 return a 400 error if you send `prompt_cache_options` or `prompt_cache_breakpoint`, so gate those fields by model [[r1]] [[r2]]."},{"t":"stats","items":[{"label":"Minimum prefix","value":"1,024 tokens","note":"visible input tokens, GPT-5.6+"},{"label":"Write and read","value":"1.25x and 0.1x","note":"0.05x read on GPT-6.1 Sol"},{"label":"Default lifetime","value":"30 min","note":"ttl 30m is the only value. Eligible at least 30 minutes after the last write or reuse"},{"label":"Writes per request","value":"4","note":"implicit mode uses one slot. A token is billed at one rate only"}]},{"t":"tabs","tabs":[{"label":"Before GPT-5.6","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Implicit only, on by default, no separate write charge. Minimum 1,024 tokens, hits count in 128-token increments, and `cached_tokens` rounds down to a multiple of 128. GPT-5.5 places breakpoints every 2,048 tokens","`prompt_cache_retention` is `in_memory` (typically 5 to 10 minutes idle, up to one hour) or `24h`. OpenAI defaults to `24h` for organisations without Zero Data Retention and `in_memory` with it. On Azure, gpt-5.4 and older default to `in_memory` and gpt-5.5 enables extended retention, which offloads KV tensors to GPU-local storage","A stable `prompt_cache_key` per shared prefix improves routing. Aim for about 15 requests per minute per key and shard busier traffic"]}]},{"label":"GPT-5.6 and later","blocks":[{"t":"table","head":["Setting","Behaviour"],"rows":[["`prompt_cache_options.mode`","`implicit` (default) puts a breakpoint at the end of the latest eligible message. `explicit` uses only yours. Explicit with none means no caching and no write charge"],["`prompt_cache_breakpoint`","`{ \"mode\": \"explicit\" }` on a content block. Not allowed on top-level `instructions` or `additional_tools`. Up to 4 writes per request, and implicit mode uses one slot"],["`prewarm: true`","Responses request that fills the cache without output. Written tokens are billed at the write rate"],["`prompt_cache_key`","Optional. Gives per-customer cache accounting and blocks cache-hit probing"],["Azure only","Usage adds `cache_write_tokens`. `prompt_cache_retention` is deprecated"]]},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"Explicit breakpoint after the stable part (Responses shape)","code":"{\n  \"model\": \"<gpt-5.6 or later deployment>\",\n  \"prompt_cache_options\": { \"mode\": \"explicit\", \"ttl\": \"30m\" },\n  \"input\": [\n    { \"role\": \"developer\", \"content\": [\n      { \"type\": \"input_text\", \"text\": \"<policy and reference text>\",\n        \"prompt_cache_breakpoint\": { \"mode\": \"explicit\" } } ] },\n    { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": [ { \"type\": \"input_text\", \"text\": \"<the question>\" } ] }\n  ]\n}","caption":"Field names follow the documentation. Check the API reference for the current schema."}]},{"label":"Azure PTU-M","blocks":[{"t":"list","style":"ul","items":["Provisioned (PTU-M) deployments keep caching but, per the Azure prompt-caching page (updated 2026-08-11), have no breakpoints and no `cache_write_tokens`. The PTU sizing page (updated 2026-09-24) says GPT-6 Astra and GPT-6 Sol support breakpoints, so check your model [[tr20]]. Cache reads can be up to a 100% input discount","On models that deduct cached tokens in full, cached tokens do not consume PTU capacity [[tr11]], so a high cache rate lowers the PTU count: Normalized TPM = peak RPM x prompt tokens x (1 - cache rate) + output-to-input ratio x peak RPM x response tokens [[tr20]]. GPT-6 Astra, GPT-6 Sol and GPT-6.1 Sol use normalized token accounting instead, where cached input and cache writes consume PTU capacity at model-specific weights and the older rule does not apply [[tr20]]"]}]}]},{"t":"p","md":"**Scope.** Caches are not shared across organisations (OpenAI) or subscriptions (Azure), and not across regional processing boundaries. Extended caching on Azure stays inside the deployment type's data zone or region [[r1]] [[r2]]. OpenAI offers a Prompt Caching Dashboard and a Prompt Cache Diagnostics tool for finding why a prefix missed [[r1]]."}],"whereToUse":["Azure or OpenAI assistants with a long policy, tool and reference prefix","Applications that need to avoid write charges on variable text, using explicit breakpoints","Interactive apps that prewarm shared instructions at startup to cut the first user's TTFT","Multi-customer platforms that want per-customer cache accounting through `prompt_cache_key`"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Implicit mode for append-only chat where each turn extends the last","Explicit mode when only part of the prompt is reused and the rest changes every call","Prewarm when the first request's latency matters and the prefix is known ahead"],"avoid":["Implicit mode when each request ends with unique text that will never be read again","Sending new cache fields to models before GPT-5.6, which returns 400","Expecting breakpoints on PTU-M deployments, which do not support them"],"flips":["Reuse is sparse (gaps over 30 minutes) on GPT-5.6+: use explicit mode with no breakpoints to switch caching off","One prefix exceeds about 15 requests per minute: spread across stable keys","Data residency rules apply: confirm that extended retention stays in your boundary"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with other configurations","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Implicit mode (default)","Append-only history, stable prefix","Each call ends in unique text","Writes the unique tail at 1.25x"],["Explicit breakpoints","Parts change at different rates","You forget to place them","Zero caching if none are set"],["Explicit mode, no breakpoints","Caching would only add write charges","Reuse is steady","Gives up savings"],["Prewarm","First-request TTFT matters","Prefix is unknown until the first call","Billed at the write rate"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"HTTP 400 after adding `prompt_cache_options` to shared client code","cause":"The request is routed to a model before GPT-5.6","fix":"Send the new fields only to GPT-5.6+ deployments [[r2]]"},{"symptom":"Costs rise after moving to GPT-5.6 although reuse seems fine","cause":"Implicit mode writes the variable tail of every request at 1.25x","fix":"Switch to explicit mode with one breakpoint after the stable prefix [[r1]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t12-s1","title":"Reused policy plus a different retrieved tail","situation":"On an Azure Standard GPT-5.6 deployment, each call sends a 9,000-token policy and tool block that never changes, then 3,000 tokens of freshly retrieved text that differs on every call, then a short question. Traffic is steady at 40 requests per minute.","constraints":["Writes cost 1.25x","Stable part is 75% of tokens","The retrieved part is never reused"],"unknowns":["Is the stable block above the 1,024 token minimum?","Does the retrieved text ever repeat across calls?"],"question":"How do you set the cache mode?","options":[{"name":"Implicit mode and nothing else","verdict":"good","why":"The stable block is cached after the first write and read at 0.1x.","tradeoff":"The 3,000 unique tokens are written at 1.25x on every call and never read."},{"name":"Explicit mode with one breakpoint after the stable block","verdict":"best","why":"Only the reused 9,000 tokens are written. The unique tail is billed at the normal rate and never pays a write premium.","tradeoff":"You must place and maintain the breakpoint, and forgetting it turns caching off."},{"name":"Turn caching off","verdict":"poor","why":"It forfeits a 90% discount on 9,000 tokens at 40 requests per minute.","tradeoff":"Right when reuse is sparse or the prefix is under the minimum."}],"flips":[{"if":"Retrieved passages repeat often across calls","then":"Add a second breakpoint after the most common passages."},{"if":"The deployment is PTU-M","then":"Breakpoints are not available. Keep the stable text first and size PTUs with the cache rate."}],"takeaway":"Write only what you will read again."},{"id":"w02-t12-s2","title":"Sizing a provisioned deployment","situation":"A team plans a PTU-M deployment for 1,000 requests per minute at peak, each with a 200-token prompt and a 20-token response. The model's input TPM per PTU is 3,400, the output-to-input ratio is 8 and the increment is 5. Most prompts share a long prefix.","constraints":["PTU-M, no breakpoints","A model that deducts cached tokens in full (the doc example uses gpt-5.2), not a GPT-6 normalized-token model","Capacity is paid whether used or not"],"unknowns":["What cache rate can the prompts reach?","Is capacity available in the region?"],"question":"How do you size it?","options":[{"name":"Size for the 0% cache rate and add a margin","verdict":"poor","why":"It gives about 106 PTUs, rounded to 110, and pays for capacity the cache would free up.","tradeoff":"Right if the prefix is unstable and a measured cache rate is near zero."},{"name":"Measure the real cache rate, size with it, and add a standard deployment as spillover","verdict":"best","why":"At 50% the formula gives 76.5 PTUs, rounded to 80, about 28% fewer than at 0%, and spillover covers a miss-rate surprise.","tradeoff":"If hits fall, PTUs run hot and traffic spills to a pay-as-you-go deployment."},{"name":"Move to Standard to get breakpoints","verdict":"good","why":"Explicit breakpoints give finer control.","tradeoff":"You lose the stable latency and reserved capacity that justified PTU."}],"flips":[{"if":"Prefix stability is not yet proven","then":"Size with a conservative cache rate and lower it as measurements come in."},{"if":"Traffic is spiky and small","then":"Pay-as-you-go may cost less than idle reserved capacity."}],"takeaway":"On PTU the cache rate is a sizing input, so measure it."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t12-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Using Normalized TPM = peak RPM x prompt tokens x (1 - cache rate) + ratio x peak RPM x response tokens, with 1,000 RPM, 200-token prompts, 20-token responses, ratio 8, 3,400 input TPM per PTU and a 50% cache rate, how many PTUs before rounding?","answer":76.47,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"PTUs","hint":"Normalized TPM = 100,000 + 160,000","explain":"1,000 x 200 x 0.5 = 100,000 plus 8 x 1,000 x 20 = 160,000 gives 260,000. Dividing by 3,400 gives 76.47, which rounds up to 80 with an increment of 5.","difficulty":3,"ref":"tr20"},{"id":"w02-t12-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"You add prompt_cache_options to a shared client and requests to a gpt-5.4 deployment start failing. Why?","options":["The cache lifetime is too short","The request exceeds the 4 write limit","Models before GPT-5.6 reject prompt_cache_options and breakpoints with a 400","Azure disables caching on gpt-5.4"],"answer":2,"explain":"The new fields exist only from GPT-5.6. Older models return 400, so gate them by model. Lifetime and write limits do not apply, and gpt-5.4 still caches implicitly.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r2"},{"id":"w02-t12-q3","type":"multi","tag":"concept","q":"Which statements about GPT-5.6 and later caching are correct?","options":["Explicit mode with no breakpoints means no caching and no write charge","The only supported ttl is 30m","Prewarmed tokens are billed at the write rate","Breakpoints may be placed on the top-level instructions item"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Instructions and additional_tools cannot hold a breakpoint, so put a breakpoint on a developer message block instead. The other three statements are documented.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What are the two prompt_cache_options modes on GPT-5.6 and later?","back":"implicit (default) puts a breakpoint at the end of the latest eligible message. explicit uses only the breakpoints you mark."},{"front":"How do you disable caching on a GPT-5.6+ Standard deployment?","back":"Set mode to explicit and place no breakpoints. There is no caching and no write charge."},{"front":"What changes for PTU-M deployments?","back":"Caching continues without breakpoints or cache_write_tokens (per the Azure prompt-caching page). On models that deduct cached tokens in full, cached tokens do not consume PTU capacity, so a higher cache rate needs fewer PTUs. GPT-6 normalized-token models weigh cached input and writes instead."}],"related":["w02-t11","w02-t13","w02-t14","w02-l4"],"refs":["r1","r2","tr11","tr20"]},{"id":"w02-t13","order":13,"group":"Prompt caching","title":"Anthropic cache_control","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"Anthropic caches only what you mark. Automatic caching adds one top-level marker that follows the conversation. Explicit caching places up to four breakpoints. Entries last 5 minutes or 1 hour, and writes cost more than reads.","useIf":"You call Claude models directly or through Bedrock or Foundry and want to control exactly which prefix is cached and for how long.","avoidIf":"Reuse is sparse, so writes would never be read, or the prefix is under the model's minimum size.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"The cached prefix is built in this order: `tools`, then `system`, then `messages`. **Automatic caching** is one top-level `cache_control: {\"type\": \"ephemeral\"}`. The breakpoint moves to the last cacheable block as the conversation grows, so you need not update markers. **Explicit caching** puts `cache_control` on individual blocks, with up to 4 breakpoints in a request. The automatic marker uses one of those slots, and the two can be combined [[r3]]."},{"t":"code","lang":"json","title":"Explicit breakpoint with a 1-hour entry","code":"{\n  \"model\": \"<model>\",\n  \"max_tokens\": 1024,\n  \"system\": [\n    { \"type\": \"text\",\n      \"text\": \"<long policy and reference text>\",\n      \"cache_control\": { \"type\": \"ephemeral\", \"ttl\": \"1h\" } }\n  ],\n  \"messages\": [ { \"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"<question>\" } ]\n}","caption":"Omit ttl for the default 5 minute entry."},{"t":"table","caption":"Prices, lifetimes and minimums (checked 2026-10-13)","head":["Item","Value"],"rows":[["5-minute write","1.25x base input. Refreshed free on each use"],["1-hour write (`ttl: \"1h\"`)","2x base input"],["Read (hit)","0.1x, or 0.05x on Opus 5.5 and Sonnet 5.5, or 0.025x on Fable 5.1 and Mythos 5.1. Multipliers stack with Batch and long-context pricing"],["Minimum size","512 tokens on the newest models, 1,024 on Opus 4.8, Sonnet 5, Sonnet 4.6 and 4.5, 2,048 on Opus 4.7, 4,096 on Opus 4.6, 4.5 and Haiku 4.5. Shorter prompts run uncached with no error"],["Isolation","Per workspace on the Claude API and Foundry. Organisation level on Bedrock and Google Cloud"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Reading usage.** `cache_creation_input_tokens` counts tokens written, `cache_read_input_tokens` counts tokens read, and `input_tokens` counts only tokens after the last breakpoint. Total input is the sum of the three. The creation total splits into `ephemeral_5m_input_tokens` and `ephemeral_1h_input_tokens` [[r3]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Lookback.** A breakpoint checks only 20 block positions back for an earlier write, so add a second breakpoint when one turn adds 20 or more blocks. An entry exists only once the first response begins, so parallel requests with the same prefix should wait for it [[r3]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"What invalidates what","md":"A change to tool definitions invalidates everything. Toggling web search or citations, or `speed: \"fast\"`, invalidates system and messages. A change to `tool_choice` or images invalidates messages, as do thinking parameters and `output_config.effort`. A breakpoint on content that changes every request, such as a timestamp, writes every time and never reads [[r3]]."}],"whereToUse":["Agents that resend a growing conversation with tool results (automatic caching)","Large reference documents or policies reused by many questions (explicit breakpoint after the document)","Assistants where tools, system and history change at different rates (up to 4 breakpoints)","Per-tenant prefixes with gaps over 5 minutes (1-hour entries)","Bedrock deployments of Claude models, which use checkpoints with 5 minute or 1 hour TTLs [[r4]]"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The prefix is above the model minimum and reused at least twice per lifetime","You can keep tools, then system, then messages in a stable order","Gaps between requests are under 5 minutes (default) or under an hour (1-hour entry)"],"avoid":["A prefix reused once: the 1.25x write costs 25% more than no caching","Fan-out of parallel requests that all arrive before the first entry exists","Changing tools, thinking settings or effort between turns, which invalidates entries"],"flips":["Gaps between 5 and 60 minutes: use the 1-hour entry, which breaks even at three requests","Steady traffic under 5 minute spacing: keep the default, since each use refreshes it free","Turns add 20 or more blocks: add a second breakpoint inside the lookback window"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with other configurations","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Automatic caching","Append-only conversations, little setup","Parts change at different rates","Marker moves with the conversation, one slot used"],["Explicit breakpoints","Tools, system and history change at different rates","You forget to update them","Lookback limit of 20 blocks"],["5-minute entry (default)","Steady traffic under 5 minute gaps","Gaps longer than 5 minutes","Silent expiry"],["1-hour entry","Gaps of 5 to 60 minutes","Sparse or one-off reuse","2x write premium"],["No caching","One-off or sparse prompts","Steady reuse","Pays full input price"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Both cache usage fields are zero on a long prompt","cause":"The prompt is below the model's minimum, or a marker sits on a block that changes every request","fix":"Check the model's minimum and mark the last stable block, not a timestamped one [[r3]]"},{"symptom":"A 50-way parallel run pays a write on every request","cause":"An entry exists only after the first response begins","fix":"Send one request, wait for its response to start, then fan out [[r3]]"},{"symptom":"Hits drop after a tool or effort change","cause":"Tool definitions, `tool_choice`, thinking or effort settings invalidate cached layers","fix":"Freeze tools and settings per session, and change them only at a session boundary [[r3]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t13-s1","title":"Fan-out of 50 sub-requests","situation":"An orchestrator sends 50 analysis requests at once. Each starts with the same 20,000-token brief, then a short, different instruction. Caching is enabled with a marker after the brief.","constraints":["Writes cost 1.25x","All requests are sent in the same second","The brief is above the model minimum"],"unknowns":["Can the first request run a second ahead of the rest?","Is total wall time tight?"],"question":"How do you send them?","options":[{"name":"Send all 50 together","verdict":"poor","why":"No entry exists yet, so each request writes the brief at 1.25x and none reads it.","tradeoff":"Right only when the requests are unrelated or latency to the first token cannot wait."},{"name":"Send one, wait for its response to begin, then send the other 49","verdict":"best","why":"One write at 1.25x, then 49 reads at 0.1x. The cost is about 6.2x of one request instead of 62.5x.","tradeoff":"The first request delays the others by one time to first token."},{"name":"Turn caching off for this run","verdict":"good","why":"It costs 50x with no premium surprises.","tradeoff":"It gives up an 80% or better saving."}],"flips":[{"if":"The 50 requests are spread over minutes","then":"No wait is needed. Reads begin after the first write."},{"if":"The brief is shorter than the model minimum","then":"Caching does nothing. Shorten the fan-out or accept full price."}],"takeaway":"A cache entry exists only after the first response begins. Warm it first."},{"id":"w02-t13-s2","title":"Agent loop with long tool results","situation":"An agent loops for up to 40 turns. Each turn adds 25 content blocks (tool calls and results). Caching is automatic. Hit rate falls to near zero after turn two even though nothing else changes.","constraints":["Automatic caching, one marker","Turns add more than 20 blocks","Tools and settings are stable"],"unknowns":["Where does the earlier write sit relative to the new marker?","Can blocks be consolidated?"],"question":"What is the fix?","options":[{"name":"Raise the TTL to 1 hour","verdict":"poor","why":"Lifetime is not the problem. The lookback cannot find the earlier write.","tradeoff":"Right when gaps between turns exceed 5 minutes."},{"name":"Add an explicit breakpoint within 20 blocks of the previous one","verdict":"best","why":"The breakpoint looks back only 20 block positions, so an extra marker keeps the earlier write reachable.","tradeoff":"It uses another of the 4 breakpoint slots and needs code to place it."},{"name":"Disable caching","verdict":"good","why":"It removes the surprise.","tradeoff":"You pay full price on a growing history."}],"flips":[{"if":"Tool results can be summarised into fewer blocks","then":"Fewer blocks per turn may keep you inside the 20 block window."},{"if":"A tool list changes mid-loop","then":"That invalidates everything, so fix the tool list first."}],"takeaway":"Lookback is 20 blocks. Plan breakpoints for turns that add many."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t13-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"With a 1-hour entry (2x write, 0.1x read), how many times the cost of one uncached request do three requests for the same prefix cost in total?","answer":2.2,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"x of one uncached request","hint":"2 + 0.1 x 2","explain":"One write at 2x plus two reads at 0.1x is 2.2x, versus 3x uncached. At two requests it is 2.1x versus 2x, so the 1-hour entry breaks even at three.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r3"},{"id":"w02-t13-q2","type":"single","tag":"diagnose","q":"Usage shows input_tokens 6,000, cache_creation_input_tokens 0 and cache_read_input_tokens 0 for a 6,000-token prompt that carries a cache marker. What is the likely cause?","options":["The model's minimum cacheable size exceeds the prompt, or the marked block changes every request","The cache is full","The marker is on the system prompt, which cannot be cached","The request was served from the 1-hour entry"],"answer":0,"explain":"Zero in both cache fields means nothing was cached. Shorter-than-minimum prompts run uncached silently, and a marker on changing content writes nothing reusable. System prompts can be cached, and a 1-hour hit would show a read.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r3"},{"id":"w02-t13-q3","type":"multi","tag":"bug","q":"Which changes invalidate cached layers on Anthropic?","options":["Changing tool definitions","Changing tool_choice","Changing the user's question at the end","Toggling thinking parameters or output_config.effort"],"answers":[0,1,3],"explain":"Tool definitions invalidate everything, while tool_choice and thinking or effort settings invalidate the messages layer. The last user turn sits after the breakpoint, so changing it does not invalidate the prefix.","difficulty":3,"ref":"r3"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How many cache breakpoints can an Anthropic request have, and what is the lookback?","back":"Up to 4 breakpoints, and each looks back 20 block positions for an earlier write. Add a breakpoint when a turn adds 20 or more blocks."},{"front":"What do the Anthropic multipliers look like?","back":"5-minute write 1.25x, 1-hour write 2x, read 0.1x (0.05x or 0.025x on some models). They stack with Batch pricing."},{"front":"What does input_tokens mean in Anthropic usage?","back":"Only tokens after the last cache breakpoint. Total input is input_tokens plus cache read plus cache creation."}],"related":["w02-t11","w02-t12","w02-t14","w02-l4"],"refs":["r3","r4"]},{"id":"w02-t14","order":14,"group":"Prompt caching","title":"Designing prompts for cache hits","minutes":9,"difficulty":"core","summary":"Hit rate is something you engineer. Put content in order of how rarely it changes, keep tools and settings frozen, shard hot prefixes across keys and alert when the cached share drops.","useIf":"You pay for a long prefix on every call and the dashboard shows few cached tokens, or you are designing a new prompt layout.","avoidIf":"Reuse is too sparse for any cache to hit, in which case the fix is to turn caching off, not to reshape the prompt.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"A cache matches a prefix, so everything before the first changed token is reusable and everything after it is not. The design rule follows: **stable first, dynamic last, history append-only**. Order content by how often it changes, and put a breakpoint after the last stable layer where the provider allows one [[r1]] [[r3]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"A layout from most to least stable","head":["Layer","Changes","Rule"],"rows":[["Tool definitions","On deploy","Fixed, sorted order. Do not add or drop tools per request"],["System instructions","On deploy","No timestamps, names or request ids"],["Static reference and examples","Daily or less","Identical bytes across calls"],["Conversation history","Each turn","Append only. Do not edit, reorder or trim the middle"],["Retrieved passages, time, question","Every call","Last. Never in the cached region"]]},{"t":"lab","id":"prompt-cache","intro":"Start with the Many tenants, low traffic preset: about 0.2 requests per minute per tenant leaves gaps that run into a 5 minute lifetime. Then load One hot prefix, where 120 requests per minute on one key exceeds the roughly 15 per minute documented before misses begin, and spread it across keys. Finally break the prefix on 70% of requests and watch caching cost more than no caching."},{"t":"p","md":"**Freeze what changes the prefix.** On OpenAI `model`, `tools`, `parallel_tool_calls`, `text.format`, `reasoning.effort`, `text.verbosity` and `context_management` all affect the cached prefix. Use `tool_choice: \"none\"` or `allowed_tools` instead of removing a tool. On supported GPT-6 models append a `configuration_update` input item to change reasoning effort without rewriting the prefix [[r1]]. MCP servers SHOULD return tools in a deterministic order, and `tools/list` MUST NOT vary per connection but MAY vary by authorization, so group users with identical tool sets [[r18]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Route and key.** Caches live on single machines, and above about 15 requests per minute for one prefix and key, requests can overflow to machines without the entry. Before GPT-5.6, a stable `prompt_cache_key` per shared prefix improves routing, and busier traffic needs a stable mapping across several keys: 1,000 requests per minute needs at least 67 keys. A key never pins a machine or guarantees a hit [[r1]] [[r2]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"When padding a short prefix pays off","expr":"pad if L > M x (r + (w - r) / N)","vars":[{"k":"L, M","v":"original prefix length and the provider minimum, in tokens"},{"k":"r, w","v":"read and write multipliers"},{"k":"N","v":"total requests that share the padded prefix inside its lifetime (one write and N - 1 reads)"}],"example":"With M = 1,024, r = 0.1, w = 1.25 and N = 10, the threshold is 1,024 x 0.215 = 220 tokens. A 300-token prefix shared by 10 requests is worth padding to the minimum. Formula from the OpenAI guide [[r1]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"tip","title":"Measure the ratio, alert on the drop","md":"Track cached tokens over prompt tokens (on Anthropic, read tokens over input plus read plus creation). A regression raises no error. OpenAI offers a Prompt Caching Dashboard and a Prompt Cache Diagnostics tool [[r1]]."}],"whereToUse":["Designing a new assistant, agent or RAG prompt layout","Debugging a cached-token share that dropped after a release","Platforms with many tenants that need per-tenant prefixes and keys","Agent frameworks that build tool lists from MCP servers","Cost reviews where input tokens dominate the bill"],"whenToUse":{"use":["A large share of tokens could be identical across calls but is not cached today","You can control prompt assembly code and tool registration order","Traffic per prefix is steady enough to reuse entries inside the lifetime"],"avoid":["Reuse per prefix is rare, so the work gains nothing","Content must be personalised at the very top of the prompt for correctness","You would weaken isolation by sharing one prefix or key across tenants"],"flips":["Prefix is shorter than the minimum and reused heavily: pad, if L exceeds M(r + (w - r)/N)","One prefix exceeds 15 requests per minute on an older model: shard across stable keys","Tool lists differ by authorization: give each identical set its own key and prefix"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with other ways to cut input cost","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Reorder for stable-first","Dynamic text sits early","Order is already stable","Small code change, big effect"],["Explicit breakpoints","Layers change at different rates","Provider offers none (PTU-M)","Maintenance of markers"],["Shorten the prompt","Padded with irrelevant text","Prompt is already tight","May drop needed context"],["Padding to the minimum","Short prefix, heavy reuse","Reuse is light","Pays for padding tokens"],["Route easy queries to a smaller model","Many queries are easy","Queries are uniformly hard","Splits the cached prefix across models"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Cached share is zero although the prompt is long and constant in content","cause":"A timestamp or request id sits near the top, or tools are emitted in map order","fix":"Move dynamic text last and sort tools. Diff two consecutive prompts byte by byte [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"Hit rate falls after turning on summarisation of old turns","cause":"Compaction or truncation rewrites the history prefix","fix":"Compact rarely and accept one miss, or keep a stable summary block appended once [[r1]]"},{"symptom":"Hits fall for some users only","cause":"Their MCP tool list differs by authorization, so their prefix differs","fix":"Group users by identical tool sets and prefix, and give each group its own key [[r18]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t14-s1","title":"Zero hits on a long prompt","situation":"A 12,000-token prompt starts with the line Current time followed by the clock, then policies, then tools that the code collects from a dictionary. The cached share is 0%. Traffic is 30 requests per minute.","constraints":["Hosted API with automatic caching","Prompt is far above the minimum","Traffic is steady"],"unknowns":["Is the tool order stable between processes?","Does the model need the time at the top?"],"question":"What do you change?","options":[{"name":"Raise the cache lifetime or prewarm","verdict":"poor","why":"The prefix changes at the first line on every call, so a longer lifetime has nothing to reuse.","tradeoff":"Right when the prefix is stable and the gaps are too long."},{"name":"Move the time to the end, sort the tools and re-measure the cached share","verdict":"best","why":"It removes both prefix breakers, so the 12,000 stable tokens match on every call.","tradeoff":"The model sees the time late, which rarely matters, and you must keep the order sorted forever."},{"name":"Place an explicit breakpoint after the policies","verdict":"good","why":"It caches the policy layer even if tools vary.","tradeoff":"The time at the top still breaks everything after it, so you must move it anyway."}],"flips":[{"if":"The task depends on the time early in the prompt","then":"Put it in a short message after the cached prefix."},{"if":"Tools legitimately differ per request","then":"Group requests by tool set."}],"takeaway":"Find the first changed token. Everything after it is lost."},{"id":"w02-t14-s2","title":"One hot prefix at 1,000 requests per minute","situation":"A support product on a model before GPT-5.6 sends 1,000 requests per minute that share one 6,000-token prefix. It uses one prompt_cache_key. The cached share is 40% and unstable.","constraints":["About 15 requests per minute per key and prefix before overflow","Model predates GPT-5.6","One shared prefix"],"unknowns":["Can the gateway map requests to keys deterministically?","Do tenants require separate keys anyway?"],"question":"How do you key the traffic?","options":[{"name":"Keep one key","verdict":"poor","why":"All 1,000 requests hit one routing target, and overflow sends most of them to machines without the entry.","tradeoff":"Right when traffic for the prefix stays under about 15 requests per minute."},{"name":"Spread across at least 67 keys with a stable mapping","verdict":"best","why":"1,000 divided by 15 is 67, so each key stays under the overflow rate and each key's entries get reused.","tradeoff":"More keys mean more cold entries to warm, and a mapping to maintain."},{"name":"Drop the key and rely on default routing","verdict":"good","why":"It is simple and can work at moderate rates.","tradeoff":"At this rate you lose the routing help the key gave and hits stay unstable."}],"flips":[{"if":"The model is GPT-5.6 or later","then":"The key is optional for routing, but still useful for per-customer accounting."},{"if":"Tenants require isolation","then":"Use per-tenant keys and accept the lower shared hit rate."}],"takeaway":"Divide the request rate by about 15 to get the minimum key count."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t14-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A single prefix receives 1,000 requests per minute. At about 15 requests per minute per key, what is the minimum number of prompt_cache_key values needed?","answer":67,"tolerancePct":2,"unit":"keys","hint":"1,000 / 15, rounded up","explain":"1,000 / 15 is 66.7, so at least 67 keys are needed with a stable mapping. A key never guarantees a hit, so confirm with the cached share.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w02-t14-q2","type":"order","tag":"order","q":"Order a prompt for the best cache reuse, from first to last.","items":["Tool definitions","System instructions","Static reference document","Conversation history so far","Retrieved passages and the new question"],"explain":"Content goes from least to most frequently changing. History is append-only, and the retrieved text and question change every call so they come last.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r1"},{"id":"w02-t14-q3","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"What does a prompt_cache_key guarantee?","options":["A cache hit for every repeated prefix","Pinning of requests to one machine","Nothing by itself. It only improves routing and accounting","Longer cache lifetime"],"answer":2,"explain":"The key helps route requests with a shared prefix to the same place and, on GPT-5.6+, gives per-customer accounting. It never pins a machine or guarantees a hit, and it does not change the lifetime.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r1"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the layout rule for cache-friendly prompts?","back":"Stable first, dynamic last, history append-only. Tools, then system, then static reference, then history, then retrieved text and the question."},{"front":"How many keys does one prefix at 1,000 requests per minute need?","back":"At least 67, from 1,000 / 15 requests per minute per key, using a stable mapping."},{"front":"How do you remove a tool without breaking the cache on OpenAI?","back":"Do not remove it. Use tool_choice none or allowed_tools so the tools array, and the prefix, stay identical."}],"related":["w02-t11","w02-t12","w02-t13","w02-l5"],"refs":["r1","r2","r3","r18"]},{"id":"w02-t15","order":15,"group":"Other caches and budgets","title":"Semantic cache","minutes":9,"difficulty":"advanced","summary":"A semantic cache embeds the prompt and returns a stored answer when a similar earlier prompt is close enough. It skips the whole model call, and it changes what the user sees, so it can be wrong, stale or leak across users.","useIf":"Many users ask paraphrases of the same questions, the answer is the same for everyone, and a slightly stale answer is acceptable.","avoidIf":"Answers depend on who asks, on live data or on high-stakes accuracy, or paraphrases rarely repeat.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Unlike a prompt cache, which reuses prefill state and leaves the answer unchanged, a **semantic cache** replaces the model's answer. The flow is: embed the new prompt, search a vector store partition for a close earlier prompt, return its stored answer on a hit, and on a miss call the model and store the result with a lifetime [[r16]] [[r17]]."},{"t":"diagram","id":"w02-t15-d1","title":"Semantic cache request path","alt":"A request is embedded and looked up in its partition. A hit returns the stored answer. A miss calls the model and stores the answer.","src":"flowchart LR\n  R[Request] --> E[Embed]\n  E --> L{Hit in partition?}\n  L -->|yes| A[Return stored answer]\n  L -->|no| M[Call model]\n  M --> S[Store with TTL]\n  S --> B[Return answer]"},{"t":"p","md":"**In Azure API Management** the policies are `llm-semantic-cache-lookup` (inbound) and `llm-semantic-cache-store` (outbound), and they must be paired. Lookup takes `score-threshold` (0.0 to 1.0, lower is stricter), `embeddings-backend-id`, `ignore-system-messages` (recommended `true`) and `max-message-count`, with a `vary-by` child to partition the cache. Store takes `duration` in seconds. Microsoft suggests starting low, such as 0.05, and warns that values above 0.2 may cause mismatches and that results can be incorrect, outdated or unsafe. It recommends a `rate-limit` after lookup and `llm-content-safety` with Prompt Shields [[r16]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**In your own code** a semantic cache is an application or gateway pattern, not a managed Redis feature. RedisVL `SemanticCache` uses `distance_threshold` in cosine distance units from 0 to 2 (the tutorial sample is 0.15) and a `ttl` (sample 3,600 seconds). The APIM and RedisVL scales differ, so a number from one does not carry over [[r17]]. Partition by tenant, user, model ID, embedding model ID, prompt template version, tool configuration version and safety-policy version [[r17]]."},{"t":"formula","title":"Break-even hit rate","expr":"net saving per request = h x C_model - C_lookup, so break-even h = C_lookup / C_model","vars":[{"k":"h","v":"hit rate"},{"k":"C_lookup","v":"embedding plus vector lookup, paid on every request, hit or miss"},{"k":"C_model","v":"cost of the model call a hit avoids"}],"example":"Derived. If the embedding and lookup cost 6% of a model call, you need a hit rate above 6% to save money, and misses also add the lookup latency. A miss pays the embedding, the lookup and the normal call [[r17]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"warn","title":"A hit can be a wrong answer for someone else","md":"Two prompts can be close in embedding space and still need different answers, for example order 123 and order 124. If the answer was built from one user's permissioned data, a hit hands it to another. Timing also shows whether a prompt was cached, and audits found cross-user cache sharing at several providers [[r12]]."}],"whereToUse":["Public FAQ and product-help assistants where one answer serves everyone","Internal knowledge bots over a public or company-wide corpus with a refresh schedule","Gateway-level cost control in front of several apps (APIM or a custom proxy)","Repeated analytics questions asked in different words with a stable data snapshot","Demos and load tests where repeated questions need fast, cheap answers"],"whenToUse":{"use":["The same question arrives in many wordings and one answer is correct for all askers","Staleness of hours or days is acceptable and you can set a matching TTL","You can partition by model, template and policy versions and measure net savings"],"avoid":["Answers depend on the user, role or live data","Wrong or unsafe answers are costly, as in regulated or high-risk domains","The traffic is a long tail of unique questions, where the lookup is pure overhead"],"flips":["Answers built from permissioned data: add the authorization scope to the key, or skip the semantic cache","A prompt template, model or policy version changes: partition by version so old answers expire","Hit rate stays under the break-even rate: remove it and rely on prompt caching"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with other caches","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Semantic cache","Paraphrased repeats, shared answer","Personalised or live answers","Wrong, stale or leaked answers, plus lookup cost on every miss"],["Exact response cache","Identical inputs repeat","Wording varies","Low hit rate, stale answers"],["Prompt (prefix) cache","Long stable prefix, varied questions","Short prompts","Answer unchanged, only cost and TTFT improve"],["Curated FAQ or answer library","Top questions are known and reviewed","Long tail of questions","Editorial effort"],["No cache","Unique, high-stakes questions","High repetition","Full cost and latency"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Users receive an answer to a slightly different question","cause":"Threshold is too loose for the embedding model","fix":"Start at a strict threshold (APIM 0.05), review mismatches on a sample and tighten before loosening [[r16]]"},{"symptom":"One tenant sees an answer built from another tenant's data","cause":"The cache was not partitioned by tenant or user","fix":"Add tenant, user and authorization scope to `vary-by` or the key, or do not cache such answers [[r16]] [[r17]]"},{"symptom":"Savings never appear","cause":"Embedding and lookup cost exceeds the savings at the actual hit rate","fix":"Compute break-even, measure hit rate, and remove the cache if it stays below it [[r17]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t15-s1","title":"Public help bot, weekly content","situation":"A public help assistant answers product questions from documentation that changes weekly. Logs show about 35% of questions are paraphrases of 200 common ones. The answer is the same for every visitor.","constraints":["Answer is identical for all users","Content refreshes weekly","A wrong answer is a nuisance, not a safety issue"],"unknowns":["How often do near-misses give wrong answers at a given threshold?","What do embedding and lookup cost relative to a call?"],"question":"How do you cache?","options":[{"name":"Semantic cache with a strict threshold, a TTL under the weekly refresh and partitions by version and language","verdict":"best","why":"Paraphrase repeats are common and the answer is shared, so a strict threshold captures most hits with few mismatches.","tradeoff":"Lookup cost on every request and a need to review mismatches."},{"name":"Exact-match cache only","verdict":"good","why":"It is the safest and cheapest to run.","tradeoff":"It misses the paraphrases, which is most of the repetition."},{"name":"A loose threshold to maximise hits","verdict":"poor","why":"Near-miss questions get the wrong stored answer, and trust drops.","tradeoff":"Reasonable only for low-stakes content where any nearby answer is acceptable."}],"flips":[{"if":"Documents change daily","then":"Shorten the TTL or flush on publish."},{"if":"The 200 common questions are stable and reviewed","then":"A curated answer library may beat any cache."}],"takeaway":"Shared answers and high repetition are the semantic cache sweet spot."},{"id":"w02-t15-s2","title":"HR assistant over permissioned records","situation":"An HR assistant answers questions such as how many days of leave do I have. Answers come from each employee's records and from policy documents that vary by country. Leadership wants to cut costs with a semantic cache.","constraints":["Answers depend on the asker","Records are permissioned","Wrong or leaked answers are a compliance issue"],"unknowns":["What share of questions are policy only?","Can answers be keyed by user and country?"],"question":"What do you recommend?","options":[{"name":"A global semantic cache with no partitioning","verdict":"poor","why":"A close question from another employee would return the first employee's data.","tradeoff":"Acceptable only for answers that carry no personal data and are identical for all."},{"name":"Skip answer-level caching. Use prompt caching for the policy prefix and cache retrieval per user","verdict":"best","why":"It saves cost without storing personal answers for reuse, and each answer is computed with the caller's permissions.","tradeoff":"Smaller saving than a whole-answer cache."},{"name":"A semantic cache partitioned by user and country with a short TTL","verdict":"good","why":"Reuse is limited to one user's repeats, so no leak across users.","tradeoff":"Low hit rate per user, plus work to keep the partition and permissions in sync."}],"flips":[{"if":"A policy-only question has no personal data","then":"Cache it in a country-partitioned semantic cache."},{"if":"Permissions change often","then":"Shorten the TTL or avoid caching answers altogether."}],"takeaway":"Put the authorization scope in the key, or do not cache the answer."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t15-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Every request pays an embedding and vector lookup that costs 6% of one model call. A hit avoids the whole model call. What hit rate (in percent) breaks even?","answer":6,"tolerancePct":5,"unit":"%","hint":"h x C_model = C_lookup","explain":"Net saving per request is h x C_model - C_lookup. It is zero when h = C_lookup / C_model = 0.06, which is 6%. Latency on misses is a further cost.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r17"},{"id":"w02-t15-q2","type":"multi","tag":"security","q":"Which belong in the partition key of a semantic cache that serves several tenants?","options":["Tenant or user and authorization scope","Model ID and embedding model ID","Prompt template, tool configuration and safety-policy versions","The time of day of the request"],"answers":[0,1,2],"explain":"Scope prevents leaks, and the model, embedding and template or policy versions prevent a stale answer from an old configuration being reused. Time of day does not define an answer's validity.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r17"},{"id":"w02-t15-q3","type":"single","tag":"concept","q":"In APIM llm-semantic-cache-lookup, what does a lower score-threshold mean?","options":["More hits, looser matching","A longer cache lifetime","Fewer embeddings per request","Stricter matching, so fewer but closer hits"],"answer":3,"explain":"In APIM a lower score-threshold is stricter, and Microsoft suggests starting around 0.05. RedisVL uses a different scale, cosine distance from 0 to 2, so the numbers do not transfer.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r16"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"How does a semantic cache differ from a prompt cache?","back":"A prompt cache reuses prefill state and leaves the answer unchanged. A semantic cache returns a stored answer, which can be wrong, stale or leaked."},{"front":"What is the break-even hit rate for a semantic cache?","back":"C_lookup divided by C_model. Embedding and lookup are paid on every request, and a hit saves the model call."},{"front":"What threshold does Microsoft suggest for APIM semantic caching?","back":"Start low, for example 0.05, where lower is stricter. Values above 0.2 may cause mismatches."}],"related":["w02-t11","w02-t16","w02-t17","w02-l6"],"refs":["r16","r17","r12","r26"]},{"id":"w02-t16","order":16,"group":"Other caches and budgets","title":"Response and embedding caches","minutes":8,"difficulty":"core","summary":"An exact-match response cache returns a stored answer for an identical request. An embedding cache reuses vectors for unchanged text. Both save calls, and both are only as safe as their keys and invalidation rules.","useIf":"The same exact inputs repeat, as in retries, re-runs, re-ingestion of unchanged documents or fixed tool and resource lists.","avoidIf":"Inputs rarely repeat exactly, answers must vary or stay fresh, or you cannot build a key that carries scope and versions.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"An **exact-match response cache** hashes a normalised request and returns the stored answer on a match. The key must include everything that changes the answer: model ID, messages, sampling parameters, tools, prompt template version and the caller's scope. It needs no embedding and cannot return a near-miss, which makes it far safer than a semantic cache. It still replaces sampling, so tasks that need fresh variation should not use it [[r17]]."},{"t":"p","md":"An **embedding cache** keys on a hash of the content and stores the vector. It saves embedding calls when a pipeline re-ingests mostly unchanged chunks, and when the same query text is embedded repeatedly. The key must also carry the embedding model and its version, because vectors from different models are not comparable. This is a design rule inferred from the Redis tutorial's practice of keying semantic caches by embedding model ID, not a vendor recommendation for embedding caches [[r17]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Cache layers at a glance","head":["Layer","Key","Saves","Main risk"],"rows":[["Provider prompt cache","Prefix tokens","Prefill and input cost","Silent misses, write premium"],["Exact response cache","Hash of normalised request plus scope and versions","The whole call","Stale answers, no sampling variance"],["Semantic cache","Embedding of the prompt plus partition","The whole call for paraphrases","Wrong, stale or leaked answers"],["Embedding cache","Content hash plus embedding model and version","Embedding calls","Stale vectors if the model changes"],["MCP list and resource cache","Server plus `cacheScope` and authorization","Round trips","A private entry crossing users"]]},{"t":"p","md":"**Tool and resource caching in MCP.** The 2026-07-28 revision lets servers mark results as cacheable with `ttlMs` (like `max-age`, must be 0 or more, absent means 0) and `cacheScope` (`public` or `private`) on `server/discover`, `tools/list`, `prompts/list`, `resources/list`, `resources/templates/list` and `resources/read`. `input_required` results are not cacheable. Servers SHOULD return tools in a deterministic order, which also protects LLM prompt caches [[r18]]."},{"t":"callout","kind":"gotcha","title":"Invalidate on every dimension that changes the answer","md":"Expire entries by TTL and also by version: model, prompt template, tool configuration, embedding model and safety policy. Add an event-based flush when the underlying data changes. A cache without a version in the key serves yesterday's logic after a deploy."}],"whereToUse":["Idempotent retries, so a retried request returns the stored answer instead of a second paid call","Evaluation and regression runs that replay fixed prompts against a fixed model","Classification or extraction of strings that recur exactly, such as product titles","Nightly re-ingestion pipelines where most chunks are unchanged","Agent platforms that call `tools/list` and `resources/read` on every turn"],"whenToUse":{"use":["Inputs repeat exactly and the answer is a pure function of the key","You can include scope, model and template versions in the key","Embedding the same text again would produce the same vector"],"avoid":["Free-form chat where the exact request almost never repeats","Tasks that need a different sample each time, such as brainstorming","Answers built from live or permissioned data without a scope in the key"],"flips":["The embedding model or its version changes: invalidate the whole embedding cache","A prompt template or safety policy changes: bump the version in the key","MCP `cacheScope` is `private`: key the entry by authorization context"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with its neighbours","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Exact response cache","Identical requests, retries, replays","Wording varies","Stale answers, low hit rate on chat"],["Semantic cache","Paraphrased repeats, shared answers","Personalised or live answers","Near-miss errors and leakage"],["Embedding cache","Re-ingestion and repeated query text","Mostly new text","Stale vectors across model versions"],["Prompt (prefix) cache","Long stable prefix, new questions","Short prompts","Does not skip the decode"],["MCP `ttlMs` and `cacheScope`","Stable tool and resource lists","Lists that change per call","Private entries must not cross users"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"After a deploy, users still get old-style answers","cause":"The key omits the prompt template and model versions","fix":"Add template, model, tool and policy versions to the key, and flush on deploy [[r17]]"},{"symptom":"Search quality drops after switching embedding models","cause":"Cached vectors from the old model are mixed with new ones","fix":"Key on embedding model and version, and rebuild the cache and index together"},{"symptom":"One user sees another's tool list or resource","cause":"A `private` MCP result was cached without an authorization scope","fix":"Respect `cacheScope` and key private entries per authorization context [[r18]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t16-s1","title":"Nightly re-ingestion of two million chunks","situation":"A pipeline re-embeds 2,000,000 chunks every night. About 3% change on a typical day. The embedding model is upgraded roughly twice a year. Embedding cost is the largest line in the ingestion bill.","constraints":["Mostly unchanged text","Rare model upgrades","Cost is the driver, not latency"],"unknowns":["Is chunking deterministic, so unchanged text yields identical chunks?","How is the vector store keyed?"],"question":"How do you cut embedding cost?","options":[{"name":"Cache vectors by content hash plus embedding model and version","verdict":"best","why":"About 97% of chunks hit the cache, so only 60,000 are embedded. A model upgrade changes the key and refreshes everything.","tradeoff":"You store hashes and vectors, and chunking must be deterministic."},{"name":"Re-embed everything every night","verdict":"good","why":"It is simple and always consistent.","tradeoff":"You pay for 1.94 million unneeded calls every night."},{"name":"Key the cache on chunk id only","verdict":"poor","why":"An edited chunk keeps its id, so a stale vector is served after the text changes.","tradeoff":"Right only if ids change whenever content changes."}],"flips":[{"if":"Chunk boundaries shift when a document changes","then":"Hash chunks after chunking, or hash at document level and re-chunk changed documents."},{"if":"The model changes","then":"Treat all cached vectors as invalid and rebuild."}],"takeaway":"Key on content and model version, not on ids."},{"id":"w02-t16-s2","title":"Agent platform and 30 MCP servers","situation":"An agent platform calls tools/list on 30 MCP servers at the start of every turn, adding hundreds of milliseconds. Some servers return different tools depending on who is signed in. The servers send ttlMs and cacheScope.","constraints":["Many servers","Some lists vary by authorization","Servers declare caching hints"],"unknowns":["Which servers mark results private?","How does the platform identify an authorization context?"],"question":"How do you cache the lists?","options":[{"name":"Honour ttlMs and cacheScope, key private results by authorization context","verdict":"best","why":"It removes most round trips, follows the server's own freshness hints and never mixes users.","tradeoff":"A small cache layer and a per-context key to maintain."},{"name":"One shared cache for all users with a fixed 60 second TTL","verdict":"poor","why":"It ignores `cacheScope`, so a private list for one user can reach another.","tradeoff":"Safe only for servers that mark everything public."},{"name":"Do not cache and call every turn","verdict":"good","why":"It is always fresh and simple.","tradeoff":"It pays the round trips on every turn and ignores the servers' hints."}],"flips":[{"if":"A server omits ttlMs","then":"Treat it as 0, so do not cache."},{"if":"A list changes mid-session","then":"Refresh when the server signals a change, and keep the tool order deterministic for prompt caching."}],"takeaway":"Use the server's cache hints and scope."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t16-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"A pipeline re-embeds 2,000,000 chunks nightly and 3% change. With a content-hash embedding cache, how many embedding calls are avoided on a normal night?","answer":1940000,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"calls","hint":"97% of 2,000,000","explain":"3% of 2,000,000 is 60,000 chunks that must be embedded, so 1,940,000 calls are avoided. A model upgrade changes the key and invalidates the whole cache.","difficulty":1,"ref":"r17"},{"id":"w02-t16-q2","type":"single","tag":"bug","q":"After switching embedding models, search quality drops although the cache hit rate is 99%. What is the most likely cause?","options":["The vector store index is too small","The cache returns old-model vectors because the key omits the embedding model","Cosine similarity is unsupported","The TTL is too long for queries"],"answer":1,"explain":"Vectors from different models live in different spaces, so mixing them breaks similarity. The cache key must carry the embedding model and version. The other options do not explain a drop that coincides with a model change.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r17"},{"id":"w02-t16-q3","type":"match","tag":"concept","q":"Match each MCP cache element to its meaning.","pairs":[{"l":"ttlMs","r":"Freshness lifetime in milliseconds, like max-age. Absent means 0"},{"l":"cacheScope public","r":"May be shared across callers"},{"l":"cacheScope private","r":"Must not cross authorization contexts"},{"l":"input_required result","r":"Not cacheable"}],"explain":"These come from the 2026-07-28 MCP revision. They let clients cache list and read results safely and also help keep tool order stable for prompt caches.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r18"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What must an exact-match response cache key include?","back":"Model ID, messages, sampling parameters, tools, template version and the caller's scope. Anything that changes the answer."},{"front":"What must an embedding cache key include?","back":"A content hash plus the embedding model and its version, since vectors from different models are not comparable."},{"front":"What are ttlMs and cacheScope in MCP?","back":"ttlMs is the freshness lifetime in ms, like max-age. cacheScope is public or private, and private entries must not cross authorization contexts."}],"related":["w02-t15","w02-t14","w02-t17","w02-l6"],"refs":["r17","r18","r16"]},{"id":"w02-t17","order":17,"group":"Other caches and budgets","title":"End-to-end latency budgets","minutes":10,"difficulty":"core","summary":"A latency budget splits a target such as time to first token into stage allowances. Run independent stages in parallel, watch the tail not the mean, and attack the stage that dominates before touching the others.","useIf":"A request crosses several stages (auth, rewrite, retrieval, guards, model, output checks) and you must hit a latency target.","avoidIf":"The system is a single model call with no other stages. Use the TTFT and TPOT topics directly.","explain":[{"t":"p","md":"Pick the number the user feels, usually TTFT for a streamed chat or TTLT for a programmatic call, then give each stage an allowance and measure p50 and p95 per stage. Total latency is the sum along the **critical path**, not the sum of all stages. Stages that do not depend on each other, such as an input guard and retrieval, can run in parallel and drop off the path [[r14]] [[r15]]."},{"t":"table","caption":"Illustrative budget in milliseconds (not measurements)","head":["Stage","p50","On the critical path?"],"rows":[["Authentication","20","Yes"],["Query rewrite (LLM call)","250","Yes, and the first candidate to remove for simple queries"],["Embedding, retrieval, rerank","60 + 120 + 150","Yes, 330 together"],["Input guard","80","No when run beside retrieval (330 hides 80)"],["Prompt assembly","10","Yes"],["Model time to first token","600","Yes, shrinks with prompt caching"],["Network","50","Yes"]]},{"t":"p","md":"Run sequentially, these sum to 1,340 ms. With the guard in parallel the path is 1,260 ms. Dropping the LLM rewrite for standalone queries gives 1,010 ms. Each step is a different dial, and the lab below lets you try them together."},{"t":"lab","id":"latency-budget","intro":"Load the Interactive chat preset and find where first-token time goes. Turn on the parallel guard, then streaming, then a prompt cache hit (it cuts model TTFT by an assumed 50%), then the smaller-model toggle. Finally set every p95 to 3 times its p50 and compare the typical path, the simulated p95 and the sum of the p95s. All defaults are illustrative, not product measurements."},{"t":"p","md":"**Tails.** The sum of stage p95s overstates the end-to-end p95, but fan-out makes the slowest call decide. If each of 100 parallel calls is slow 1% of the time, 63% of requests see at least one slow call [[tr4]]. Defences are per-stage deadlines, a fallback when a stage is late (skip the rerank, serve without the rewrite) and **hedged requests**: after about the p95 delay send a duplicate and take the first reply. In a Google benchmark a 10 ms hedge cut the 99.9th percentile of 1,000 reads from 1,800 ms to 74 ms for 2% more requests [[tr4]]."},{"t":"p","md":"**Streaming and guards.** Streaming lowers perceived latency but not total time [[r14]]. An output guard that needs the whole answer defeats it, so budget TTLT, or guard in chunks. Pair latency with output token counts, and set separate SLIs: TTFT p95, TTLT p95 per output-length bucket, and cache hit ratio [[r14]]."}],"whereToUse":["RAG assistants with rewrite, retrieval, rerank, guard and generation stages","Agents that fan out to many tool calls and wait for the slowest","Voice and live-assist products with a hard first-response budget","Gateway designs that add token limits, content safety and caching in front of the model","Incident reviews that ask which stage moved the p95"],"whenToUse":{"use":["More than one stage contributes noticeable time before the first token","Some stages are independent and could run in parallel","You have per-stage percentiles from traces, not only an end-to-end average"],"avoid":["Treating the sum of p95s as the p95 of the whole path","Optimising a stage that is under 5% of the path while the model call is 60%","Budgeting only for p50 when the product promise is a tail percentile"],"flips":["An output guard needs the full text: the target becomes TTLT, so stream status and guard in chunks if allowed","Fan-out grows: tail amplification matters more than any single stage's p50","The model call dominates: use caching, a smaller or routed model and shorter output before tuning the rest"]},"alternatives":{"title":"Compared with other latency levers","head":["Option","Wins when","Loses when","Cost or risk"],"rows":[["Parallelise independent stages","Guard and retrieval can overlap","Stages depend on each other","More concurrent load, failure handling"],["Remove or condition a stage","Rewrite helps few queries","Every query needs it","Quality impact on hard queries"],["Prompt caching and a smaller model","Model TTFT dominates","TTFT is already small","Quality or write-premium risk"],["Hedged requests","Tail from idempotent, cheap calls","Calls are costly or not idempotent","Extra load, must cancel the loser"],["Streaming","Humans read the answer","A program or full-text guard consumes it","Does not cut total time"]]},"pitfalls":[{"symptom":"Mean latency improved but complaints continue","cause":"The tail moved little, or fan-out makes slow calls common","fix":"Track p95 and p99 per stage, add deadlines and hedge idempotent reads [[tr4]]"},{"symptom":"Streaming was enabled and the first word is still late","cause":"TTFT is dominated by stages before the model, such as rewrite and rerank","fix":"Chart the budget by stage and shorten or parallelise the biggest pre-model stage"},{"symptom":"A regression is blamed on infrastructure","cause":"Answers got longer, which raises TTLT but not TTFT","fix":"Bucket TTLT by output tokens before opening an incident [[r14]]"}],"scenarios":[{"id":"w02-t17-s1","title":"Chat that must start in 1.2 seconds","situation":"An interactive RAG assistant takes 1,340 ms to the first token at p50 on the illustrative budget above. The product target is 1,200 ms at p50 and 1,800 ms at p95. Answers stream.","constraints":["Stages: auth, rewrite, embed, retrieve, rerank, guard, assembly, model, network","Target is TTFT, not TTLT","Quality must not drop for hard queries"],"unknowns":["What share of queries need the LLM rewrite?","Is a stable prefix available for caching?"],"question":"Which changes do you make first?","options":[{"name":"Run the input guard beside retrieval and skip the rewrite for standalone queries, then cache the stable prefix","verdict":"best","why":"The guard drops 80 ms and the skipped rewrite 250 ms for most queries, taking the path to about 1,010 ms. A prefix cache then trims model TTFT.","tradeoff":"Hard follow-up queries still need the rewrite, so route by a cheap check."},{"name":"Turn on streaming","verdict":"poor","why":"It is already on. Streaming changes perceived total time, not the time before the first token.","tradeoff":"Right when the target is felt completion time and answers are long."},{"name":"Switch to a smaller model","verdict":"good","why":"It lowers model TTFT directly.","tradeoff":"It risks quality, and the larger savings are available without that risk."}],"flips":[{"if":"Guard policy forbids running before retrieval completes","then":"Keep it sequential and focus on the rewrite and prefix cache."},{"if":"Model TTFT is 60% of the path after changes","then":"Move to caching and model choice next."}],"takeaway":"Remove or overlap pre-model stages before spending quality on the model."},{"id":"w02-t17-s2","title":"Agent step that waits on 20 tool calls","situation":"An agent step issues 20 read-only calls in parallel and waits for all. Each call has a p50 of 120 ms and 1% of calls take over 2 seconds. Users see the step stall about one in five times.","constraints":["Parallel fan-out of 20","Calls are idempotent reads","A partial answer is acceptable for non-critical sources"],"unknowns":["Which sources are critical?","Can slow calls be cancelled?"],"question":"How do you protect the tail?","options":[{"name":"Set per-call deadlines, hedge idempotent reads after about the p95 delay, and degrade when non-critical sources are late","verdict":"best","why":"With 20 calls the chance of one slow call is about 18%, so tail control matters. Hedging is cheap for idempotent reads and a deadline bounds the step.","tradeoff":"Hedges add a small share of extra calls and need cancellation of the loser."},{"name":"Retry every timeout immediately and without limit","verdict":"poor","why":"It multiplies load during incidents and still waits for the slowest retry.","tradeoff":"Fine for a single rare call, with backoff and a cap."},{"name":"Raise the timeout so slow calls finish","verdict":"good","why":"It returns complete data.","tradeoff":"The step waits for the slowest call, so the stall remains."}],"flips":[{"if":"Calls are not idempotent or are costly","then":"Do not hedge. Use deadlines and fallbacks only."},{"if":"Fan-out drops to 3 calls","then":"The tail matters far less. Simple deadlines may be enough."}],"takeaway":"Fan-out turns rare slow calls into common slow requests."}],"quiz":[{"id":"w02-t17-q1","type":"numeric","tag":"numeric","q":"Stages in ms: auth 20, rewrite 250, embed 60, retrieve 120, rerank 150, input guard 80, assembly 10, model TTFT 600, network 50. The guard runs in parallel with embed, retrieve and rerank. What is the time to first token in ms?","answer":1260,"tolerancePct":1,"unit":"ms","hint":"The parallel branch costs the longer of 80 and 60 + 120 + 150","explain":"20 + 250 + max(80, 330) + 10 + 600 + 50 = 1,260. Without the overlap the sum is 1,340, so the guard hides 80 ms.","difficulty":2,"ref":"r14"},{"id":"w02-t17-q2","type":"numeric","tag":"diagnose","q":"A request fans out to 100 parallel calls and each call is slow 1% of the time, independently. What percentage of requests see at least one slow call?","answer":63.4,"tolerancePct":3,"unit":"%","hint":"1 - 0.99^100","explain":"0.99^100 is about 0.366, so 1 - 0.366 = 0.634. The Tail at Scale paper states the 63% figure for this case.","difficulty":2,"ref":"tr4"},{"id":"w02-t17-q3","type":"single","tag":"tradeoff","q":"When are hedged requests an appropriate defence against tail latency?","options":["For non-idempotent writes that must run once","For cheap, idempotent calls, sent after about the p95 delay with the loser cancelled","For every call from the first attempt, to be safe","Only when the mean is already low"],"answer":1,"explain":"Hedging duplicates work, so it fits idempotent, cheap calls and is triggered late to cap extra load. Duplicating writes is unsafe, hedging everything doubles cost, and the mean is not the trigger.","difficulty":2,"ref":"tr4"}],"flashcards":[{"front":"What is the critical path in a latency budget?","back":"The longest chain of dependent stages. Stages that overlap, such as a guard beside retrieval, add only if they are longer than the chain they overlap."},{"front":"Why does fan-out make the tail matter?","back":"With 100 parallel calls that are each slow 1% of the time, 63% of requests see at least one slow call."},{"front":"What did the Tail at Scale benchmark show for hedging?","back":"A 10 ms hedge cut the 99.9th percentile of 1,000 reads from 1,800 ms to 74 ms for about 2% more requests."}],"related":["w02-t01","w02-t02","w02-t14","w02-l7"],"refs":["r14","r15","r13","tr4"]}]});
